      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>2 ALM' McDOUCALL'S PATENT SELF RAISING FLOUR MTTRE-7 THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGTIST 9, 1913</head>
        <p>2 ALM' McDOUCALL'S PATENT SELF RAISING FLOUR MTTRE-7 THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGTIST 9, 1913</p>
        <p>_ old labar CHICALA A DRY FEED FOR CHICKENS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>POULTRY.</head>
        <p>POULTRY.</p>
        <p>Poultry Farming as a Livelihood for</p>
        <p>Women. ADVICE ON MANAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>A correspondent of " The Times" writes on " Poultry Farming for Women" : It may be said that, with certain reservations, poultry farming has been successfully undertaken by women.. Too great importance should not be attached to the popularity of fancy fowls among those whose unlimited. ti.pital has enabled them to get together some of the finest stock hi the country, housed on model lines, bred, reared and exhibited with professional assistancie. Plants of this kind are not as a rule economic- ally run—tough in some cases they have proved extlemely profitable—and must not, therefore, be regarded very hopefully by the ordinary woman with a limited amount of capital. In this case the production of eggs and table poultry at first _on a moderate scale is likely to prove more profitable. There should be at least 12 months' training on a poultry farm for the purpose of gaining an insight into the ins and outs of the business. Then not less than £3OO isrequired to start a farm of any site. A suit- able site must be found near a good market, the rent not to exceed £5O per annum, the land being dry and well drained with a gravel sub- soil and southern slope; if possible, while out- houses and fresh grass land are a great asset. Spring is the best time to make a start, for stock and sittings can then be readily pur- chased, while chickens. may be reared without difficulty with a view to autumn egg produc- tion. Not more than two pure breeds should be kept, and these of the general purpdse type; Orpingtons, Wyandottes, Plymouth Rocks, or. Sussex, the, hens of which are good layers, while the cockerels make 'excellent table fowls..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>GRAVEST- DANGER.</head>
        <p>THE GRAVEST- DANGER.</p>
        <p>The general lines Lobe adopted in managing a utility poultry farm depend largely on its configuration, Runs divided with wire net- ting are almost invariably erected, however, while three distinct departments should be kept in view—those of the breeders, the chickens, and the layers. Perhaps the gravest danger to be avoided is that of overcrowding, in regard to which a permanent stock of 60 birds per acre is commonly accepted as the maximum permissible. Careful grading is another point to be remembered—cockerels being separated from pullets after ten weeks' growth, cocks from hens except in the breeding season, and -chickens distributed in 'three sizes.' The trap nest and recording System are essential, and must be Carried to their natural sequel, i.e., universal toe punching or ringing. MI such light work as this, together with feed- ing, the handling of chickens and stock gener- ally, incubators, the packing and disposal of produce, office work, carpentering, Ste., are perfectly suited to robust,,healthy women and calculated to keep any one busy from morning till night. On the other hand, there are cer- tain jobs, such as moving houses and coops,. cleaning out. carrying round water, and hand- ling foods in bulk, which cannot be so regarded. -Male assistance 'mutt therefore always be reek: oned with, a man or a mdn and' boy being employed. -</p>
        <p>Poultry farming cannot, therefore. be recom- mended to women as a sole means of lieW hood ;,and.it certainly cannot be recommended. to the- delicate woman or to any One whose heart is not altogether in the open-air life. The work is distinctly hard-, the daily round beset by. all kinds of weathers, and there is practically no off season. No profit can be ex- pected for the first three years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>YOUNG STOCK.</head>
        <p>THE YOUNG STOCK.</p>
        <p>There is really Milk v..ork among the chickens during August, provided that the weather is favourable. So long as they are well housed, suitably fed, and supplied with plenty of green stuff, they are pretty well able to look after themselves. If, on the contrary, the weather is cold and wet, constant care and attention will be necessary, since a chill during the first few months is rather a serious matter, and one that should be. avoided at all costs. A some- what critical stage of .chickenhood is when the baby plumage or down is being replaced by leathers, and special treatment at such a time is necessary. The formation of. new feathers' takes a good deal out of the system, and unless special foods are provided, it frequently hap- pens that the chickens become sick, when the least cold or neglect proves fatal. k little lin- seed added to the food assists greatly in the growth of the new plumage so also does raw meat, both of Which should be generously pro- vided. The dry system of feeding, so excellent in many respects, should not be continued after the chickens are a month old, a combination of the dry and the moist methods being Substi- tuted. Two feeds a day of soft food, one first. thing in the morning, when the crops and in- testines are quite empty, and t nother about 4 p.m.. should be provided, the remainder of the day the birds having to scratch about for 'mains, which should be thrown among the Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Ens, Poultry, etc.</head>
        <p>Ens, Poultry, etc.</p>
        <p>P- ULLETS.—Thousands laying, 30s. dozen; two months old 10s. • three months old 14s, dozen; live delivery anywhere; list free.—Goodwin, Stratford, Essex.</p>
        <p>• SIR „FRANK NEWNES'S - HOUSE DESTROYED. Hollerday Hill ,House, Lynton, the summer residence of the late Sir George Newnes, and now the property of Sir Frank Newnes, Was destroyed by fire late on Monday night. The damage, which is covered by -insurance, is esti- mated at £lO,OOO. The fire is suspected to he the work of suffragettes, but there is no evi- dence of this. The house was unoccupied. The fire started in two places, on the north and south sides, and was preceded by several explo- sions. The fire brigade, with only a hose reel and an insufficient supply of water, were power- less to stop the .rapid progress of the flames. The house was a massive stone structure, con- taining 22 bedrooms. •</p>
        <p>SUICIDE AFTER LOSING A FAVOURITE DOG. The loss of his old Scotch terrier, which he had had to have killed owing to its blindness and age, so preyed upon the mind of a Keswick man named Thomas Harper, aged 60 years, that he committed suicide. At the inquest on Tuesday, when evidence to this effect was given, a juryman remarked *that Harper was very much attached to . his dog, and would. almost have looked.after it before himself., The Coroner added that he was not talking senti- mentally when he said that there were many persons to whom the death of a favourite dog would be a very great- trouble indeed. A ver- dict of suicide whilst temporarily .insane was returned.</p>
        <p>11111-0-51,1FErn Ln FROM INDIOES 4i When it is such a simple matter to take 30 drops of Mother Seigel's Syrup after meals. Thousands testify that it does 1 remove &amp; prevent inciigestio:n. MOTHER Eri3c iGhLits YY _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>THE NEW MILK INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>An informing and suggestive article on 'Milk and Co-operative Trading" appears in The Times." After contending that, although the home farmers have a .monopoly Of the Eng- lish market, .the consumer has not. yet suffered from MS inability to draw supplies from abroad, the writer continues: Only once—as consequence of the drought of 1911—has the price :been raised Since it WaS lowered to its present level more than 20 years ago. The . cost of production has fluctuated, but the pro- ducers and middlemen, except on the occasionr mentioned, have shared the -gains and losses. For some time after the daiuing industry was organised on its present ;basis, things went smoothly, the demand keeping pace with the supply. As time went on, however, and pro- duction increased, the middlemen began to get the better of the farmers, and the need arose for action to regulate the output. It was in the dairying industry that the co-operative movement had its first real beginning in British</p>
        <p>farming. . The dairy farmer has already gained sub- stantially ,by the principle of combined trading. There have sprung up in different parts of the country local organisations for the control of the milk output, and their number is increasing steadily. Action of this kind became necessary to prevent the loss in summer of the advantages ! gained in the winter months. Individual deal- ing was practicable so long as the supply was short of the denia.nd, and would still be appli- cable in winter. From the time that dairy farming assumed its present proportions, the advent of the gra-zing season, made the position of the producer impossible. The supply was in excess of requirements, and as few of the individual farmers had facilities for utilising the surplus milk in other ways, the wholesale trade was able to command its own terms. More remains to be done, but already there are in operation in Cheshire and Wiltshire impor- tant central factories, financed and conducted exclusively by farmers, at which the milk not required for the standing contracts can be converted into cheese or butter. - These institu- tions have solved a problem that long per- plexed the dairying industry. They have assured to the producer a fair return for his milk without affecting the cost Of the article to the consumer. They have simply secured to the contributing members the share of the retail price to which they are entitled. / New milk is the best paying,article the dairy farmer has to sell, provided the trade is pro- perly regulated, but it is impracticable for the county or district to maintain a uniform output all the year round: There will, in the nature of things, be a larger quantity of milk in sum- mer than in winter; and as the consumption does not fluctuate in proportion, it is important that provision should be made whereby the benefits of the augmented production in the grazing season should mit go to the distributing agencies, but to the farmer, or, at any rate, that the increase should not be turned to his dis- advantage. There is-but one solution for the surplus milk problem, and that has been devised n several districts in the form of central .milk factories, all of which are prospering and giving indications of expansion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>P BRIGHTER OUTLOOK.</head>
        <p>P BRIGHTER OUTLOOK.</p>
        <p>Good progress has been made with the cut- ting of winter oats, and 4n the earlier districts a few fields of wheat and barley are in stock. The sunshine and higher temperatures in the beginning of the week hastened the ripening in a measure that will reduce the interval between the early and late spring corn. Even this im- provement, which was again arrested by a return, of _cooler conditions in mid-week, will not. make .reaping continuous, at any rate in the South, where, owing to the adverse spring, sowing was unduly prolonged and the growth of the late §eedlings retarded. The outlook, however (says "The Times"); is decidedly bet- ter, both as regards the state of, the crops and the Convenience of harvest arrangements; Bar- ley 'and oats•on- the light _soils have developed in a wonderful manner in the last three weeks, with the result that the quality of theyrain,: as • well as the 'quantity or the straw,-`will%exceed ,expectations. More sunshine is ,needed to harden the -sample and to give to barley the • bright colour that counts for so Much- in' the malting market. A 'short crop of oat straw is a serious matter in the northern counties of England, in Scotland, and,in Wales, but fortu- nately the crops in those parts are -of greater bulk than. in the earlier -counties where the drought was most acute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>RURAL HOUSING.</head>
        <p>RURAL HOUSING.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries wish it to be made known that they are desirous of obtaining information upon the subject of the erection of rural cottages for labourers and others.. They Will be grateful if any land- owners- or local authorities: who have recently erected such' cottages will 'furnish tlieni with particulars of the cost of the cottages, and the result of. their enterprises, both financially and otherwise. A form of particulars, will be sent On application, to any person who is willing to supply such information. Communications should be addressed to the Secretary, Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, 4,. Whitehall-Place, .LOndon, &amp;W., and need not be stamped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>AN INSURANCE POINT.</head>
        <p>AN INSURANCE POINT.</p>
        <p>An important decision in regard to a farm labourer's claim under the Insurance Act was given in the High Court by. County Court Judge Tindal Atkinson, when William Sapsford, aged forty-two, of Lambourne, Boinford, sought to , reg Over from Mr. Luke James Furze, farmer, of Eppingydamages for loss of sickness benefit. •He ,Claimed that he would have been entitled to these from January.. 23rd, 1913, but for the failure of the defendant to pay eleven contribu- tions under the National Insurance' Act. When the Act came into force Sapsford was in the employment of Mr. Furze until September 29th, 1912, -when he left his employment and entered the service of a farmer named llowarcL His Honour, in giving judgment, said the card ought to have been stamped up to September 30th by Furze. The plaintiff's second effiriloyer paid the contributions regularly.. The' defend- ant had failed to pay 'eleven contributions that he was bound to pay, and the plaintiff had con- sequently lost £l3 in respect to sickness benefit. He held that the plaintiff, was not entitled to the benefit of Section 70 of the Act, as he was not a member of an approved society actually at the time he was in Furze's employ. He gave judgment for the defendant, with (leave to appeal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>MAIN CROP POTATOES.</head>
        <p>MAIN CROP POTATOES.</p>
        <p>Present appearances point to a small yield. of main crop potatoes. The 'seed .was planted late and growth was delayed by, drought and low temperatures. Although there is no great luxuriance of haulm (says "The Times"), the crop appears healthy, and there is an excep- tional profusion of flower, a feature whin not only indicates freedom from disease. but fore- tells a good yield of tubers in' proportion to the growth above ground. Rumours have gained currency of had attacks of blight in Ireland, but. investigation shows that, fortunately, there is nO cause for alarm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>GARDEH.</head>
        <p>THE GARDEH.</p>
        <p>A USEFUL DWARF FLOWERING tinnur..—lt is diniella 'to understand why that charming little .dwarf slirub Spirwa japonica Anthony Waterer is not more freely planted. For tilling a lttrge lawn bed, or for grouping towards the front of the shrub border it is excellent, and has the merit of doing well in :rather poor sail. If pruned hard 'back each winter or spring, as it should be, it only grows from 15 inches to 1$ inches high, and in, July and early August is covered with its large; heads of deep _rose red 11,,wers,—" The Garden." I.ll.AckaEmuts.—These provide a very useful crop during the early autumn, and the plants are greatly benefited by one Or two good soak- ings of- niannre-Water, e_specially during the ti.me the fruits are setting. Green fly is some- times troublesont,. more particularly about the (Continued :3t next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>FAMINE PRICES. WORK AND WAGES. The Days When a Four-Pound Loaf The State of the Metal and Leather Cost Tenpence. Trades. EXPENSIVE "SEVENTIES."</head>
        <p>FAMINE PRICES. WORK AND WAGES. The Days When a Four-Pound Loaf The State of the Metal and Leather Cost Tenpence. Trades. EXPENSIVE "SEVENTIES."</p>
        <p>[" EXarailler " Special." If food prices hi Warrington have risen.con- siderably, we have not yet. reached , the ".famine" rates of the " seventies." Bacon can go, up .to a record if necessary, but other edibles will scarcely reach the figures demanded . and paid forty years .ago. "I *remember the time well," said a worthy aged housekeeper to the "Examiner" inves- tigator, "The French and Germans were light- ing, and we poor folk had to pay more for our keep. But the war made plenty of work, and wages were good, so we could afford to Pdy good prices for food. "lii 1871 the four-pound -loaf 'cost tonpence. You could, not get it .for less. They said . the Frerichies were buying all the itour; and Canada did not send us wheat then.. Imagine having to pay sixpence' for a 1)01111(1 of sugar, and I think ‘a pound of tea cost .something like</p>
        <p>precious in those day, and 3s. " Tea' was very a pound was made .to last a long time. We all drank .weak tea, and before the leaves were * finally thrown, away they • were used again to brew still weaker tea for the children.</p>
        <p>Coals 'Very ',ear.</p>
        <p>coa.s utso rose in price, and in 1870-1 you .ad to pay about zBs. tor a ton. bacon, uucr sold al, nigh. prices. nveryiiiing was \ cry ueztr. bntener ;nein was so costly Lout illObt poor peopie could not buy Alter tue w„,-, prices however came down, anii so aid wag„s. t tie colliers uuring the war were making Xj.it Or x 5 per week, and later Lucy could not earn more man 3us. " We seem to be coming back to the high prices of the • seventies.' remember twenty years ago prime Cheshire bacon -could be bought Jur o.ld. per pound, an, d now it 'is ild. at least. Ham,- twenty yearS ago; was 6a. and 7d. per lb., while now it is Is. to Is: 3a. Heady boiled ham, 25 years ago, cost is. to is. 4d. per lb.' to-day it is about In those old days most of us used to have our own hams, for hundreds of folk kept pigs, and killed anti cured them for their own use." More Luxuries. , Do not the People have more luxuries to-day than forty years ago?" Of course they have. Tea was -a luxury then, but it, is not to-day. Cocoa was prac- tically unknown. It was not Until 1880 that I knew what cocoa was. A sample was left at the house, and, thinking that it w;ould have to be made like tea or coftee, we stewed it in the teapot. Nly, it tasted .awful. It was,SOille time before we forgot the taste, and learned to like cocoa. "Then look at the jains we have nowadays. Forty -years ago jam was rarely seen on the tables of working folk, and any little there was to be seen was all home made. -Very few folk knew hoW to make jam. Now we can buy the best kinds made by --artleY for a few coppers per lb. "Confectionery was .another thing not often seen. The chief was home-made gingerbread. But look at the choice we can get now at any baker's shop." -</p>
        <p>A Candle Thrown In. "You think that the necessaries of life are, high as they are now, not so dear as forty</p>
        <p>' years ago?" . 4.1-es. while some things are dear, such as goes quite as far as it did in the.' seven- bacon, other things are cheaper. A .sovereign to-day ties,' hut • not 'so far as it did in tie cheaper times JAventy years ago. "Things Were still worse during the Crimean War. I have heard my .Mother say • that in 1856 -file._ four-pound loaf of ,bread. cost. but the bakr would not e If- cut of the shilling. He Rei)t, the gave you a candle to make up for ' e give you th lia penny, change . sthheillhinalfgny."</p>
        <p>Mr. S. W. Royce and Co., Manchester, report at the end of July k,s follows regard- ing the metal markets : At ter the hi avy fall recorded in values of pig iron a month ago there has been during the *month comparatively a calm, prices of Scotch iron showing a fall of is. per ton and of Mkidlesbro' iron an advance of 6d. per ton. Some fair business has been done, shipments are satisfactory and stocks at Middlesbro' are showing some decrease, and although latterly the inquiry isl slack, many people being away on holiday, the market has a fairly good tone. Copper was dull and receded slightly in the early part of the month, but has since improved slowly but steadily, showing an advance of .E 2 from the lowest or 255. during the month, and is now firm. Tin fell heavily during the first three weeks of this month reaching the lowest price for this year or last, but has since advanced £8 per ton, and is strong. Spelter is about ss. down and is slightly firmer. Pig lead has advanced about 20s. per ton and is strong. LEATHER PRICES RISING.</p>
        <p>Dealing with Warrington and district the "Leather World" says :—July figures may be considered as quite satisfactory; there was some falling away in amount as compared with June, but as the latter month saw the clearing out of some accumulations of offal, the difference is largely accounted for, and we are certainly, far ahead of the usual July turnover. Buyers have been covering the ground very thoroughly on the look-out for suit- able sto:k to come forward, but the real difficulty is the impossibility of finding the stuff wanted. For a long time past all the demand has run on one line—light goods; what there was within measurable distance has been sold, and it is now quite out of the question to find even a tithe of the light bends which are wanted. Leather prices are gradually moving up, and signs are that they will go far before the top is finally reached. In English, all weights from 6 to 101 b. are being quoted 20d. to 21d. for best selections, 12's and 14's at 201 d. to 20K, and 16's at 21d. to 211 d. Secondary selections bring about -‘7ld. to id. less, and thirds about a further penny down. No. 1 English shoulders, light, 131 d.; -mediums, 13d. to 131 d. ; heavies, 12d. to 121 d. English bellies, 10-id.; mediums, 9-14 d. to 10d.; and heavies, 91d. In rough leather, strap butts are offered sparingly, and I am informed the following have been made : For 14's to 20's, 221 d., and for all weights over, 22d.; rough shoulders for strap dressers have sold readily at 15d. to 16d. On the balance, hides in July were up a full farthing over June rates, and, unfor - tunately, it is too clear that the movement is not spasmodic, as careful statistics taken personally show the movement has been systematically regular over the period of five years taken for comparison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>70 CHECK MALINGERING. Rechabite High Chief Ruler and the Doctors' Attitude.</head>
        <p>70 CHECK MALINGERING. Rechabite High Chief Ruler and the Doctors' Attitude.</p>
        <p>The proceedings of the forty-sixth High Move- able Conference of the Independent Order of Rechabites began at Ayr on Tuesday, Mr. W. A. Platt, of Bury, High Chief Ruler, presiding. In his presidential address, Mr. Platt, who h offialelultitneentitohn to the Insurance Past two years, a s been devoted in e 0 ns de a Act. Their society, he said, had always con- tended that State. insurance to be effective must be compulsory, and the Act had already proved beneficial, their own provisions for dealing with it having .ensured comparatively smooth work- ing. Some minor amendments, which he thought were desirable, were that the three waiting days -should be made wcirking days, that women married before' July .15 last year and subsequently becoming uneniPloyed should be allowed to become special voluntary contribu- tors, that national health insurance business should be .transacted free of stamp duty, and that societies should be relieved of the cost of free transfers and of the Cost of filling in the medical index slips: " UNREASONABLE." ATTITUDE OF THE DOCTORS. A larger question was a medical one. The attitude of the doctors in 'refusing to attend their uninsured and. juvenile members was un- reasonable, and, failing their coming to terms, the friendly societies must ask for leave to com- bine and form a medical association for all their members, or, preferably, for a State medical service. Despite the unexpectedly heavy sick- ness claims he did not blame malingering as much as some people did, but the duty of check- ing_ malingerino. lay. in the hands of the doctors, and if they had not courage enough to do it, medical referees should be State appointed and paid. Interest at present centred on the amend- ing Bills in Parliament, and while they would know their requirements better after .three years' experience and a valuation, at present they wanted amendments providing medical treatment for aged or permanently disabled members of friendly societies not insured under the Act, to provide full benefits in case of insured persons between 21 and 70. 'to secure medical and sanatorium benefits .for persons exempted frOm compulsory insurance, so that ot their rb paide by their employers might be used half, and to relieve members when unemployed, or on sick funds, from paying the employer's contrib ttion. ,Dealiirg with other affairs of the Order, he remarked on the record increase of the last two years, but said that as the compulsion of the Insurance Act forced all adults into approved ,societies. there must be a limit to their expan- sion. Whilst, however, they could not modify their total abstinence pledge to gain members. they could consolidate, give more attention to propaganda- and more to children. After a vote of thanks ,to Mr. Platt. he pro- nosed a. vote of eondolence with those bereaved by the Bishon-Brico2s mine disaster, which was carried by a standing vote. The conference is by the Jamest on rpoora. about 330 officers and &amp;legates h'int' ;11 attend- 'nee. The representatives of the Warringto,, district are Bros.. C. S. Fellows (onditnr), T. A rrawsmith. and S. Feriley. Re,wora and Prorlatain are represented by Bro. Edward H.</p>
        <p>Helridge.</p>
        <p>- -- FARM AND GARDEN.—Continued. time the plants are in bloom, and if the insects are noticed the plants should be. sprayed at once, or the fruit may be spoiled.----" The</p>
        <p>Garden." , WiNnow BONES.--The watering and training Of plants hi window boxes aeed constant attention, for by these means the plants are maintained in good .healtli, and the disptay all that the grower could well desire. ln all cases where the plants are growing away luxuriantly and flowering freely, they should be watered will( soot water or liquid manure (gwitio) twice each week. - Plants that should be doing well just now are the following: Mar- guerib.'s, Fuchsias, Pelarionimns, Calceolarias Petunias, Heliotropes, and Lobelias. They are the more popular subjects, although [tic are. if course, ninny other equally pretty ; useful plants that bike very kindly to cut ill window hoxeS. I have seen very that effects made wilh. Violas and Pansies. blooms shoula be persistently removed - the plants. as they detract from the heaut the display, and seed pods very soon c, deterioration to take place.— " EconOmist and Horticultural Review."</p>
        <p>tioe</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WOMEN WORKERS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Some Industrial Aspects of the Suffrage Question.</p>
        <p>PILGRIMAGE IMPRESSIONS.</p>
        <p>E (I.pme admits that the conditions of life in most of our large-towns are deplorable, /but seldom has it been realised more poignantly than by the Suffrage pilgrims as they walked through miles of sordid streets and saw the many hopeless and degraded faces. There is, indeed, much work to be (lone: and .though no one thinks that the advent of Woman Suffrage will at once bring the millennium, we do think that "two heads are better than one," and that as the home is managed better, and the children are better brought up, when husband and wife work together, so in the *ate, the many pro- blems can be .faced with more hope of a solu- tion if women take their share with men. Many opponents of Woman Suffrage fail to realise that it is the desire for service, rather than the wish to exercise a privilege, that is the motive power of our movement. We find ourselves, in every department of social work, handicapped by our want of political influence. This is why many: women are giving .up other social work to devote themselves wholly to the task of winning the vote, realising that they Can do little good without it. There is thus a terrible waste of good work that might be used at -once for the service of the country. But, whose fault is this waste? No one would be more thankful than these women to return to their work, and it rests with the Government to enable them to do so. WHERE THE SHOE PINCHES. It is often said that men are quite willing and anxious to legislate in the true interests of women. This is often the case ; but " you've got to be a woman to know where the shoe pinches." Through Want of knowledge, or lack of iinaginationg men, with the best4inten- tions, make terrible mistakes in matters dealing with women. HoW is: it that under. the: National Insurance Act (which is paid for by the taxes of men and women) married wbmen; who are not working outside their home, do not receive sick benefit? How is it that the mater- nity benefit (a splendid thing, if only it were properly administered) is paid to the father, and not to the mother? But, it may be objected, we admit that the Insurance Act needs amendment, and is indeed now in the procesS of 'being amended. That is true, but there is no single amendment in favour of women. There is no Government proposal to give the maternity benefit to the mother; and, more than that, the men's trade unions are organising themselves against any transference of that benefit from the father. No measure has ever ,set in a stronger light the need for Woman Suffrage than the Insurance Act.</p>
        <p>EQUAL PAY FOB EQUAL WORK. The principle of equal pay for equal work, so essential to the interests of men and women alike, is set forth in a judgment recently deliv- ered in Australia by Mr. Justice Higgins "This' is the first time that this (Arbitration) Court has had to deal directly with the ques- tion of female labour. The 'union here insists on equal pay for equal work. This phrase seems to carry justice on its face, for obviously, where a- woman produces as good results as a man in the same kind of work, she ought not, to get less remuneration." Ike continues:— "The State Cannot afford to ,let a girl With a comfortable home pull down wages, nor ' demand that the employer shall make 'himself acquainted- with the domestic necessities of every employe; There' has been observed for a long time a tendency to substitute women for men in industries, and it is often the result of women being paid lower wages." .The " Mel- bourne Age," approving of this decision, says "Woman entered late into the industrial arena, rind she still bears- the'-consequenees of her erstwhile political inferiority : henceforth 4 equal pay for 'equal -work.' will possess th, furor., of on authoritatively accepted principle of national ethics"</p>
        <p>E. L. B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>HOUSING PROBLEM. Cheshire Medical Officer Suggests Temporary Dwellings. LYME HOUSE FAMINE.</head>
        <p>HOUSING PROBLEM. Cheshire Medical Officer Suggests Temporary Dwellings. LYME HOUSE FAMINE.</p>
        <p>The housing problem in rural Cheshire :is acute, and how to cope with it forms the sub- ject of a lengthy article by 1)1.. Meredith Young, the medical officer for the county, in the annual report just issued. - Dr. Young's view is that in Cheshire the erec- tion of cottages to suit the agricultural labourer cannot be economically effected under the Housing and Town Planning Act, and he suggests that councils should sanction the erection of temporary dwellings of a limited life. This course—though not for farm labourer tenants—has been adopted in North- Wiell rural district. j have -seen these buildings," says Dr. young, " and am much Mil-messed with thcir possibilities, though, like most temporary dwellings, they are not adequately proof against variations in temperature ; they are excellently ' planned and fitted up. " Facts could be adduced to shoW that the erection of cottages tii.suit the agricultural</p>
        <p>labourer cannot be economically erected, for it may- be plainly stated that in this part of the country' at all events, the rental which can be. afforded by farm labourers is limited by the figure of 3s. per week, and I have never yet' heard of any cottage suitable for such people which could be erected and prove self-main- taining (to. say nothing of paying interest on capital invested) if it were let at a less rental than 4s. to 6d. per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ONE SOLUTION</head>
        <p>THE ONE SOLUTION</p>
        <p>"There is only one solution of the difficulty which is at all satisfactory, and that is for councils to take advantage of section 27 of the, Public Health Acts (Amendment) Act, 1907, and sanction the erection of temporary buildings, limiting the'time.for which these are to stand to a reasonably long period. Most temporary</p>
        <p>buildings, if iiroper:ly" put together. and</p>
        <p>. _ perly eared for, have a life olabout• 25 years, or possibly more, and \fiat a little more care in .building, the disadvantages incidental to</p>
        <p>extremes Of. temperature can be minimised. "The only other possibility is for councils to build two classes of working man's house— one class with a few extras in ,the way of accommodation than the other—and by charg- ing a slightly higher rental for the better-class house, enable this to assist in reducing their loss in the lower-class house. But the margin is an exceedingly narrow one, and one has to be specially favoured in the waiter of .site. labour. and materials before success can ,be attained." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>LYAIM WANTS COTTAGES</head>
        <p>LYAIM WANTS COTTAGES</p>
        <p>Dealing with the question of hOusing Lyinni, the report states that in 1012 six ne) houses only were erected in the distric</p>
        <p>Systematic house-to-house inspection had he',</p>
        <p>c(;ntinued</p>
        <p>"I am of opinion that, there is a shortage ni cottages with three bedrooms, but the extent of this shortage we are not yet in a. position to state. We find small families with the larger houses and vice-versa.. Up to the present we have been able fairly. Well to deal_ with the cases of actual overcrowding, but, we are tO adhere willt definite rigidity to 300 cubic feet. Per person in sleeping rooms, before long some more three bedrootned cottages will be needed. " Again, a sanitary I.home' means 111010 than builder or inspeetor can effect, it includes the intelligent co-operation of the tenant, it ITICIIIIS leant mess in house, person and surroundings. It means cleanly habits. seap and wafer, and open winLlows. You will find two houses side by side, exactly similar in age and -structure, one a thome--the other a hovel. Much of this lies beyond the scope and power of Medical Officer of Health or Inspector. but often it renders much of their labour futile."'</p>
        <p>_ in the area controlled by the Iluocorn Burnt Counnit, the report states that the dense,' popu- lated parts are receiving consider:dion, and that it, is intended to proceed wil the rural portions later. Alterations in houses had been effected in the townships.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>UNITED STATES. Significant Change in Immigration Statistics. COST OF LIVING.</head>
        <p>THE UNITED STATES. Significant Change in Immigration Statistics. COST OF LIVING.</p>
        <p>The Report for the year 1912 of Mr. Consul-General Bennett on the trade of the Consular District of New York, affords interesting reading. The Report shows that last year there was a decreastiin the amount of emigration from this country to the IJ.S.A Only 19 per cent. of the whole immigration came from Northern and Western Europe, while 68 per cent. came from the Iberic and Slavonian countries of • Eastern and Southern Europe and Western Asia. and 19 per cent. from Italy and Russia respectively. The enormous amount or emigration from the two last named countries is illustrated by the following figures showing the change which has occurred since 1855 in the distribution Of population in the southern part of Manhattan Island. Whereas in 1855 the population consisted of 14,535 English, 110,466 Irish, 73,952 Germans, 834 Italians, and 1,149 Russians, the figures in 1910 were: 5,741 English, 18,247 Irish, 16,335 Germans, 107,456 Italians, and 192,000 Russians. - In view of the notorious indisposition Of English immigrants to submit to the unsatisfactory 'conditions prevailing in American factories, it is not surprising to read that whereas formerly the work in those factories was done by English,Scotch, Irish, and Welsh people, with a fair sprinkling of native-born Americans, to-day these races are quite outnumbered by the mass of immigrants from Southern Europe. In the textile mills of RhOde Island, for example, British workers have been practically eliminated, their places being taken by Hungarians, Poles, Italians, and Jews from Russia and Central Europe. The report contains a table of compara- tive prices of the main articles of food in 1911-12, which shows that there has been a consistent rise in the price of nearly all articles of food and household consumption. Taking the average price for the ten-year period 1890-1899 as a basis, the cost of liv- ing had increased by 586 per cent. in 1912 —in other words, the purchasing power or a dollar had been reduced to about 63 cents. Although there has been an advance in wages, the report makes it clear that "even at a liberal estimate of the average increase, the average workingman cannot to-day with the results of a day's labour buy as much food as he could buy in 1899. Wages have gone up, but have not kept pace with the increase in living expenses."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Commercial Failures.</head>
        <p>Commercial Failures.</p>
        <p>According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette, the number of Commercial Failures published in Great Britain and Ireland during the first four weeks ending Saturday, July 26th, was 516. The number in the corresponding four weeks of last year was 625, showing a decrease of 109, being a decrease in 1913, to date, of 396. The number of Administration Orders published in England and Wales for the four weeks ending July 26th, was 449. The number in the corresponding four weeks of last year was 430, showing an increase of 19, being a net decrease in 1913, to date, 0r149. The number of Bills of Sale (including re-registrations) published in England and Wales for the four weeks ending Saturday. July 26th was 581. The number in the corresponding four weeks of last year was 633, showing a decrease of 52, being a net decrease in 1913, to date, of 285, The number, published in Ireland for the same four weeks was 9. The number in the corresponding four weeks of last year was 15, showing a decrease of 6, being a net decrease in 1913, to date, of 8. The mortgages and charges registered by limited companies in Ireland during the four weeks ending Saturday, July, 26th, amounted to £92,500. The amount for the corresponding four weeks of last year was £3,000. The total amount registered in 1913, to date, was .£352,300 (in addition to the issues in previous years by the same companies), as compared with £174,911 for the corresponding period of 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>Do Athletic Pursuits Develop Beauty</p>
        <p>of Form?</p>
        <p>LADY " SHOTS " AT BISLEY.</p>
        <p>The wedding of ,Prince Arthur of Connaught and the Duchess of Fife, fixed for the 15th of October, will be a notable event of the autumn season, and the two Royal fiancees, the Duchess of Fife, alid the princess Victoria Augusta of Hohenzollern—the bride-elect of King Manuel of • Portugal—naturally arouse much interest at the present moment. When Prince Arthtir of Connaught marries, there will re- main four bachelor -grandsons and four un-</p>
        <p>married granddaughters of Queen Victoria. These are the three sons of Princess Henry of</p>
        <p>lenberg and Prince Albei:t, of Schleswig-</p>
        <p>Holstein. - It is curious to note how few have been the male descendants of the younger sons of the, monarchs of the House of Hanover. Queen Victoria's sons were not an exception to this rule. Her second, son, the Duke of Edinburg left no male heir; :the Duke of Connaught has only one son; the Duke of Albany left but one—the present remaining Duke of ,Saxe Coburg-Gotha', to which duchy Prince Arthur relinquished his right in favour ,of his cousin. The unmarried granddaughters of Queon Victoria are Princess Victoria, a daughter of Icing Edward, Princess Patricia of</p>
        <p>Connaught, and the Princess Victoria of Schles- wig-Holstein,- a-daughter-of Prince 'and Princess Christian.</p>
        <p>RIFLE-SHOOTING FOR WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Since the early nineties, rule-shooting for women has steadily increased in favour, and the "lady" shot at Bisley is an established fact. The pioneer -Of service rifle-shooting amongst women was an American—Mrs. W. C. Leah, of Jersey, who was mainly instrumental in forming - and coaching the team that won the Kolaporc Cup in 1898. Mrs. Chapman was another veil-known shot at that time, and in the last few years the number of markswomen has largely increased. Women now fire at Bis- ley in open competition with men, using Ser- vice rifleS, but hitherto no organised competi- tion between the sexes has been arranged. This year, however, teams of men and women will compete in a formal match On the miniature ranges. IL is Tnierpossible that in the near future- schoolgirls, will be taught to shoot, and that-competitions will take place between boys' and girls' schools, similar to those now in vogue between the various colleges and schools for boys.</p>
        <p>THE FEMININE FIGURE. The change that has taken Place of late in the feminine figure is so marked that scieWisis assert the coming woman will be void of grace</p>
        <p>and beauty of form, if: attractive in feature. Sculptors share the same opinion and assert that women are -gradually departing from the highest Standard of art—the Greek type. Dress experts, too, state that a new type of woman is rapidly developing, and that modern ath- letics and games do not develop female sym- metry, as did the Greek games, appears to be the general opinion of artists .and _scientists. Broad:shoulffers and flat feet are characteristics of the new type that,:linOs little favour s,,v*itil artists.</p>
        <p>AUTUMN 1,1711 cAl'F&gt;4. - There is little doubt that the chief decoration of gowns for the coining autumn and winter will be on the hips, and to preserve the slimness of line, Which Fashion, still demands, will re- quire ho little .ingenuity on the part of the tailor and dressmaker. Draperies are to be a feature of the coming Modes, but designers may be trusted to' show care and discretion in their arrangement. A, rilturri f the f.ir cape predicted for the autumn also, but the new shapes are quite different from anything with which we are familiar at present, or that has hitherto been known by that name. N.,jther round, nor Pointed, time neW capes dip on one side, and are raised on the other ; they will therefore be irregular as to length, and cer- tainly may lay claim to nevelty. Brown is to be a favourite colour for the lining of these garments. Marabou is likely to be much uscd in the place of fur, as trimmings for carves, and edgings on tunics, coatees, and the like.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TUNIC c.oATEE</head>
        <p>THE TUNIC c.oATEE</p>
        <p>The vogue of the tunic has become a triumph. In its many modifications it is, this sunnuer, seen on dresses- for every part of the day, for every function and 'occasion: It has undergone numerous transformations since its first intr:)-</p>
        <p>- - duetion in the form of a pannier, suggested</p>
        <p>modern sartorial artists,•by the dresses and modes in vogue in the days of lhe Treorio). Shorn of its original and characteristic fulness and bunchiness, it fell into line with Ihe rre- vailing craze for slim effects, and became deser- vedly 'popular, for it adapts itself to the g:,..ner- ably of figures, and is,, in 'addition, a useful mode. • In its latest modification, the coatee (a cross between a• Russian coat and a tunic) has harked back to the pannier, in one res-..-ct —that .of fulness—but, being made of thin, .almost filmy, materials, the fulness adds not at :all to the slim effect fashion still detTlall•lS ,of her votaries. This coatee is made chiefly of -a very ,soft. thin .satin, ,- in some • bright colour, that Contrasts with, the frilled lace 'skirts' worn with it.</p>
