        <p>CAPTAIN SCOTT S KEEN tig APPRECIATION OF HIS COMRADP- of Never, it may be believed, has a Parmr" gl explorers combined so much of intelleottlit power with physical fitness, and the res.B,;lol was apparent in the high level of nitinp appreciation, of intelligent co-operal"_ig: and, wise enthusiasm. There were it'sg takes, of course, but errors due to e5.c11-5t 'rather than defect Of zeal; while a specu/ito in some practical ,job might be unecltlgt: the abstract calculations connected wiwi lap The salient fact was that the bt1_11„- relations, the moral and social a; phere, from first to last continued itu a cloud. ot4 Time after time Scott is impelled to likpr, this " marked and beneficent chara,c',/ie istic of our community." To quote lcalb;el passage: " I am very much imPre's:roi with the extraordinary and gent cordiality of the relations which ekl;ot amongst our people. Ido not supPose.:ely. a statement of the real truth—nan- be that there is no friction at all—W,";,10 credited; it is so generally thoughl' "life the many rubs of such a meD( as this are quietly and purP,.`liefe sunk in oblivion. With me is no need to draw a veil; thereiclea nothing to cover. There are no strs,a'ore relations in this hut, and nothing..'eg emphatically evident than the unive': amicable spirit which is shown occasions: Such a state of affairs -W° delightfully surprising under aliLicom, ditions ; but it is much more so vo'4o' remembers the diverse assortment company. This theme is worthy of °lot; sion. To-night Oates, captain in s„ing cavalry regiment, has been scraSat'o), over chairs and tables with Debovr„tall young Australian student. It is a trl" to have collected such men." +e istic This interesting and charac,-- rtli passage is reported in facsimil° oe. August number of the "Strand Maga-</p>
