      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>"'_,.......-_</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 9, 1913. HEARD IN THE COURTS. ' AN INJURED WORKER.</p>
        <p>ployed by him. There was a man whose name was Conlon. Defendant cross-examined McHugh at some length as to whether it was really Connelly whom he employed, and McHugh strongly asserted that it was.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (to defendant) : Do you say that this man was not employed by you? Defendant: His name is not in the book, unless it is a clerical error.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : Will you swear on oath that he was not employed by you? Defendant': No. Defendant was ordered to pay .R 1 and the whole of the costs in the last two cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>• MARKET STALL PILFERING. Warrington W'oman's Smart Capture.</head>
        <p>• MARKET STALL PILFERING. Warrington W'oman's Smart Capture.</p>
        <p>A Warrington w9man, who keeps a stall at Widnes Market, had an encountet- with a pil- -1 ferer, and the sequel was heard at Widnes Police Court on Monday, when Hannah Phillips, a married woman, of Derby-road, Farnworth, pleaded guilty to the theft of a cotton skirt and bag, the property of Mrs. Shaw, second-hand dealer, 287, Liverpool-road, Warrington. Superintendent Foster said on Saturday night Phillips made several purchases at a stall occu- pied by Mrs. Shaw. While Mrs. Shaw was attending to another customer she saw prisoner stoop two or three times at one end of the stall. There was a little girl with her, and Mrs. Shaw was amazed to see the woman placing articles in a bag, which the girl was holding open. Mrs. Shaw accosted the woman and demanded to know what she had taken. The woman said her girl had . picked up a frock which had. been dropped by a man. Mrs. Shaw found that the frock belonged to her. . Phillips admitted the theft and was arrested. She had been let go for a similar offence a fort- night before. Major Wareing said such offences in markets were too frequent, and they must make an. example. Phillips would be fined Os.. includ- ing costs, or have 14 days .in prison as an alter- native.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MEAN THEFT. Three Months for Stealing Sixpence</head>
        <p>A MEAN THEFT. Three Months for Stealing Sixpence</p>
        <p>from a Child.</p>
        <p>What the Mayor described as the mealiest and most contemptible theft he had 'heard of, was investigated at Warrington Police Court on Monday, when Michael Hagan', a well-known local Character, was sent to prison for three months for .stealing sixpence, the money of Sarah Jones, 87, Priestley,street.. On Saturday night Alm .Tones sent her little girl to draw 4s. from a Mrs. Ryder, Bank-street. As she was coming down the steps of the house she slipped, and the money rolled into the street. Prisoner, who was standing near, was observed by several people to rush forward and pick up the sixpence. An officer took the prisoner into custody and on the way to the Police Station Hagan complained .of toothache. Whilst the officer's attention was diverted, pris- oner slipped the sixpence out of his mouth, and dropped it on to the ground behind him. Prisoner complained that he had not been given a chance, as he had only been nine hours out of prison. and he . was brought, there on that paltry' charge. It would have been an easy matter for him to have swallowed the six- pence. The Mayor: Have you ever swallowed a six- pence? Prisoner: Before I would get convicted on such a charge. I would do anythincr. The Chief Constable said that last Friday nrisoner came to the Police Station and saw Mr. Thomas. the deputy police court missioner. He told that gentleman it was his intention to re- form. and obtained a note for presentation to a local clothier for a snit of clothes. He was supplied. and it was afterwards ascertained that be had sold the clothes for more money than the clothier had actually sold them for. Prisoner had a bad record, and made his nth appearance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>UNWILLING WITNESS. Warrington Wounding Charge</head>
        <p>UNWILLING WITNESS. Warrington Wounding Charge</p>
        <p>Reduced.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, the adjourned -case was heard 'in which Thomtof Fearnley (69), Silver-square, was charged with unlawfully wounding his sister, Mrs. Elizabeth Bishop, 4, Silver-square, and his brother-in-law. John Bialiop. • The Chief Constable said that on July 26th prisoner was alleged to have . struCk his Sister over the head with a hammer, and dealt his brother-in-law a blow in the face with his fist. Dr. Law, .who-examined the.woman at the Infir- mary, 'found her. stiffering from concussion of the brain, slight fracture, of the skull, lacera- tion two inches in extent just in front of the left ear, a bruised right eye, partial fracture of the fourth rib and bruised right side. When charged; prisoner practically admitted the , offence and said the woman threatened him with a poker. About 10.30 on the night in question prisoner, who was drunk, was creating a die-. turbance in the street when Mrs. Bishop remonstrated with him. He procured a hammer, and it was alleged that he struck his sister across the head with it. He (the Chief) was of the opinion that the woman was now an unwilling witness. On the Chief Constable's recommendation, the, charge was reduced to one of aggravated assault, as the prisoner was not in such good health. John Bishop (58), 4, Silver-square, said he had not been well for some time. In answer to the Chief Constable. he could not say whether he was struck or whether he had a fall. " Tom," he. said,-" was a good lad, only when in drink." Prisoner left himself at the mercy of the Court. Ile would never taste beer any more as long as he lived. This affair bad given him a shock. Mrs. Bishop said she probably used impmlence to the prisoner and nrovoked him. She did not, knoir• Whiither •she ,slitype&amp;or not. • • Prisoner. was bound over for six months in one surety of .1:5, snd was ordered to pay the eosts of the case—Xl is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>FOOLISH PRACTICE. Labourer's Fatal Ride on Motor Trailer.</head>
        <p>A FOOLISH PRACTICE. Labourer's Fatal Ride on Motor Trailer.</p>
        <p>TRIALS OF A 'DRIVER.</p>
        <p>Tramping from Clitheroe to Warrington on Thursday of last week, two men jumped on to the draw bar of a motor waggon and trailer. When in the borough, where it was their inten- tion to look for work, one. of .the men,. Daniel O'Brien (FL),, a. general labourer, of no fixed, abode,. alighted from the bar.. ~.As his. feet touched the ground, he •slipped, and was run over by the. trailer.. AS a result, .he died in the Warrington Infirmary on. Thursday night: . Coroner Brighouse conducted the inquest at the Coroner's .court on Tuesday, and com- mented on the dangerous practise of riding on the trailer bars. Elizabeth Moss, 32, Deal-street, off Chappell Hill, Huddersfield, said deceased was her brother. She•last saw him several Months ago, when he worked at Leach's Chemical Works. Prior to that time she had not seen him for twelve years. She had a. letter from him at Warrington three months 'ago. Timothy Doyle, unemployed general labourer, said he had known deceased for three months. They had walked from Clitheroe. through Blackburn, Damen. Bolton and Manchester, and were coming to Warrington to try and get work on the timber • boats. When about three miles from Warrington they jumped on to the motor waggon. The driver did not know they were on the draw bar. In Buttermarket-street deceased was sitting on the near side of the waggon and tried to get off. As soon as his feet touched the ground, the 'corner of the lurry struck him, knocking him forward. The wheels nassed over his legs, and be (witness) shouted to the driver to stop. .They found der•eased lvinf, in the roadway n, few yards- behind, and bleeding from the thighs.• • . A GREAT TEMPTATION.</p>
        <p>The Coroner characterised the practice of riding on the bar as a nuisance. Engineers had been trying to think of some device to pre- vent men- getting on the bar, but had been un- successful. Of course, when men were tired of plodding along the road, he supposed it was a great temptation to jump on. A Juror: They will risk anything. Richard Ashton, the driver of the motor waggon, said he was employed by Mr. William C. Taylor, Shaw, near Oldham, and lived at 119, Salt-street, Shaw. He was carrying yarn, and when in Buttermarket-street he felt the engine give a snatch. He pulled up within 10 yards, as he was only going at the rate of about three miles per hour. It was his intention to stop 30 -yards further up the road, if the acci- dent had not happened. He had been a driver for 10 years, and had never experienced an accident before. They could not keep men off the bar, and it was annoying to the drivers. Another nuisance was that . men sometimes mounted the trailer. even if it contained cotton, and commenced smoking, which was a very risky practice. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death." They expressed their sympathy with the relatives, and said that the driver of the waggon was in no way to blame, as he did not know the men were on the bar. They also com- plimented him on the open way in which he had given his evidence. The Coroner expressed agreement with the jury. It reflected great credit on Ashton that he had been driving along the crowded high roads in Lancashire for so long a time without experiencing an accident.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>BOYS' ESCAPADES.</head>
        <p>BOYS' ESCAPADES.</p>
        <p>Children Waylaid and Robbed in the</p>
        <p>Streets.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday, before Mr. A. J. .Jolley (in the chair) and other magistrates, Walter Dunn (12), 59, Grafton-street, and Albert Gilbert (11), 397, WeMeld-street, were charged with stealing a watch, value 10s., the property of Mr. ify. Martin, and 2s. 6d. from a little girl named Holden. Dunn was also charged with stealing is. from a child in the street:</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that one of the boys kept watch outside Mr. Martin's house while the other (Mr. Martin's nephew) went in and stole the watch. They offered the watch to another boy for Is. 6d., and he said, "Come with me to my mother, as I have no money." The woman was suspicious, and said she would buy the watch, providing the boy could go home with the two defendants and see if it was their watch. When they got some little distance up the road, the defendants said they were going for a drink of water,, but .the other boy said he would go with them. They said' they would hit him if he did not leave them. The boys had been thieving wholesale. They had waylaid children, who had been sent errands by their parents. and had stolen money from them. They had stolen doorkeys and had admitted it was their intention to. get into the premises. They had broken into a Picture palace, and were com- mitting thefts daily. A number of keys had been found buried in the backyard at Dunn's home. Dunn had been pilfering wholesale since he was birched in March, and in most of his escapades the Other boy had been associated with him. - Dunn was sent to .the Lostock Junction 'Re- formatory School until he attained the age of sixteen, and Gilbert was ordered to receive six strokes with the birch.</p>
        <p>An order was made for the payment by Dunn's father of is. 6d. per week towards his maintenance. The Chief Constable explained that the cost to the ratepayers and the State would be from Bs. to 9s. per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>NOVEL POLICE CALL.</head>
        <p>NOVEL POLICE CALL.</p>
        <p>A Wife's Ingenuity in Summoning</p>
        <p>Assistance.</p>
        <p>A flower vase was put to a novel use by a, Warrington woman. She wanted police assist- ance, as she alleged that she was being assaulted by her husband, so she threw a large vase to the ground. The crash reached the ears of a policeman, and he promptly went to the aid of the woman.</p>
        <p>A sequel to this novel method of summoning police assistance was heard in the Warrington Police Court yesterday, when Thomas Butler appeared to answer a charge of assaulting his wife, Mary Ann, at their house at 11, Winwick- road, Warrington. The wife said that defendant recently bad been in the habit of assaulting her when she asked him for money to keep the house. He had struck her and seized her by the throat. On Wednesday night she found, on her return from a picture theatre, that he had another woman in the house, and because she com- plained he assaulted her, and she had to sum- mon the police. Butler averred that his wife got her injuries, she showed in court, in a fight with the other woman but this was denied by Mrs. Butler. P.C. Burrows said he was in Winwick-road, when he heard the vase smashed. He went in the direction from whence the noise came. and met the wife, who complained that the husband had been ill-using her. She said she broke the var ,so as to attract attention and .get help. From what what lie gathered 'the trouble between husband and wife was due to the fact that Butler would not give his wife money, and per- sisted in going about with another woman. Butler: Yes.. my wife was fighting the •other woman, and all I did was to fetch the missus into the house. - - _</p>
        <p>' Mrs. Butler-: No; when I got home from the pictures I found him with the other woman in the house. find he told me to get.outside. The Mag cst rates adjourned the case ' for a month to see how Butler would have to his wife. They warned him to give up keeping company with other women.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>INSURANCE ACT WARNING.</head>
        <p>INSURANCE ACT WARNING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Builder Fined for Failing to Return Cards. CONTRIBUTIONS UNPAID.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, before his Honour Dudley Stuart Smith, K.C. (vice-chancellor of the County Palatine) and other magistrates, Charles Henry Johnstone, builder, Windyhaugh, Sankey, was summoned for failing to return the unemploy- ment 'lnsurance ;boots to John McHugh, and Elias Lomax, whom he had employed, on the termination of their employment.—He pleaded guilty. Mr. W. B. Forshaw prosecuted on behalf of the Board of Trade, and said the prosecution was under, ,ipiart 2,, of ,the NonatiAtwrance Act, and- altogether the defendant would be charged with some eight offences. Had they proceeded in all the cases there would have been about 30 offences to inquire into. They did not want to be vindictive, but they wanted to stop this sort of thing. After reading the regulations of the Act, Mr. Forshaw said that if the employer .kept the cards back, it inflicted a great hardship on the workmen and the Labour Exchange. They were only proceeding in those two cases of not handing the cards back to the men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>REFUSAL OR OMISSION?</head>
        <p>REFUSAL OR OMISSION?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: It was a refusal? • Mr. Forshaw : That is so. The, Chairman: Do you -mean a refusal or 'simply, not handing the cards over? . • Mr. Forshaw : ,We say that the workmen have asked for the,carde.and defendant has refused to hand the&amp;over"; but in_fairnese to the de- fendant they must say that -yesterday.: he-handed. the books over to, the Labour Exchange, and McHugh's card was "stamped iip.to date. M. Johnstone said he had not refused to return any of ther cards. Lomax had left with- out notice, and he (defendant) did not know until the next morning that he had left. He did not ask for his card, and it was lost. He had admitted that fact to the Labour Exchange •Inspector. When McHugh applied on a Sunday morning, the card was not fully stamped.- ~He went to ..the _house on the follow- ing Sunday and used such impertinence to his (defendant's) wife that she had to order him off the premises. He had not seen McHugh since, and the Labour Exchange had not applied to him for the cards. Mr. Forshaw said, the Board of Trade wanted an 'example which would act as a warning to employers. Defendant: At the most it amounts to care- lessness.</p>
        <p>Defendant was fined ss. and costs in each case.</p>
        <p>CONTRIBUTIONS NOT PAID.</p>
        <p>'Defendant was further charged with having on May 3, 10 and 17, failed to pay the contribu- tions of McHugh.; on April- 14th having- failed to pay the contribution of James He 15133,7, and on April 26th that or Michael Connelly. Defendant pleaded guilty in the cases of Helsby and McHugh and not guilty in the case of Connelly. Mr. Forshaw first dealt with the cases of McHugh and Helsby. McHugh, he said, was a foreman bricklayer, and was employed by the defendant from October, 1912, until May of this year. When McHugh first went for his card to Mr. Johnstone, it was not fully stamped. and the defendant promised to stamp it and return it. Not until the previous day, when proceedings had • been instituted, did the de- fendant return the card to the Labour Ex- change. Helsby was employed from July 15th, 1912, until April Bth this year. Instead of there being 39 stamps on his card there were only 26. Defendant said his defence in the case of McHugh was just the same as in the previous case. Helsby left him without notice. He (de- fendant) gave Helsby the cards, and he thought* they were fully stamped. Helsby made no comment at the time, and he had not seen him since. , Defendant's first intimation of the alleged offence was a letter he received from the authorities.</p>
        <p>Mr. Forshaw explained that defendant was liable to pay a sum three times the amount of that which he had neglected to pay. Defendant was fined ss. in each of the four cases; ordered to pay double the amount of the stamps be had not provided in Helsby's ease, and .-t5 ss. towards the costs of the prosecution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WAS HE EMPLOYED?</head>
        <p>WAS HE EMPLOYED?</p>
        <p>Michael Connelly. 8, Dolman's-lane, said he was employed by defendant from Aril 22 to May 2nd.</p>
        <p>, . Defendant said he could not find any record in his wages book of Connelly having been em-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. J. _llepherd (in the chair), T.</p>
        <p>Locker and C. Jackson Holmes.</p>
        <p>ARREARS OF MAINTENANCE.—Edward Savage, of Dewsbury, was summoned for arrears of main- tenance in respect of his wife, Selina Savage, Winwick-road, Warrington.—Mr. A. Browne mentioned that the case .was adjourned on the understanding that defendant (who did not appear) should pay 15s. per week until all the arrears had been cleared off. In default the lump sum had to become due. They had had no 'instalments that morning,., and he asked for commitment. He also asked that the wife, should be allowed something towards the advo- cate's fee.—The Chairman: He should have been paid by this morning? You have not given him much time.—Mr. Browne: The con- ditions were that he should have paid by this morning.—The magistrates ' ordered that the woman should be allowed £1 for the advocate's fee, and that Savage should pay the money some time that day, or go to prison for one month. DRUNK IN CHARGE.—Francis Edward Birchall, 54, Catherine-street, was ordered to pay ss. for having been drunk whilst in charge of a pony and trap; WITH WHAT -INTENT ?—John Horan, . hailing from. Congleton, was remanded until Thursday, on a. charge of loitering in Butteimarket-street, for an -unlawful purpose.—The Chief Constable said that prisoner asked P.C..Gibbs the..way to Manchester. As the officer was directing him, another man came up and said he had told the prisoner the way about half-an-hour before. Prisoner, who was standing in front of Tyrer's shop, said his intention was to break in some- where, but, up to the time of the officer coming to him, he had not found a suitable place.— Prisoner said he did not remember what he was doing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. A. J. Jolley (in the chair), Messrs. W. Roberts, J. Rymer Young, and Dr. Naden. " DRUNKEN, WORTHLESS CHARACTER." - John Horan, hailing from Congleton, was charged on remand with loitering in Butterniarket-street for an unlawful purpose on August sth.—The Chief Constable repeated his statement of Tues- day, and added that he had been in communica- tion with the Congleton police, who described prisoner as "a drunken, worthless character." They had, however, never suspected him of felony. There were 24 previous convictions for drunkenness, &amp;c.—Horan was sent to prison for 14 days. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Alderman James Evans, Messrs. G. Pilling, S. Wright and A. J. Jolley. A MEAN THEFT.—George Postlethwaite, of 8, Mwris-court, got too much drink, called at his mother's house, and stole a jacket and vest, the property of his youngest brother. These he pawned for 55., went to Widnes with the money and was found in a pretended fit in one of the streets of Widnes. Sent home- by the Widnes police, he was arrested at Warrington and charged with theft. He admitted the offence, and, partly because of the mean nature of the theft, and a very black record, Postlethwaite was now given two months in prison with hard labour. _ . ,</p>
        <p>_ DESERTED WIVES.—TWO Warrington wives asked the magistrates to give them separation from their spouses. Mary Clayton Lunny said she was married to Edward Lunny, theatre musician, twelve years ago; and there, were three children. He left her 12 weeks ago, and was 'now at a TerritOrial camp in Monmouth-- shire. ' The husband wrote that the family trouble was due to hira.and his drinking habits, he was very sorry for what had happened, and offered to pay 10s, per week.. The Court fixed th_e amount at 'Ms., and gave separationfar- ried eighteen months ago, Ada Hardman sought separation from James Hardman. They had lived in Greenall-street and Lovely-lane, but the wife now alleged that Hardman would not find her a home, and added that he had been in five different jobs in spine months. . The Bench thought it was a I pity to separate so young a couple, and adjourned the case for four weeks so that the husband might provide a home, and ordered him to pay ss. per meek during that time. "To THE BEAST MARKET."-.A. warrant was issued against Eliza Prince, who was summoned for fraudulently removing goods. and James Prince for assisting in the removal. The goods were furniture taken from 51, Wash-lane, Latchford.—Mr. Lowe, estate agent, said the Princes owed 10 guineas for rent, but had re- moved the goods from the house at two o'clock in the morning to evade distraint.—A carter, who took the furniture, said he was told to ^" take it _to the beast market at. Wrextiarn."-: Mr. Lowe added that they had traced the parties to 'Wrexham by the aid of the Welsh police. As the parties did not appear, the magistrates thought the best course was to issue a warrant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before his Hon. Dudley Stuart Smith, K.C. (vice-chancellor of County Palatine), Messrs.</p>
        <p>S. R. Armitage, F. A. FrOat and J. 'ball.</p>
        <p>BICYCLE ' ON rooTrAnr.--David Jones, 114, Peckers Hill-road, Sutton,' was fined 2s. 6d. for having ridden his bicycle on the footpath at Burtonwood.</p>
        <p>HUSBAND AND WIFE.—The adjourned case, in which Rosina Richardson, of Sankey, summoned her husband, Charles William Richardson, 38, Minto-road, Hillsboro'' Sheffield, for desertion, was heard.—Mr. P. H. Swallow appeared for the complainant, and Mr. J. W. Knowles defended.. Swallow said the,case..h-ad. been adlourned on the suggestion of.the Pollee Court MissiOnOi; to 'gee if it-Ivere possible to get the parties to come to -soe amiahle arrangement.- in the. meantime, at the suggestion of the husband. the -Woman- had returned to him. She stayed a couple of nights, but he told her to go, and practically turned her out. She had come back to Warrington. and it seemed most unlikely that they would come to any arrangement.— Mr. Knowles said that as the parties had co- habited since the adjournment, the case must end. The defendant was still willing to find her a home.—Complainant said defendant had told. her that ,he .had no affection for. her.—The case' was dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>KILLED BY A CRICKET BALL.</head>
        <p>KILLED BY A CRICKET BALL.</p>
        <p>"A DOCTOR'S CONSULTING-ROOM HIS CASTLE."</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at the London Hospital on Wednesday on Alfred Arthur Gilbert, 23, of Grove Villa, Grove-road, South Woodford. Whilst playing cricket at Higham's Park, Essex, on Thursday, Gilbert was struck on the head by the ball. After being treated at the local hoe- 'Alai:he was conveyed to the London Hospital, where, in spite. of an- operation, he died from hemorrhage of the' brain on Satarilay. Friends .of Gilbert who were playing at the same time in their evidence, comPtainod that when they took him to Dr. 11-2(70411, of V; cod- ford, after the accident, that gentleman Aid not attend to him and sent them away. Dr.. McCosh explained that blbert's friends opened the door of his consulting-i&amp;Jin hoist he was performing an operation. Be expiwtu- lated with them upon their ...on•luot. .r.as not able to attend Gilbert, and told them to leave.</p>
        <p>A juryman said he thought the doctor should have seen the man. The Coroner observed that a doctor's consult- ing-room was his castle, just the same as the juryman's house, and Gilbert's friends acted most unjustifiably. Tha jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WIDOW'S GRIEF AT 'PIT VICTIMS' FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>WIDOW'S GRIEF AT 'PIT VICTIMS' FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Scenes of a very affecting nature were asso- ciated with the funerals of 21 of the victims of the Cadder Colliery disaster at Glasgow on Wed- nesday. When seven of the coffins had been carried into the Cadder Parish Church Mission Hall. the mourners were startled by the distkessed appearance of Mrs: Robert Ramsay, a young widow, whose husband was one Of the victims. She .hovered over the coffins in a dazed condi- tion, and on recognising that containing .her husband's body, flung herself on it in wild despair, exclaiming, '` Oh, Bob," and weeping piteously. As the coffins were being removed to the hearse, Mrs. Ramsay again broke down, and her distress was-such as to cause even ninny men present to weep silently.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Peculiar Case Heard in the Warrington County Court.</head>
        <p>Peculiar Case Heard in the Warrington County Court.</p>
        <p>WHAT IS A WEAVER?</p>
        <p>A. girl of sixteen was the central figure in a peculiar claim under the Workmen's Compensa- tion Act, heard by his Honour, Judge Stanger, K.C., in the Warrington County Court, on Thursday. She was Alice Ogden, of 5, Eldon- street, Warrington, and she claimed compensa- tion from Messrs. Armitage and Rigby, Cock- hedge Mills, for the loss of part of a finger through an accident which occurred while she was weaving in the firm's mill at Cockhedge. Mr. C. H. Unsworth, who appeared for the girl, said applicant was engaged as a two-loom weaver when the accident occurred on February 19th last. The girl went to the mill at the age of thirteen, and, after ten weeks, she was selected as a helper by a weaver named Mery Dean, and thus got the rank of weaver's helper. That position she filled until January last, when, in consequence of a two-loom weaver, named Annie Harrison, being away sick, the applicant was igiven the looms of Harrison. She worked these looms from the beginning of January until the accident. During that time she earned 13s. to 14s. a week. She had been paid compensation on her average -earnings as a weaver's helper, and for seven weeks as a two-loom weaver, which compensation worked out at 6s. sd. per week. There was no dispute about that.</p>
        <p>Where the dispute lay was that applicant said she had gone into the grade of a two-loom weaver, and as not now a weaver's helper. Her mother refused to allow her to become a helper. The other point was the question of permanent incapacity. The accident necessi- tated the amputation of the first jchint of the forefinger on the right hand. - The evidence was that the removal of the finger joint: incapaci- tated her as a weaver. . She could not turn out the same amount of work. COULD NOT HAVE LOOMS. .</p>
        <p>. When she was discharged from the Warring- ton Infirmary in May, she went to the mill and asked what the firm were going to do about her case. An overlooker, named Mr. Riley, told her she could not have her two looms back again, and would have to go back as a helper. She refused to do this. Mr. Hibbert, the manager of the weaving department, told her the same thing, but the girl refused to become a helper again. Alice Ogden gave evidence bearing out her solicitor's statement. As a helper she started at 3s. 2d. a week, and got a rise of, 3d. every month until the maximum of ss. 9d. was reached. While a helper she considered herself in the service of Mary Dean, although the over- looker had power to dismiss. The part of the finger amputated was used much in the work of weaving:- especially in piecing,: pushing the' shuttle and unweaving "floats." THE WEFT HOLE."_ _ _ _</p>
        <p>When she asked for the looms, added Ogden, Mr. Riley said lie was not going to take another girl off to put her on. She asked Mr. Riley to had her a job in the "weft hole," and he replied it was " a lazy job." She would have earned from 6s. to 9s. per week in the "weft hole." Mr. Riley said that if she went there she would never get back to the looms. Mr. Hibbert also told her she must go back to the position of helper. "I replied to that," said witness, " I am not going back to helping,' and said if I did not get my two looms back I would go to law with them." _ _</p>
        <p>_tier finger was now very tender, and she could not now earn the same wages as she did before the accident. She could not do the same amount of work.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined by Mr. Burgis, barrister, for the respondents, Ogden said that when the accident occurred she was doing "sick work." When "rorking sick" on looms it did not mean that the one who worked for the sick person did not establish a claim to the looms, and gave them up on the sick persoi's return. She ad- mitted that if she had got the two looms she would have gone back at once. Several, other. witnesses said that the girl could' not do the same work as formerly, owing to the lost finger tip. Eliza Ryder, who said she had met with a similar accident to her finger. contended that the girl could not work as well after the accident.</p>
        <p>"Did you work in the mill after your- injury?" asked Mr. Burgia. , • "I had to work," retorted witness. "There was no- compensation in those days:" (Laugh: ter.) EVIDENCE FOR THE FIRM.</p>
        <p>Dr. C7D.-LT;',l3;nfoi:--iesidWt ;ti-i:ieo-n at the Warrington Infirmary, was the first witness for the employers. He said that he considered Ogden quite able to resume work. The wounded finger was perfectly healthy, and it had a free movement. There was no pain on pressure being applied. He tested the girl in tying knots, and she did it with the right hand with re- markable rapidity. She used the injured finger to_write her name, as well as to tie the knots..</p>
        <p>Mr. Unsworth : Have you any experience of weaving? Dr. Law: I have no experience of the work done by weavers except what I have been told. Mr. C. H. Hibbert,.weaving manager at Cock- hedge Mills, said that when the accident occurred Ogden was only sick weaving. She waa acting temporarily for a weaver away sick, and when the weaver came back she would have returned to the duties- of a wearer's helper. As soon as a permanent vacancy occurred he would have been prepared, if Ogden had re- turned to her work as a helper, when she was asked, to give the girl two looms. During Ogden's absence another girl had been given loonig, and Ogden seemed to resent' it. His Honour: When Ogden was away sick, why was not some other person put temporarily on the looms intended for her?</p>
        <p>Mr. Hibbert : Another girl had taken her place, but she would not have had long to wait for looms, if she had come back. When I told her that she should go back to the helping she asked for work in the "weft hole," but I said .that would not be a good job for her. If she had cared she could have gone back to "sick weaving.," and would have got a permanent position in a short time. I do not think she is incapacitated by the injured finger. Sam Riley. overlooker, thought Ogden was now quite able to do hr work. Since May there had been -a number of •vacant looms, and if Ogden had been at work she would have been put on some of the vacant looms. ' A MAIMED- WEAVER:-</p>
        <p>- . .Mr.:BUrgis said that' there was one weaver in .Lancasbire who had `lost a right hand. yet was able to work. He was calling a witness to. prove that the loss of a-finger did:not:militate against a weaver following, her- occupation." This witness was Sarah Ann 'Roberts, a weaver from Tyldesley. She said that she had lost the forefinger of her right hand. but she was still able to work in the mill, and she had four looms. She was, despite the loss of her finger, able to make a good wage. Mr. Unsworth : Can you get through as much work as you did_l2efore your accident?</p>
        <p>The Witness:: Yes, sir, quite as much. , Mr. Burgis contended that Ogden,- although she had worked temporarily two looms, had not acquired the grade of weaver. Still, after the accident, she was not incapacitated from doing weaver's work.</p>
        <p>Mr. Unsworth : If the girl' had not met with the accident? she would have acquired the status of a twO-loom weaver before now. His Honour said that the evidence showed that Ogden, after she got better, was capable of doing the work of helper and also to do "sick weaving." Could he say she was capable of earning 6s. 5d per week? Mr. Burgis said she could have started almost at once on "sick weaving" at 13s. or 14s. a week. . TITE GIRL LOSES THE .CASE.</p>
        <p>. . HiS Honour said it wai a very peculiar case. He could . not ,help thinking that but for the circumstance that Ann Harrison did not go back to work, and that a vacancy for a two- loom weaver occurred while Ogden was in the hospital, and that -another girl got the posi- tion, Ogden would have gone back to her work as a helper. Finding, however, that there had been a vacancy, and that somebody else. whom Ogden thought was not rightly entitled to it. had got the position, she had a feeling of dis- satisfaction, and her mother was also much dissatisfied. And but for the accident applicant might not have lost the, opportunity of promo- tion. That was a circumstance, however, he could not take into account in a hearing under the Workmen's Compensation Act. He had to consider the wage the applicant was earning at the time of the accident, and then see if she was able to earn that wage when compensation ceased, and if not earning it to decide what compensation she should have. It was, he thought, unfortunate that the mother gave such advice to her daughter, and he thought if the (laughter had gone back to work the employers would have made some reasonable allowance and given her a chance to become a weaver. He hoped that after the case she would; be taken back, and that she would be willing to go back. It was his opinion that she had not yet reached the grade of a permanent two-loom weaver. .</p>
        <p>_ He had to find •substantially in favour of the employers, but considering the fact that the girl had not been at work, and her future was doubtful, he thought there should be declara- tion of liability.</p>
        <p>Mr. Burgis asked for costs against the appli- cant. The Judge made a formal order for costs. on the undr‘rstanding that' the order would not be enforced.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS EXSERVED.T</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>HOUSEWIFE OF TO-MORROW.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSEWIFE OF TO-MORROW.</p>
        <p>It is usually accepted that women are very susceptible to compliments, while it is not uncommonly assumed that they can be stimu- lated to continued action in any desired dir- ection by the application of delicately judici- ous flattery. If these conceptions be founded on fact there should surely and rapidly be a marked, recrudescence among women of a conscientious interest 'in the• daily round of domestic duties; for reports of Commissions and Interdepartmental Committees con- cerned with the physical condition and de- velopment of the population emphasise the fact that the future prosperity of the nation depends upon the right fulfilment by women of their maternal and domestic functions; and that suggestions for remedial measures are futile unless the co-operation of mothers be secured. Surely the women of Britain could receive no more emphatic- and compli- mentary assurance of the enormous power they wield; no stronger or more flattering testimony could be furnished them of their control over national destinies.</p>
        <p>It is perhaps little surprising that this dignified conception of their national value has not yet dawned, on the limited horizon of the mothers of the masses; or has so far failed to pierce the thick atmosphere of un- intelligent drudgery in which so many carry on their constant struggle for subsistence.</p>
        <p>DOMESTIC DUTIES. But it seems reasonable to anticipate a less circumscribed sense of duty and broader views of their social responsibilities from the more fortunately situated home makers of the upper and middle classes, possessed of a wider outlook on life, and relieved from the stress of daily toil by at least a modest com- petence. To a moderate -,extent -these anticipa- tions are realised, though the percentage of parents is but small who actually believe that the right rearing of their families un- der healthy home conditions is an art to be acquired, based upon carefultx organised ex- perience, not an innate irilitinct, developed on marriage and sufficiently safeguarded by rule of thumb traditions. How can an intel- l'lgeint interest in domestic duties be re- awakened in twentieth century Women? The solution of this problem depends, as do all so- cial questions, upon the just combination of many complex factors, of which one is the education of rung people in a right concep- tion of the dignity and function of the home io the maintenance of national efficiency. To attain this end many methods can be adopt- ed. In the United States, for instance, the movernent to readjust the existing dishar- monies -.between home claims and modern life was originated some years since by college. trained women, who recognised that the lo- gical result of a general disregard of matern- al duties and of home hygiene would be mor- al as well as physi-4,1' degenerition. It is full ten years since the President of the National. Household Economic Associa- tion, a medical woman, appealed for the co- operation 'of every, woman in America to im- prove the present average conditions of home life. " Whether we live or die, and how we live or die, is largely determined by our household conditions. We • cannot afford :to hifire this cradle of life, the home, in an in- firior 'or defective' condition, else is the life that -comes out of it, malformed- and'</p>
        <p>It is fifteen years since another active worker in this field, who holds a position of great importance in the scientific and 'educa- tional world, accused women publicly of be- ing "the stumbling blocks in the way of so- cial, industrial and ethical progress;" she pointed out that reform must begin in the daily life of the household; and she herself promptly put her precepts to the test of prac- tice. The pioneers of the movement are im- bued with the conviction that with few ex- ceptions social progress percolates from above downwards. If the masses are to be in- fluenced for good, it behoves the most intelli- gent, highly cultured women to direct their trained minds to the study of home prob- lems; to test the outcome of their conclu- sions by practice, and by force of example as well as precept to raise the standard of fami- ly life .aguong_ethe more ignorant. The best lmerican women still profess 'this faith, af- ter many years of persevering work, and are 3ncourage4 by the growth of the seed they have sown.</p>
        <p>A MODEL. KIT,CEJ.D.I. It 1F be of interest to offer an illustration of the merino taken by one of their number to supply her household with wholesome, varied food; to minimise labour in its preparation, and to surround herself with conditions as favourable to these ends as would obtain were she engaged .in the pursuit of science in a laboratory, or of art in a studio. The neces- sary reorganisation cost money, but she de- clares it to be the most paying investment of her life. This lady is the wife of a leading solicitor in a prosperous city in the State of Michigan. Her house is characterised by the simplicity of its furniture and fittings; cleanliness, convenience and moderate com- fort are throughout the distingPuishing fea- tures; but it is in the kitchen that the sharp contrast- exists between this. and a similar house in Britain. Lined as it. is with glazed cupboards, .the • floor- space - measures about ten feet square. The c/mtre is occupied by a cooking -table or bench of hard wood, such as. .is found .in all the .American schools and Col- leges where domestic science is studied. It is about four feet long by two and a half broad; one half is covered wnith sheet zinc; over this portion, at the back, runs an iron grid, supported about six inches above two or three gas rings for cooking purposes. Slid immediately under the table are two boards, for pastry and meat respectively; below are taco drawers, of which the deeper' one is pro- vided- with one or two sliding trays to • ac- commodate the small ware in constant use. Beneath the drawers are sliding metal bins containing flour and potatoes, while a cup- board is also provided for china bowls and other articles. At one end is a glazed earth- enware sink supplied with hot and cold water; at the other a semaphore displays its arms, upon which are hung the necessary cloths; beneath are placed the larger uten- sils. The glass doors of the spae.ous cup- boards reveal stores of pots and pans, crock- ery and napery, groceries and other needful accessories.</p>
        <p>From • one • corner of the kitchen the ice cave- opens with its separate compartments. for dairy produce -and fruit, fish or meat;- the supply of ice being introduced from without. In another corner is placed the large Aladdin oven, heated by oil or gas; where-soups, and stews can be slowly pre- pared, dishes kept. hot, or, when desired, cakes and biscuits baked. Seated on a high stool at this cook;ng. table, the house mistress conducts her' cooking- processes skilfully, ex- peditiously and accurately. Time. strength and labour are saved; only when she expects eliv‘al guests does she find outside assistance indispensable. Under ordinary circumstances she has leisure, in reason, for social life, for intellectual interests, for family *claims; largely because she 'has concentrated her trained intelligence, her scientific studies, her skilled observation, upon these and other improved methods for the execution of dally duties. The kitchen in a British middle class home may be just as spotless, just as airy, just as neat. But the intelligent lali- our saving devices are absent, and the me. thods are governel by traditions which ot, tamed when the industrial facilities and scientific knowledge of to-day were non-exis- tent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>SCHOOL AND COLLEGE TRAINING.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL AND COLLEGE TRAINING.</p>
        <p>Now this old ordei must change and give place tr. new; family Life nmt be adjusted</p>
