        <p>as are industrial processes to modern condi- tions; housewives as -well as mechanics must show themselves judiciously progressive_ But the substitution of improved methods can only be effected by degrees and must be based on good training. The mere raising of ststandardsahnadvepraneact°l ideals will not suffice, essential as it is; and phlefsehedagagoinod work. Ame r.ealnf are to be raised among the masses, the dig- nity and worth of the object must be testi- fied to by the classes. For them, therefore, oppor*unities must be afforded to aeanire the requisite imoe ieu4e. e4ct Anti siva . a firm conviction of the national and social importance of the right conduct of home life in all social grades, several four year courses in Household Economies have been instituted at different colleges and universities, to which cordial support has been given by the professors, and of which the number of stu- dents steadily increases. claims it h a n That the irainingasosofcihaotemde w in- tellectual recognition pos- sesses a high educational, as well as national and economic, value is another fact increas- ingly turned to account by educationaliists so keen and enterprising, as our Linariesti cousins. Domestic Science is no Cinderella .in the best High Schools of the United' States. The objects of giving this instruction to well to do girls are threefold: first, purely educational; that is to train them in scienti- fic - methods and in an appreciation of econo- mic values; second, domestic; to arouse in- terest in and to add dignity to the familiar and artistic art;sityiec the training; ; g processes and env.ronment of home life, by using these as the field for the application f scientific par nandthirdly,oo its Vtmiti mate posit ion asta nicoilpolg.eiso nl; na 'importance among social institutions. The results are without exception satisfactory. In Britain the teaching of domestic F•ei- ence has pursued a different course. For many years it has found a place in the time- table of elementary schools to a greater ex- tent' than was attempted in the United States till recently; but it has received scant atten- tion in the scheme of secondary education, while only recently has it been included in a Cry.:ege, curriculum. That all efforts up to the present have failed to produce adequate results is forcibly realised. rndifferenee, want of interest, distaste for domestic work, and what is far more serious, unintelligent neg- lect of children, the nation's most valuable asseis, are still rife among our people. But at last it is recognised that some change of method in schools, some quickeni,g. of inter- est among all classes, is imperative, and the i result is seen. in the formulation of suitable Courses of study for those who by heredity. education and positon are better fitted to perceive the sociological and economic bear- lugs of this matter, and who shall be thus gradually and intelligently influenced to i the efficiency standardi of progress in all that pre- 'notesn the home life of Great Britain.</p>
      </div>
