        <head>"Not a Creed, but a Life."</head>
        <p>"Not a Creed, but a Life."</p>
        <p>At the Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sun- day morning, the pulpit was occupied by Mr. Peter Woods, who spoke from the words: 'AIL things that I have heard of my Father I have made known unto you. The words,he said. revealed the closeness of the relationship which existed between Christ and His disciples, and which might exist between Christ and us. 'llo His friends, Christ -reveals, not a creed, but a life. The real aim of the Saviour's life was to teach men to live. A new conception of God - Our Father." None of Christ's pre-- was given. We were taught when we prayed to dec such Inti- mate relatienship., -.This intinwy mlist-nol. be.; fed it. Love,7muF.;t hold first say raised us to :the height of t4e„ lightly, but in proportion to the depth., of plalavceet;_haantdrrottme Ptrue family life here" en-earth, . and the love of the home, must be a symbol of the relation between liod and Us. - .</p>
