      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>," The Joy of Religion."</head>
        <p>," The Joy of Religion."</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Owen presided at the meeting of Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on Sunday. An address on "The joy of religion -" wa.s giv-n the- Rev. T. T. James. He said that SQIIIP peoples had an idea that religion took away the brignt- ness of life, but there was no ground for such an assumption. Religion might depriya us of some things that did not really count. but 3t ,gave us things of inestimable value. By ihe beauty of the world God wanted to glad lea us. The gospel was the good news to men and re- vealed His v ill to us. The average Christian lacked power upon his fellows because his daily - life was not a manifestation of Christ. Atl God's resources were on the side of man. Tie could make our littleness great and our dulness bright. Christ was always doing good to those who were morally down—as instanced in the case of the woman of Samaria. It ‘‘n,3 ber liie and not her theology that Christ cared for. Christ's life was not to be entirely spirituuliseti, at His great care was for the body as well as for the soul. God's great love was for the whole, world and for the whole man-T-body, soul and sprit C. Cotton and Mr. W. Deswiek took Part in theJneeting.. Mr. W. H. Payton was at the organ. Greetings were conveyed to the Sheffield Wycliffe Brotherhood through a member who was present at the meeting.</p>
