      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>NEW INSTRUMENT WHEREBY EVERYONE CAN TREAT THEMSELVES AT HOME IS BEING DEMONSTRATED in WARRINGTON for a FEW DAYS ONLY.</head>
        <p>A NEW INSTRUMENT WHEREBY EVERYONE CAN TREAT THEMSELVES AT HOME IS BEING DEMON- STRATED in WARRINGTON for a FEW DAYS ONLY.</p>
        <p>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS. Notices of births, marriages, and deaths, in memoriam notices, and acknowledgments of sympathy are inserted in the " Examiner " at the following rates :—Not exceeding 20 words, is. 6d. each; every additional seven words, 6d,</p>
        <p>14 DAYS HIRE AND TRIAL ARRANGED.</p>
        <p>FREE TESTS. Hours: 9-30 to 7-30, Saturday 4-30. 4, MUSEUM ST.,WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE ATTACK</p>
        <p>INSTITUTIONS.</p>
        <p>ENGLISH TARIFF " REFORMERS " AND THE REJECTED DUTCH TARIFF. Two years ago we were told by the Tariff "'Reformers" that Holland was about_ to adopt Protection, and that England would then be the only civilised country in the world which pursued -a Free Trade policy, They have, how- ever, been singularly silent about the recent Dutch" General Election, at which tbe people, baying devoted two or _three years to -the-utre- ful _consideration of the 'question, inflicted a crushing defeat on the Government and its fiscal proposals, thereby reaffirming once more their aversion to anything in the nature of a Protec- tive tariff. One of the Protectionist contributors to a recent newspaper controversy on this matter suggested that the proposals put forward q the late Dutch Government were to all intents and purposes the same as those proposed by the Tariff "Reformers" in this country. If this be true, then an examination of the Dutch pro- posals which have just been defeated should' throw a little much-needed light upon the policy of the Protectionist party in England. An in- vestigation of the proposed Dutch schedules shows that, in the first place, raw materials were to come in free. "Semi-manufactured" goods—e.g., metal castings, planed wood, atone stabs, and tanned hidos=were to be taxed from three to six per cent., ad valorem. On "mainly manufactured " goods e.g., doors, window- frames, mantel-pieces, motor car chassis; the duty duty was to be ten per. cent.; while "fully manufactured" goods, ready for immediate and Independent nse, were to be subject to a duty of twelve per cent. But the significant point._ about the rejected Dutch proposals, if they are to be taken as a standard of comparison with the proposqla of our own Tariff "Reformers," lies in their treat- ment of food-stuffs. It was proposed to 're- impose. the ,duty of 4d. per cwt. on flour from cereals, which was in force- up to 1877, the framers of the .tariff having decided 'to treat flour from cereals as a seini-manufadured article. A duty of B.s: rild. per cwt. was to be placed on butter and margarine;, while the .duty on cheese was to be raised from 4s. 21d. to £1 is. 2d. per cwt. . Should any such proposals for the taxing of staple articles of food be included in the pro- gramme. of the Protectionist party here, it is safe to predict that they would meet with the same fate as in Holland. On August 2nd,l9M The Times" published a speCial article on the Dutch proposals, and to those who are accus- tomed, to the usual arguments put forward in favour of Tariff Reform here, the last para- graph of the article in question will suggest some interesting reflections. The article stated that a stream of protests and petitions against the proposed tariff 'Was being' poured upon the States-General, and continued in the forefront is placed the objection that the cost of living, already high in Hol- land, must necessarily be increased, since the cost of production, in consequence of the duty on semi-manufactured articles, will rise. , This possibility is not denied by the supporters of the project. But they reply that, if there is to be- social legislation, there must also be money to carry it out, and, at the same time, the benefit to home industries is insisted upon. These, how- ever, the opponents affirm, are well able to take care of themselves, as. the progress , —which has been made of :recent years proyes.. Further, it is pointed' out that' this argument is a faflacious one, if revenue -is the real object of the revision. Either the Exchequer will benefit, in which .case Dutch manufacturers will be where they are at present, or home'industries will pro- fit, in which case the required revenues will not be forthcoming. The imposition of higher duties will, it is feared, inevitably lead to the erection in other countries of Customs barriers against Dutch exports, while in some cases injury to the Inter- national position of the Netherlands is apprehended. Thus the tariff which we are told is to enable us to breakdown the tariffs of other countries, is believed in liollandand apparently "The Times " feels no disinc)inatio.n to conciir in the belief—to be. likely to produce an absolutely opposite effect. ' „</p>
        <p>UPON REPIIE,SENTAiIVE</p>
        <p>It has been amusing to observe the indigna- tion which the Opposition • Press has professed because Mr. Lloyd George, last week, in his Carnarvon speech, ventured to make a lively retort to the habitual virulence of Tory attacks. .They pretend to find in Mr. Lloyd George's com- plaint of a Tory attack upon democracy nothing but the hysterical fiction of an exasperated politician: The plain man merely recapitulates the salient facts of the last few years' political history, in order to assure Himself whether the Tory Press or N Ir. Lloyd George is right. In January, 1910, the country gave a decisive inajority to the Liberal Government at a General Election provoked by the claim of the Lords that they, and not the Commons, should have the last word in legislation. In December, 1910. the Government won . another decisive victory, after ,introducing a Bill to limit the veto of the Lords. Yet practically all . the im- portant measures of the present Government are held up in 1913. as in 1908; the Opposition 'threaten that, when they get into office, they Will repeal the Parliament Act. Democracy and representative institutions will be reduced to a shadow of it name • in this coutitry if such a state of things continues. And the only remedy appears to be, as Mr. Lloyd George says, that we should have a Second Chamber, in which all parties. all sections, and all creeds can obtain equal treatment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
