      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>ITURDAY, AUGUST 9,1913</p>
        <p>THE AVIATION TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>The conquest of the air, inevitable though it would appear to be, is exacting a terrible .toil Of human. life in its accomplishment. .. Ever since man seriously esSayed to " tly," the world has repeatedly been ahocked-bi disasters, which have cut short careers of brilliant promise and deprived :as of fine specimens Of manhood,- wliotn,'We_could•-ill-afford to lose. . It is, how- ever, no exaggeration to say that the tragic end of Colonel „Samuel 'Franklin Cody, while flying his late,st' biplatle at Aldershot 'on Thursday,. created a more profound sense of personal los.s in the minds of the British people than any of the list of aviation fatalities which have pte- ceded it... Colonel Cody was not an Englishman by birth, but we have long since learned. to regard him as one of our own. He had ,a true affection for the country of his adoption, in which all his aeronautical experiments had been-made, and he had, in fact, become a naturalised • British subject. The outstanding . feature of his career was his dogged determina- tion and calm intrepidity, in face of discour- agements to less courageous natures, would have been overwhelming , lie was a conspicuous representative of ,the quietly per- sistent and self-confident typc of character for which the English-speaking . 'races .4aye ./an abiding admiration: Rebuffs Only seemed to fire him With redoubled enerlk,'and no,amount a scorn or ridicule would divert him from -his purpose. • .</p>
        <p>For along time People declined toitake Cody seriously' as an ,aviator, :but he lived to com- mand ,the recognition which .was his. due, and became, not only the .leading airman in Eng- land, but •one of the, most successful construe- tors.- He was the first man in this country to fly in. a British-built aeroplane, as he was the; first to share with Colonel Capper the distinc- tion of navigating a British dirigible balloon from Farnborough round St. Paul's, and thence in the Crystal Palace, a leat largely due to hint as being responsible for 'the installation of the „power plant and all the mechanical appliances. AiS great. triumph; however,. was the oyerconi-,: .19.g.0f.1hd-scepticism ot the military authorities. Last S-ear,.with-an aeroplane virtually the same, sas that discarded by the War Office less than four years previously, he won the first prize in the Army air tests on Salisbury Plain—£s,ooo —had hLs machine bought .by the Government for £2,000, and obtained, in the official report, commendation which was denied to the picked work of the great- French constructors— Bterlot and Farman, to mention' two. He..had made a complete study of the science of flight., and his outstanding achievement w,S a varia- tion in flying speed of as. mticp,asjg miles an hour between his highest, and his lowest speed —a feature of incalculable value in a-, military machine. As Colonel Seely, Secretary of State for War, put it, in a telegram of sympathy to Mrs. Cody: "The science of aeronautics in this country owes much to' his mechanical genius - and courageous perseverance, and the War Otijee has special reason- to mourn the loss Of valuable services, both in -regard to his expert knowledge' of man-lifting kites, which he placed at their disposal, and by reason of his contributions to military aeronautics."</p>
        <p>The cause 1!)f the disaster is apparently inexplicable. While at a height of some 150 feet from the ground the machine suddenly wavered and crumpled up, sending Cody and his passenger, Mr. W. 11. B. Evans, the well- known Oxford "Blue," to immediate death. One suggestion is that a weakness in the j bamboo framework was responsible for the j collapse, and there is another theory that an j explosion occurred. As is always the case j when a tragedy of this kind happens, there will doubtless be many to question whether our advancement, in the science of aviation is worth the heavy price we are called upon to pay. So long as human nature retains Sits finest, qualities, the desire to conquer uncertain elements, and to achieve worthy renown, will impel men to carry their lives in their hands, .and face the gravest odds. Moreover, aviation has introduced an entirely newt calculation into the possibilities of warfare, and England owes a debt to the brave men who are willing to expose themselves to the perils of the air,' in -order to keep their country abreast of, the times- in this- increasingly important respect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BURIAL GROUND RATES.</head>
        <p>BURIAL GROUND RATES.</p>
        <p>• The monthly meeting of the Warrington Assessment Committee was held in the BoaAl- room at the Workhouse yesterday morning, Mr. W. Furness presiding. There were also prEbent : Messrs. A. J. Jolley, G. Mather, E. L. Bent, J. Billinge, J. A. Hughes, J. W. Knowles, A. Bet- tomley (clerk), R. I. Partridge (assistant clork), E. Gunson (valuer), W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer for Warrington), and E. Foster (assist- ant overseer for Penketh). The Rev. J. R. Jones appealed against the assessment of the burial ground in St. Mary's- road, Penketh, viz., gross I'2o, rateable .40. The Assistant Overseer explained that the assessment had not been fixed on any definite basis.</p>
        <p>The Vicar explained- that his fees for 1911 for land sold for graves amounted to £8 10s. ed., and for 1912 £l2 Os. 6d. There were other emoluments which were not assessable. The committee reduced the assessment to £lO gross and .29 10s. rateable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 9, 1913. THE PASSING WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 9, 1913. THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>THE PARR HALL ORGAN. The - Parr Hall organ has not been very tenderly treated by the critics of late years, but any individual who may have imagined that abuse has been needlessly hurled at the instru- ment has only to read the. report presented to the Parr Hall Sub-committee by Mr. W. H. Payton, whose professional advice was sought, to conclude that the complaints, though bitter, were hardly strong enough. It appears that the only part of the organ worth considering is the case, and Mr. Payton strongly advises that, with that slight exception, no particle of the existing instrument should be used in the con- struction of the new One. Music-lovers will heartily and feelingly endorse that, recommenda- i tion, and we hope that 4 for the credit of the town, no proposal for a patched-up instrument,</p>
        <p>if brought forward, will be seriously entertained. Mr. Payton is of the opinion that a suitable organ, built on the !nest modern principles, can be obtained for £1,500,, and the General Pur- poses Committee has decided to invite tenders, according to the specification which he has pre- pared.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>BATHS FOR '44.4v'ORKING MEN'S HOUSES.</head>
        <p>BATHS FOR '44.4v'ORKING MEN'S HOUSES.</p>
        <p>That every dwelling-houSe,should.be equipped'l with a-lath has long'leen.the opinion of ithoso who wish 'to 'give the working- classes a -fair chance essential o l practising that cleanliness which .is ss fto. good -health -the home. From ee the landlord's point of view, an obstacle to this , very necessary provision has been the amount charged by the Corporation for the supply of water used in the baths. The Street Improve- ment Committee has taken up the, question in earnest, and has passed a resolution requesting the Water Committee to consider the advis- ability' of discontinuing the charge for water for baths in all houses below the rateable value of £2O. At first sight the proposal appears ayer, has yet to be inquired into. reasonable, but the financial effect, as it con- ter Committee has already cvve,rennS ot e trh4atte Pt h e requested the 'Engineer to present a taken the recommendation into consideration, ia.enpdo s</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>DANGER TO HEALTH.</head>
        <p>A DANGER TO HEALTH.</p>
        <p>It ought to be fairly well known, in these enlightened days, that the practiceof spitting in the public streets may easily be the means of disseminating disease. Evidently, however, the fact is not fully appreciated in Warrington,</p>
        <p>, e, for tile attention of the Health Committee has by thstbeeli called to th danger to health caused oughtless indulgence in this habit. The Town Clerk has been instructed to prepare a bye-law dealing with the matter, but we hope common sense and due consideration for the well-being of others Arvin prevail to such an extent that it will be unnecessary to take steps to. enforce the -bye-law, onee it becomes Opera- tive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LADIEh AND THE BOWLING GREENS.</head>
        <p>LADIEh AND THE BOWLING GREENS.</p>
        <p>hllv.oef.'st6he tbhoo‘lr,oling gy r e app re- victoria Park foir.vtinhegir exclusive use on Tuesday , trundlers afternoons-that the Town Hall, Parks and Baths committee, wis?ly decided to extend the con- cession to the greens in St. Elphin's Park and Liverpool-road. The question then arose as to whether the ladies, emboldened by the improve- ment of their sporting prowe,ss by diligent prac- tice on their own particular day, should also be 'allowed to compete with mere man for places on the greens on the other days of the 'week. Having been excluded from pursuing their favourite .sport, under municipal auspices, on Tuesday afternoon, the men could not be con.- sidered ungracious or selfish in wishing to have full possession .of the greens for the reniainder of the Week. The head gardener having reported to the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee as to the number of ladies using the greens, instructions have now been given that on all days, with the exemption of that specially • set apart, for the fair sex, the • greens - shall be reserved for men only.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ADVERTISING WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>ADVERTISING WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Towns Committee, 'Which Was formed to devise Means of acquainting the universe with Warrington's many advantages as a manufac- turing' centre, has once more sprung into activity. Warrington, by reason of its geo- graphical situation, would be difficult to excel as a convenient locality for all kinds of works-- in fact, its well-known title of "the town of many industries" is a proof that its -favoured position is already fully recognised: There is, ,niways room for development, and •e.ommittee has taken -steps :for the .ptiiht7p:Prealas nOf prospectus, "showing'. the • cts of tbe,borough as an industrial centre." A. draft, wasJarought forward at a recentmeet- ing; and, instructions thereon having been -given to the Town Clerk, the matter- was deferred for further consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LATE DR. FOX.</head>
        <p>THE LATE DR. FOX.</p>
        <p>A strenuous career of public usefulness is closed by the death of Dr. Edward Austin Fox. From early manhood until his retirement, a few years' ago, he devoted himself, with .conscien- tious zeal, to the art of healing, and he took a special pride in placing his -skill at, the dis- posal of the Warrington Infirmary. That institution always had a commanding place in his affections, from those early days when bie became associated, as an honorary surgeon, with the old Dispensary, in Buttermarket-street. No one watched with greater interest and satis- faction the wonderful growth and progress of the institution, both in accommodation and equipment, and we vividly .recall the pleasure which it afforded Dr. Fox to participate 'in the opercing, proceedings in connection with .the recent ,extension. Heliad a .great love for the town ,of 'his adoption, and, as far as a busy practitioner's life 'would allow, took a worthy share of its -public work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>LOCAL BANK AMALGAMATION.</head>
        <p>THE LOCAL BANK AMALGAMATION.</p>
        <p>The forthcoming amalgamation of the War- rington and Buncorn Savings Banks is an event which the Management Committees of both institutions are justified in regarding with</p>
        <p>considerable satisfaction. It is not, in this instance, a case of,a decaying bahk being taken over by a flourishing one, with a view to its preservatiOn or rejuvenation, for both the War- rington and Runcorn 'Banks are in the hey-day of prosperity, and are doing an invaluable ser- vice to two large working-class communities. rthe fusion of forces is, however, eminently desirable, as it will place the extra advantages of the Special Investment Department at the disposal of Runcorn depositors, and, in addi- tion to protecting the interests of the Warring- ton Bank in the Norton, Halton and Widnes dis- tricts. will undouhtedly bring about an accession of business to the parent institution. The com- mittees of both banks are to be congratulated upon taking a step which should assuredly be fraught with -lasting benefits to the two impor- tant undertakings; which will shortly be known by the one -title of the Warrington; Runcorn and. District Savings Bank.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>SAN ITORWM ENLARGEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE SAN ITORWM ENLARGEMENT.</p>
        <p>The scheme for the: proposed •alterations and additions to the Sankey Sanatorium has not yet reached the stage at which a definite move for- ward with the work can be made. Plans have been before the Local Government Board for some considerable time and, with the object of expediting matters, a deputation, consisting of the Mayor; the Chairman of the Health Com; inittee (Alderman James Evans), the Medical Officer of Health and the Borough Surveyor, waited upon the Board on Wednesday. The outcome of the meeting is that the principle of the extension is approved., but Several points concerning the amendment of details have not been .finally settled. Another important ques- tion which has now arisen is the provision of a new smallpox, hospital, in place of the Sankey Sanatorium, which is now entirely, given over to the treatment of tuberculous patients. It is incumbent upon the authorities to be pre- pared for all emergencies, and a scheme is in contemplation whereby another hospital will be built on land belonging to the Corporation. This question was also, raised in the conference with the Local Government Board, but no binding arrangement was arrived at..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>CHILDREN'S EYEs!ogin".</head>
        <p>THE CHILDREN'S EYEs!ogin".</p>
        <p>Singe. the inspection of school Children came into operation, the cause or Such an alarming amount of defective eyesight, as ,at present hampers the young in their studies, has been mystery. All manner of suggestions were , made, only to be discounted on investigation. A feasible cause, of at least a part of the defec- tive sight amongst school children, has been suggested by Dr. Gibson, who is school Medical inspector for Mid-Cheshire. ) The doctor has been examining the books used in schools,. and, as a result, has drawn the .conclusien that these are, in many cases, injurious to youthful eyes. Curiously enough. a committee, which has gone into the matter at the instance of the BOard of Education, has arrived at: practically the same Conclusion. The investigations should not end with that ; they ought to be prosecuted, in ,Order definitely to ascertain the nature of print and paper that removes -the lurking danger.,' It is a waste of money to have to repair the ; eyesight of the children if the cause can be prevented, and, if the new medical theory is correct, prevention seems simpler than cure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH SHOW.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH SHOW.</p>
        <p>The Sun beamed on the enterprise of the Stockton Heath Garden Society on Bank Holi- day, and, from every point of view, the twelfth annual summer show was the complete success it deseived to be.' -Recalling the earlier exhi- bitions on which the society once prided itself, one could not fail to be impressed with the wonderful improvement in quality, which the experience of the intervening years has made possible. Many -of the blooms displayed were really superb examples' of what, an intelligently followed hobby can produce, .and showed that the SoCiety ii*exerciMfq.., wholesome and in influence among the :gardeninl. fraternity— unit in Stockton Heath their name is.legion. We were particularly glad- to see that much pro-. tninence is being-given to the work of the juvenile *section, and the • many beautiful and remarkably varied collections of wild flowers which the -youngsters had sent to the show were a proof that -Saturday afternoon rambles in the Cheshire lanes had been turned to educa- tional and refining uses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>NIAYOH AND AMALGAMATION„</head>
        <p>THE NIAYOH AND AMALGAMATION„</p>
        <p>_ There was much good-natured chaff at the opening eeremonY with reference to the rela- tions between Warrington and Stockton Heath. The, Mayor, however, took occasion to put in a good word in season, and his plea for amal- gamation, though short, was, very pointed, and must carry considerable weight, as indicating the,sympathetic attitude of the chief magistrate towards a proposal which at present seems to he hanging fire. . Not many months ago the "Heathens," in their desire to escape from the ever-increasing burden of county and district rates, -were, apparently,- quite eager to throw in their lot with the borough. Reflection has; how- ever, induced hesitation in many quarters—in fact, one prominent public Man in . Stockton Heath did not hesitate to tell us, a few, weeks ago, that amalgamation was "as dead as a. door nail." The Town Council is. however, pledged to consider the question of "Greater Warrington," ' as it affects several parishes, besides Stockton Heath., and, judging from his speech on Monday, the Mayor is inclined to take an optimistic view of the Situation. As his Worship pointed out, there is unquestionably a community of interest between Warrington and its suburbs on the other side of the Shin -Canal.. but 'as to whether amalgamation would be fridtually beneficial, "opinion is somewhat' sharply divided. .The outcome of the special meeting of the, General Purposes Committee. which IS to consider the question., will be ziN.vaited with considerable interest by all sections of ratepayers, whether municipal or rural.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>HOUSING PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>HOUSING PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>_ The rural districts around Warrington have to contend with housing problems, but, the mat- ter does not appear to be so acute as in other areas. still, it is sufficiently bad in Cheshire to cause the Medical Officer to devote consider- able Space in his annual report to the subject. Dr. Meredith. Young does not think the recent Housing Act can meet the case:-.. He contends that cottages -to suit agricultural tenants can- not be erected economically enough to make them pay, and argues on the lines that a cot- tage cannot profitably be built and let for 3s. per week, the usual price paid by farm servants. He advocates, instead, the erection of tempor- ary structures, to last 25 years. .But is not the doctor, in making this recommendation,' viewing economy .froni. a wrong angle? _Tem- porary buildings at the initial 'point, are about one-third less in cost than permanent struc- tures, but the maintenance charges. are con- siderably higher, and, At the end of 25 years, the 'erected-for-its-day cottage will have, to be thrown upon the scrap. heap, while the per- manent structure will be, at its best. Reduced, to its logical basis, the question becomes—is it not more economical to erect a permanent, suitable building, at a cost of ,R2OO, than a tem- porary one, not suitable, at a cost of. .Rl5O, which "Will have to be rebuilt, at similar cost, every 25 years? The labourer is worthy of a decent home, and, if he is to be kept. on the soil, the landlords must' loosen their purse- strings and ,provide a suitable abode.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SUNSET IN THE LOWLANDS.</head>
        <p>SUNSET IN THE LOWLANDS.</p>
        <p>The rocking woods their chanting cease. O'er Nature steals the mood of peace. Across the carse the sunset beams Flow in ardent, golden streams, They (.4e the river's placid flood And burnish all the gay green-wood. At length the bright sun sinks to rest, Yet, gleam his banners in the west. Now slowly ebbs the light away, And dark-tress'd night resumps her sway. . C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>From Everywhere.</head>
        <p>From Everywhere.</p>
        <p>A girl marries to gain liberty; a man remains single to keep it. And many a. woman who angles in the matrimonial sea is looking for a goldfish.</p>
        <p>Women usually have more religion than men, because they need it less. The folks who "choose their own company" shouldn't always boast about IL</p>
        <p>The Secret. The secret of a woman's dress, says a local philosopher, is the pocket. The young ladies of Warrington say , they do not keep such "secrets."</p>
        <p>A Kind of Love.</p>
        <p>A little Stoekton Heath boy was being ques- tioned upon general knowledge. He was asked to describe a cannibal. "A cannibal is a man," came the answer, " who loves his fellow-men."</p>
        <p>Not Any Longer. . "Pa," said the modern youngster, "have you heard that the Council are, not going to have the tramway poles in Bridge-street any longer?"</p>
        <p>"No; why?" "Because," said the boy, scouting for the door, "they're long enough already."</p>
        <p>Pigeon on tlie Bench. A live pigeon fluttered about the Chairman's desk at the London Sessions on Thursday, and hopped into the Deputy-chairman's hand. It was one of several birds which Were the subject of a charge of theft. To One Thrashing : • Os.</p>
        <p>In a ease arising out, of a trade dispute beard at London Sessions op Thursday, prosecuting counsel asserted that a boxer had been engaged to give a particular man a thrashing for 65., or a more severe one, to prevent him working, for 10s.</p>
        <p>13;000 Miles to Get Married.</p>
        <p>At the Parish Church ,of .Torpenhow, Cum- berland, 'Adolph Hanson and Eviline Jackson, of Auckland County, New Zealand, were, mar- ried on Wednesday. They had travelled 13,000 miles to be made husband and wife, in the church where, 25 years ago, the bride's parents were married.</p>
        <p>Guardians Share Lunatic's Prize. One of the "three lunatics in East Sussex, Asylum, who have Won a -X-100 prize in a weekly paper's competition, has been in the asylum for 45 years, and the Bye Guardians have retained £23 of the man's share as a set-off to the cost of his maintenance. Ten pounds is to be spent on luxuries fot: the man.</p>
        <p>Were You Aware of the Fact That— In Japan it is against the law to smoke till 20 years old? Flesh-eating birds can't sing? St. Petersburg is the unhealthiest capital? Cotton grows in Peru in 11 different colours? The Greeks still train their soldiers in stone throwing? They Were Poor. Company. _</p>
        <p>In a recent book, Mlle. van Vorst tells a story of a dinner at Chicago given by the ambitious wife of a millionaire tanner. The lady noticed, to her annoyance, that her husband did not speak a, word to their smart guests, and when she got an opportunity she whispered angrily, " Why don't you talk?" " What's the good?" replied the tanner, contemptuously. "There ain't one of 'em as knows a thing about leather:" • -</p>
        <p>. . There's Always Something. •Harry Lauder tells it story of two Glasgow women who met in the street and began to dis- cuss the domestic affairs of ,a newly-married couple. "Aye, Mrs. McTavish," said one, "SO Jeannie's got mairret." "She has that, Mrs. :McAlpine," replied the other. "An' how's she :gettile On?" .the first'wOman wanted to Ithow. " Oh,!no sae bad at a'," was the reply. "There's only One thing the matter. she thinks she could hae got a better man. But, then, there's aye , something!'</p>
        <p>Said the Wrong Thing. The hairdresser was of the loquacious type, and after 'talking On all manner of Subjects, he became personal. "Your hair is getting thin, sir," he informCd the customer. e</p>
        <p>_ "Perhaps it is!" answered the man being operated upon. . "Yes. Now, we have some stuff here which will make any hair conic. out." The barber was surprised that the customer .did not.,,immediately ,order, a :large .supply. of the " restorer." He overlooked the fact that he did not give the stuff a flattering recommenda- tion. .</p>
        <p>The Top Hat a "Moribund Incubus." We are interested to note that even the "Medical Press," in alluding to the alleged centenary of the top hat, calls it a "moribund incubus' upon the head of the doctor. Every- one has noted its disappearance from the dress of our younger and gayer men. But hitherto the, doctor has generally regarded it as the emblem of sober authority. Now it is pleasant to know that when we invite his attendance he will hang' up 4-1-IMnburg' or a straw in- our hall' and skin up cheerfully to our bedside.- For we want "courage; gaiety, and the quiet mind," and not the pompous -smoothing 'of a Silk hat.- The " stove-pipe ". -should be reserved for the "Most solemn arid' ceremonial occasions, such as weddings and, funerals, to which .we must all be called 40Oner or later. -Even sO we imagine, in happy forecast, the last silk hat, mori- bund and mortuary, on the head of an under- taker.—" Westminster Gazette."</p>
        <p>A Terrible Coincidence.</p>
        <p>Some time ago an amusing story was told connected with Dr. Chavasse, the Bishop of Liverpool. Dr. Chavasse is, as most people know, the father of two sets of twins, and soon after Mrs. Chavasse had had twins for the second time a curate from a neighbouring parish was asked to preach the Sunday morn- ing sermon in Dr. Chavasse's church. By an extraordinary coincidence, he chose as his text the words, "Two are better than one." A quite audible giggle came from one or two members of the congregation, and quite ignorint of the cause of his hearer's amusement, the curate gazed at them in shocked surprise. Then. fearing that there might be somethn!,.! amiss with his surplice, he tried to examine himself. Finding nothing amiss; he looked, sternly at the congregation and .repeated his text, in a loud, clear voice, "Two are' better than one." As he now seemed to be calling attention to his little joke, the entire congregation quivered with, suppressed mirth.</p>
        <p>"The Pigs All Nowt" Amusing anecdotes were related to the Lan- cashire County Council at Preston, on Thurs- day, by Dr. Chadwick, chairman of the Mid- wives' Committee.</p>
        <p>Alluding to improvements in sanitation, he Mentioned that once he . attended a • woman, whose bed, he noticed, was in a room which had a pigsty just outside the window. The reply to this protest was: "What of that! The pigs ail nowt. They're doibg gradely wee]." He remembered seeing the woman's father on his death-bed. He was 90 years old, had kept a "pub." 50 years, had never been drunk once, and had been married twice. My his first wife he had 12. children, and 14 by the second. Mentioning this to another doctor, the latter said that was good, but he knew of a gentleman in Momnouthshire who also matried twice. He had 11, children by his first wife, and 22 by his second. The 33 children and the father had all been to church at once, occupying six pews.</p>
        <p>"!can't help thinking what ,a great boon and a blessing the maternity benefit will be in these cases," concluded Dr. Chadwick, amid roars of</p>
        <p>ulei</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>SIXTEEN HUNDRED O BOYS IN CI).</head>
        <p>SIXTEEN HUNDRED O BOYS IN CI).</p>
        <p>How the Church Lads' Brigade Assembled</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>at Prestatyn.</head>
        <p>at Prestatyn.</p>
        <p>GENERAL METHUEN'S PRAISE.</p>
        <p>From our Special</p>
        <p>PRFSTATYN, TUESDAY,</p>
        <p>Last Saturday the North-Western Camp of the Church Lads' Brigade for 1913 at Prestatyn began a week's work that promised to be as fruitful for good as any of the preceding gath- erings. That, by the way, is saying a good deal, for the North-Western is the pioneer camp, and it has kept in the very front rank since it indicated the right path to others. In this camp, assembled largely at their own expense, are no fewer than 1,600 lads, who are . _</p>
        <p>no mean imitators of Cromwell's all-conquering soldiers—young men of principle and of reli- gion, anxious to serve their country by doing their duty at all times, and willing to submit themselves to semi-military discipline, while they are being instructed into the conception of duty. The lads are gathered from far and wide, contingents coming from Warrington, Whitehaven, Manchester, Lancaster, Preston, Derby, St. Helens, Ashton-in-Makerfield, Wigan, Leicester, Loughboro', Knutsford, Birkenhead, Carnarvon,. Chester, Northwich, Sale, Hale, Altrinchain, Macclesfield, Prestbury; Alderley Edge, Wihnslow, Styal, Tupperley, Nantwich, Helsby, and other centres.</p>
        <p>- The perfect discipline and accomplishments of the lads could not, perhaps, have been better displayed than by the manner in which the camp- became a living thing on Saturday. The units began to arrive in the earlY morning, the travelling having .been done by night, and iY afternoon the camp was lit for inspection by no less distinguished men than Lord Methuen and General MacKinnon, who confessed ,them- selves, before leaving, to have been very well pleased with all they had seem</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>ispiriting Church Parade.</head>
        <p>ispiriting Church Parade.</p>
        <p>I attended church parade on Sunday miming</p>
        <p>and found, the cere-mony. an inspiriting one. The whole camp turned out in first-class order, accompanied by .so many chaplains as to sug- gest that a good many churches had been denuded of their shepherds, in order that these particular lambs might be tended: It was really an impressiVe sight—and a promising one in times whene we are supposed to be giving ourselves ,up, wholesale and hopelessly, to</p>
        <p>pleasure and materialism, and godlessness. Si teen • hundred lads assembled in a hollow square to engage reverently, in the midst of</p>
        <p>their short, annual holiday, in divine service, was some proof that the country is still sound of heart. Most people have heard the splendid</p>
        <p>iusie that our Church Lads' Brigade bugle</p>
        <p>bands can produce; few could conceive the grand effect that the massed bands—brass, bugle, and drums—gave forth in this camp. On Saturday night the hills on the one hand, and the beach on the. other, resounded to the sweet strains of these ardent young musicians, the tunes of well-known hymns making visitors, who were not aware of the nature of the camp, wonder what could be the meaning of it all. The meaning was apparent on Sunday—the bands had been combining and making them- selves perfect for the morrow. And perfect. they assuredly were, for the 'accompaniments they played to the finest and most militant 3! the Church's hymns—" Fight the good fight, "Onward, Christian_ soldiers," and the like— gave the one finishing touch that was needed to make- the .service a real and' a magnificent reality. The I:iimP Chaplain (the' Rev. Tt. W.-White, M.A.), who was the ,preacher took fiat advan- tage .of his opportunity to advocate the claitrs of duty, love, and courage upon his young audi- ence. • Ditty, he said, was to overcome all obstacles; courage was to know no fear in doing right according to the dictates of con- science, and love was self-sacrifice in the ser- vice of others, not that sentimental, rotten thing that so often merely means self-indulgence and the suffering of others. The Brigade made a brave show as they marched back to their lines, saluting the Briga- dier, Colonel H. Moore (late Colonel Command- ing Bth Ardwick Battalion, M.R.), on entering</p>
        <p>the camp. There are two battalions, one under Lieutenant-Colonel Simpson (Wigan) apd the other under Lieutenant-Colonel Gordon (Head- quarters, London).</p>
        <p>Captain• Appleton and Warrington. Captain Appleton, of Warrington, who ranks as Lieutenant-Colonel in the Brigade, was pre- sent, delighted with the very fine ,nature of the camp, and with the progress of the, organisa- tion, in which he has always been keenly-inter- ested. I looked him up on-Monday - morning; but found him busy—as usual—having been asked by Lieutenant-Colonel Simpson to take charge of the second. battalion for him, as he had urgent calls in other directions. Colonel Appleton was acting major for Colonel Simp- son. Another item of local interest is that: Sergeant 'Edwards, from the Warrington Depot, South Lancashire Regiment., is, by the courtesy of Major Green, acting as sergeant-major of the Second Battalion, and is doing very good work. The Warrington lads are not so largely repre- sented as they ought to have been. Boisterous. but Serious.</p>
        <p>The camp presents that pictUro. of mixed boisterousness and seriousneth so peculiarly appropriate to boy life.:.; . Above all, notwithstanding the man/ acti- vities on its military—and musical—side, the camp, 'as a. whole, prides. itself on its religious aspect, and the influence brought to bear in this direction at home is developed to the full here. For instance, immediately after reveille, shortly after five in the morning, and immediately before lights out, shortly 'after ten o'clock at night, there is "prayer silence." At these times there is absolute silence, and the boys in their respective tents engage in private prayer, the marked hush .and the sacred exercise on hand being well calculated to make a lasting impression on the minds of those who are at the most impressionable period of their lives. Holy Communion is administered, and the chaplains attend fully and conscientiously to all the details of their particular calling.</p>
        <p>The Military Side. The work the lads engage in is much the same as that of the Territorials. Early morn- ing parades are followed by other duties, but .the evenings, are given .up to entertainments promoted to find counter-attraction's to enjoy- ments that boys away from home might easily find. A striking feature .of the camp Is the large number of officers present, who have been at all, or nearly all, the camps since they were instituted, nearly twenty years ago. This is proof that the work of looking after the welfare of lads has a fascination—and a reward—that gives it more than a fleeting interest. Another feature is the number of real war medals that are worn by the officers and others. The Brigade staff includes, in addition to those I have mentioned elsewhere: Staff Chaplain, the Bev. C. E. Jenkins, • Leicester; Brigade Major, Lieutenant-Colonel J. D. Daintree (Captain, R.N.), Oxford; Brigade Quartermaster. Lieut- enant-Colonel Ilearsey (Captain, retired pay) ; A.D.C., Captain 11. Stuttard. The Ambulance.</p>
        <p>All the boys are supposed • to be medically examined before going to camp, but every pro- vision is made for the comfort of, and Koper attention for, any who May fall by the way. Camp life has its trials. To be washed Out of one's tent is a trying ordeal for a lad ; • to be scorched under a tropical sun is, perhaps, as dangerous. On Sunday morning the/ boys dropped in some profusion, owing to the execs- SiVe heat, but the ambulance boys, fifty in num- ber, stationed at different points during the service, were equal to every call, and Conveyed the sufferers to the shelter of the tents or hospi-</p>
