        <p>A Open-air Musical Festival. rt ()De angements are practically complete for the musical festival,_ which will be held in Park, in aid of the Warrington Infirmary, °unday, August 17th. A choice programme illi,s,,,acred music will be submitted by massed Atrum, voice choirs, under the conductorship of alsc,* V. 11. Payton. Crosfield's Prize Band will Way 'some excellent selgctio us.</p>
        <p>The Sunny Spell. • th, eltsant weather has been experienced rit;"l3hout the past week, and the many W ar- • 44,4,t:-o_,a people who have been holidaying by the bt,ue are returning home quite bronzed. The im7Pects of good weather over the week-end are o,yrable, although the barometer is showing el,:iight downward tendency. The. fall, how- " 8 not sufficient to give indication of a thci`"l in the weather for the next few days, and. "tiny spell is likely to continue:</p>
        <p>Health of the Town. more girls than boys came to happy War"- beiVII parents last week. -- The births num-, ir 47, consisting of 23 females and 14 nialeki.. corresponding week last year the number 14d 11 (21 males and 20 females). There were eatts, giving a rate of 9.8, amongst the lowest Fii,o44,neashire; compared with 10.6 for the carre- -1 Itt nfuing week of 1912. Two new cases of scar- one of diphtheria, and nitte of tuber- the()llB were reported, while 54 patients were in • '°BPitals. '</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Choristers' Ttip. A ht, Pleasant day, was spent 14 the choir mem- Fitr'Zkod friends of the Emmanuel Church, Bold- 'll,7ti Warrington, at West Kirby on Saturday. stit,"ceasion was the annual trip, and the plea- secured. made the outing a record one. tV-el of interest were visited., some trusted Nnaselves upon the briny in boats, and other 46..Pt'tted members ventured upon a joy ride Plcg the beach upon the backs of the donkeys. 411,e11e doffing shoes and stockings, a considerable tligosr waded across to Hilbre Island, where lit4ddlers spent a merry time. The more el1,4!I did the journey to the:island- in 4 pony, se.</p>
        <p>Groom Gains Diploma 4 interesting honour was secured at the 140;5.' LaJicashire Show at Burnley by Mr. John • ttottz of 11, Kendrick-street, -Warrington, n employed by Messrs. Peter Walker and ,IV(.l,'ld• This took the form of a diploma, is presented for the most points secured keilegeoeral cleanliness, good grooming, and 44,34.1vtai.oPpearance of the animal exhibited. Ilierc," tew diplomas are 'awarded each year, and t keen competition for the honour. Thus qt..). 'we, in securing the ." Mrs. Herbert L. he niploma," has achieved a notable feat 131'Se he exhibited was the well-known 441 44, The General," and although it took s -tur„,•—'ood place in the agriculture geldings at it has now more than 30 first and awards standing to its credit.</p>
        <p>te, -ellers and Choir at Llangollen.. - totitu,,,af the most enjoyable picnics ever held. in -144L-Ltia,._ll with. tlie -Working _Men's Mission Sel-,I•Y Nehool took place on Monday, when .t.I,W 4.14171 and Members of the Choir journeyed, to et"en. :Leaving., Bank 'Quay. Station ,at i°*tikaq' IheY arrived at the picturesque Welsh ne o'clock, refreshments being served sainnn en route. Favoured' with glorious ~a• sixteen-mile drive to Glyndylydwy pr"lt k.- i ed in Passing through Llantysilio, s —11" Y a greatly admired..especially the •Fallss,m-here- a short stay was „`ilog through Berwyn,' the party arrived :agollen in time for dinner. The aft.-q.norin l'ent climbing 'some 'of the mounsns, 'Nvor . n."Ying the pleasure of a trip oy boat to )7144:7-f lioteea.l was served at the Wa.vez•by q em- i , and afterwards the 1,1).1 d ~ visited oeauty spots n the district, inelud si!'t mata!3, ewYdd and Castell Dinas flrati. 'Tiied, 'ilivi„"sit,ed, the visitors boarded the 9.211 tiain, -g in Warrington about 11 p.m. ai the Electricity Works. on Sun day morning fire broke out e' prnoratin o El ticitv Works at Howley. wlttlitd}ZtftiPiul3l,irgade wae: quickly on the scene. 'iteutuThe fire' eetign of Superintendent Turner, ~ ahor, et h., engine, Major. After. for the a he been working from the rive Mar Ilery a defect- was discovered in and and another engine was sent for,. I 8111 e8.4'111...the fire from the. other side ot.stbei. thunile,4l'he fire was confined.. to the j:0:4; 1)N. fire `ouße and stack elevator. Th. task tj toe I was rendered exceedingly difficukt ar, tiv kir quantity of coal dust which filled 114(1„ ho°,d almost stifled them. After about bee.'ll .ivrork the fire, which is supposed to thttrlk, niqi,(ll.le to spontaneous combustion. was : . tor.l4eetril" There was no interference with 1%-4e of "3' supply of the boroligh. A large 41,1: in charge of the Chief Constable. tII4IIR to•ti3" In the vicinitY of the works, but, Usual I eordrltyo hour,tbliee. fire did