      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Gift to St. Luke's Church.</head>
        <p>Gift to St. Luke's Church.</p>
        <p>• A handsome pocket silver communion service has been presented to St. Luke's Church, War- rington, by Mr. W. E. Wild, brother of the late H. H. Wild.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Cycling at Southport.</head>
        <p>Cycling at Southport.</p>
        <p>In the open cycle handicap at the Southport Infirmary sports on Saturday, the third prize was ivon by C. Berry, Warrington Athletic Club. lie was just beaten by men with long handicaps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Extra Pay for Police.</head>
        <p>Extra Pay for Police.</p>
        <p>The Watch Committee has decided that each member of the Borough Police Force be granted seven days' extra pay in consideration of extra duty performed in connection with strikes in Warrington in April and May, as also on the occasion of the recent Royal visit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Renovation of Holy Trinity Church.</head>
        <p>Renovation of Holy Trinity Church.</p>
        <p>The fund to meet. the expenses entailed in the renovation of Holy Trinity Parish Church, Warrington, has now reached the total of ,C 483 15s. 10d. A balance of £l7 still remains to be raised, hut the treasurer (Mr. Gill) is hopeful that this will soon be forthcoming.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Billiard League.</head>
        <p>Billiard League.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the Warring- ton Billiard. League will be held on Monday.. August 18th, at MO p.m., in Rylands' Recrea- tiOn Club, Gorsey-lane, Teams intending, to join Must send in their applications by to-night (Saturday) to Mr. John Craik, 278, Manchester- road, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Amongst the Anglers. The _anglers of Warrington were well repre- sented at the great contest held on the river Weaver at Northwich on Bank Holiday. There were 260 competitors. The second prize was won by W. Millington, Warrington, with 11b. 14oz. Mr. weight, and F. Jolley, Warrington, was fourth with 11b. 6oz. 12dr. of fish.</p>
        <p>Holy Trinity Day School. Progress is now being made with the provi- sion of the new infant department at Holy Trinity Day School, and the alterations are due to be completed in time for the opening of the school after the holidays. The difficulty over the teacher question has been overcome,, and Miss Bullen will probably be head mistress of the infant department.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Shunting Fatality.</head>
        <p>Shunting Fatality.</p>
        <p>J. Lees, who was employed at the Lead Com- pany's Works, Sankey Bridges, died yesterday afternoon in the Warrington, infirmary, as the result of an accident which befel him on Thursday afternoon. Shunting operations were taking place, and as Lees was Nr alting alongside the line, he was overtaken by a shunted waggon and crushed against the wall.</p>
        <p>Winwiek-road Bowling Green. The Local Government Board, having intim- ated that they would prefer any additional sum required for the construction of the bowling green in Winwick-road to be deferred until after the completion of the work, when the total cost could be ascertained, the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee has resolved that the proposed application for the loan of .1.322 19s. 11d, be accordingly deferred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Music in the Park.</head>
        <p>Music in the Park.</p>
        <p>The Warrington R.A.0.8.i Military Band will play the following selections of music to-morrow (Sunday), at 3.15 • o'clock: Hymn (selected); quick'march, "The Children of the Regiment," Julius-Fucik; selection, " Mirella " (by request), Gounod ; overture, "Poet and Peasant,' Suppe; selection from the works of Verdi, Hartma • intermezzo, " Cockico," Reeve's; grand march, "Entry of .the: Gladiators,", Julius rucik. Con- duCtor,'Mr. E. Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Church Day Schools Endangered.</head>
        <p>Church Day Schools Endangered.</p>
        <p>_ . The Rev. W..Braeecamp, vicar of St. Paul's, Warrington, states in the Parish Magazine that the St. Paul's day schools are in danger of being taken over by the Education Committee unless the finances are placed upon a sound foundation almost immediately. He appeals to his congregation to raise a fund of Xl,OOO, and states that the Bishop of Liverpool has headed the list with.a donation of £lO. "At all costs and self-:sacrifice," he adds, "the schools must be Raved to the Church." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>C.E.M.S. Do a Good Turn.</head>
        <p>C.E.M.S. Do a Good Turn.</p>
        <p>•The members of the Church of England Men's Society are adopting the Bey Scouts' policy of doing "good turns." They have practised it in connection with St. Paul's Church, Warring- ton. The grave of the Rev. Mr. Lowe, the first incumbent of the church, has lain for many years in a dilapidated condition, and the .C.E.M,S. ,decided ..that such was not creditable. to the church, so they took the task in hand, and restored the grave into a respectable condi- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Forthcoming Marriage of the Rev. H.</head>
        <p>Forthcoming Marriage of the Rev. H.</p>
        <p>Ogden-.</p>
        <p>A marriage of great interest will be solemnised at Golborne-street Baptist Church on Thursday next, at 2' p.m., when the *Pastor (Rev. H. Ogden) will be married to Miss A. Wright, daughter of Mr. William Wright, of Cross-lane, Grappenhall. The service will he choral, and the officiating minister 'will be the Rev. T. N. Tattersall, of London, assisted by the Revs. G. Curtis (Hill Cliffe) and J. S. Chedburn (Latch- ford).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Across the Sea to 'Wed.</head>
        <p>Across the Sea to 'Wed.</p>
        <p>Miss Walker, a prominent worker connected with St. Paul's Church, Warrington, is pro- ceeding to New Zealand to marry Mr. Sam Mason, who was formerly connected with .the church, and emigrated to the Antipodes for health reasons. The marriage Will take place in New Zealand early in the autumn, and Miss Walker has received presentations from St. Paul's Girls' Sunday Sack)] and the Girls' Friendly Society, on behalf Of which she accom- plished much .useful work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Shrewsbury Great Floral Fete.</head>
        <p>Shrewsbury Great Floral Fete.</p>
        <p>The great annual fete of the Shropshire Horti- cultural Society will be held on Wednesday and Thursday, August 20th and 21st, in the Quarry Grounds, Shrewsbury. A new feature of very great interest this year will he a unique display of rock gardens, and a magnificent collection of roses is ensured for the champion classes. The bands engaged are those of the Coldstream Guards, the Royal Marines, and the 4th Performances will be given by the premier artistes of Europe, and an incomparable day's entertainment will wind up with brilliant Pyrotechnic effects and gorgeous illuminations of the peerless limes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>'Worker's Claim Seined.</head>
        <p>'Worker's Claim Seined.</p>
        <p>There was an unexpected settlement in a Workmen's Compensation Act claim, which was expected to take up a considerable amount of time before Judge Stanger, K.C., at Warrington County Court on Thursday. John Henry Spen- cer ,wire drawer, of 148, Gorsey-lane, Warring- ton, claimed compensation from the Longford Wire Company, Warrington, in respect to acci- dental injuries. he received while at work. Mr. licLeery. barrister, for applicant, said that his 'chief witness, Dr. Fox, had been called away to attend the funeral of his father, who had died this week, and he could not go on without the evidence of Dr. Fox. The Judge suggested a conference between the parties, and later it was announced that the case WAS settled. The applicant was to receive £l5O, and there was no order as to costs.</p>
        <p>• school attached to that communion in St. Helens, was supplemented by private tuition by the'Rev. Arthur Ullathorne. He afterwards went to St. Mary's Roman 'Catholic College, London. Destined for the medical proression, he studied for a short time under Dr. Light- burnei of Blackburn, and passed the Medical Registration. Examination at Durham -Upiver- sity. He commenced his medical curriculum at the Liverpool School of Medicine and Liver- pool Royal Infirmary, and Was' resident exhi- bitioner at that hospital for the last six months of his studentship.. In the year 1871, having passed his final examination' of the Rival Col- leges of. Physicians and. Surgeons,. Edinburgh, he came to Warrington a stranger and-settled down as a general practitioner. In 1872 he married Mary; the 'only daughter. -Of the late Mr. Davkl Green, of Warrington.- -Dr.' Fox-de- voted himself to his professional duties with great zeal and quickly built Up a large praCtice In those days there were only twelve Medical men in Warrington, several of them being very old, and the population of .the town was about 33,000.</p>
        <p>Dr. Fox's long and .useful association with the work of the *Warrington Infirmary- began in 1876, When he was elected hon, surgeon at the old Warrington Dispensary, in Butter- market-street,.. the forerunner .of. the present fine institution In Kendrick-street. At that time only six beds•and two nurses were pro- vided---it striking contrast to the large and magnificently equipped Infirmary vihich now serves the needs of the borough,. Dr. Fox re- mained on the active staff of medical officers until about ten years ago, when, on the retire- ment Of Mr. Charles White, he was elected consulting surgeon—an honour earned by years of ungrudging service. In 1885 Dr. Fox became a life governor of the, institution, by virtue of his being the executor of the late Mr. John Bickerstaffe, dOnOr of £5OO. In 1891 Dr. Fox passed the required examina- tion for practitioners of 15 years' standing at the, Durham University, and the Degree of Doc- tor of Medicine was conferred upon him. In 1900 he was appointed a Justice of the Peace for NVarrington, and until recently was very regular in his attendance on the bench at the Borough Police Court. In politics' he was a strong Conservative, but. the question Which particularly interested him was the mainten- ance of denominational instruction in the ele- mentary schools—a subject upon which be spoke during several Parliamentary electiOns. In the year 1898 the attention of Dr. Fox was called by the late Mr. Hobert Garnett to the distress existing in the West of Ireland through the failure of the potato crops, and having satisfied himself of the urgency of the demand he collected £3OO, which was sent ,to the Cen- tral Fund •in Manchester, £lOO having been contributed by Mr. H. Thornton.: • ' The doctor *represented for several years the .medical profession in Warrington on the Coun- cil of the Lancashire and Cheshire branch of the British. Medical Association. . -</p>
        <p>For one year Dr. Fox served ns a member of the Warrington Board of Guardians:</p>
        <p>MAGISTERIAL TRIBUTES,</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police. Court on Wednesday, the Chairman (Alderman Smethurst) expressed the sympathy of the magistrates.' with the bereaved family, especially so in view of the fact-that they had, only recently lost their Mother. Dr. Fox .was very well known. in Warrington. lie (Alderman Smethurst) had known.him.personally for 30 years, and he riad always found him a kind, genial ma.n.• By his death the town had lost a-worthf servant. Dr. Fox had been a consulting surgeon at the In- firmary for a long number of years, and he had always taken a deep interest in that insti- tution.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Jackson Holmes associated himself with everything the Chairman had said. The doctor had filled a very useful position in the town, and he had enjoyed a great._ amount of public confidence. • Judging by the very large practice which he had acquired, he occupied one of 'the chief positions as a physician in the town. He (Mr.. Holmes) had comeinto contact with the deceased -gentleman more in his capacity as a magistrate. He was very regular in his attention to the duties of the (dice, and very careful in the decisions, he gave.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Alliance Box Company's Annual</head>
        <p>The Alliance Box Company's Annual</p>
        <p>On Saturday the Alliance Box Company's annual Picnic took place. About 100 of the employes left the Central Station in reserved carriages at 9.20 a.m., en route for Liverpool. Arrived there, they were met by two special cars, which conveyed them to the Landing Stage, where, they boarded the Eastham boat. The sail was enjoyed by all, and after a pleasant hour in the Eastham woods and gardens, the party re- turned to luncheon at Messrs. Cottle's Cafe, Lord-street, Liverpool. Afterwards a vote of thanks to the organisers of the affair was pro- posed by Mr. Fairclough. Mr. Dolan suitably replied, and also expressed the gratitude of the workpeople to their employers for the kind interest taken and the substantial assistance given to the outing. The party then crossed to New Brighton, and after a merry time spent on the sands, assembled for tea in the Tower, where the remainder of the evening was passed. The weather was perfect, and the verdict of the party on returning was that they had never spent a more -enjoyable holiday.</p>
        <p>TENNIS, CROQUET, CRICKET B:,* other outdoor Games.—.T. S. GARLAND, 51, 53 &amp; 55, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 9, 1913. DEATH OF Di. E. A. FOX.I PARR HALL ORGAN. I GALLANT SCHOOLBOY.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 9, 1913. DEATH OF Di. E. A. FOX.I PARR HALL ORGAN. I GALLANT SCHOOLBOY.</p>
        <p>Long and Devoted Service to the Infirmary. NOTABLE CAREER.</p>
        <p>-rOne who .will be chiefly remembered for devoted work on behalf of the Warrington Infirmary, passed away on Tuesday in the person of • Dr—Edward Austin Fox, J.P., for many years one of the best known medical practitioners in the borough. Since his.retire- ment from practice, about eight years ago, Dr. Fox had resided at Holly Bank, Mount-road, New Brighton, but he frequently visited 'War- rington, and took duty for his Son, Dr. Edward Fox, during that gentleman's holidays. Two months ago Dr. Fox sustained a; severe blow by the death of his wife, and he never regained his accustomed cheeriness of disposition. His health, which had previously given rise to some anxiety, continued to decline, and a visit to 'Buxton failed to produce any improvement. He became much worse.. on Sunday, and the end came peacefully at three o'clock on Thes- day morning. The doctor leaves four sons— Dr. Edward Fox ; Mr. Arthur Fox, solicitor; Mr; Herbert -Fox, ,8.A., London, who is in the Indian Civil Service at. Bombay; and Mr. Reginald Fox, a dentist—and three daughters. The news of his death was received with deep regret by his many friends in Warrington, and, as a tribute of resnect, the flag at the Town Hall was .displayed at half-mast. Dr. Fox was born in St. Helens on May 131 h, 1845, and was therefore 68 years of age. He came of a well-known Roman Catholic family, and his early education, received at the only</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>....-.10k,,,,p.,::::%•••• ' . .... , ... ... .. * .A 4 •T 4 '.•R'.,,,,. : . ‘...,. ::::::::•:-..'•••••••.7.,:.. ''''•l.... - -.:1:.• .. .::,::::r ... ~.h.-4414':::::!:•:,-" ....J4t4:11j;,:...:::„:„ --...i:.• ---., ..-....:- ..,•;,,,...v.,::::.!,-„T.i•A::........,.. . :-....:••,/ -•• • ..•••;:-:,••,..,:::::•,:t•s:•,-.:: s:,I.0:::::•-•:r...--..•.; • •••‘ :;‘-' ' :•'..*.,•- . ,•••• ••...., ~ • ~. .*:,:-..: • . , opf„ .•::!a•,,.:. ' •• . ~ ...„..,:,. ~ , --1. ••••. ' ' . -.. ~.. ; • : V: 11:!,,:-..-.i?.,:4.•••••,:i;;ii'&lt;i`i.-,:,..; • .•-f i•• ' • • '.'•:..i.-it:•--i:s:-,.....-..., -,...,,,.:,..,,,,'..:, .- S , ,</p>
        <p>Photograph by Birtles. THE LATE DR. E. A. FOX, J.P.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Quiet simplicity characterised the funeral, which took place at the Flaybrick Hill Cemetery, Birkenhead, on Thursday. Prior to the inter- ment, Requiem Mass was offered by the Rey. Father Fox, 0.5.8., brother of the deceased, in SS. Peter and. Paul's Church, New Brighton. The principal mourners were: Dr. E. Fox, Mr. A. Fox, and Mr. R. Fox (soils), Mrs. N. R. Taylor, Mrs. W. P. M'Ardle. and Miss Fox (daughters), Dr. John Fox and Mr. T. A. Fox (brothers), Messrs. W. A. Tayjor and W. P. M'Ardle -(sons-in-law), Mrs. E. Fox (daughter- in-law), Dr. James Fox, Messrs. Harry Fox, and H. Green (nephews). The medical men of Warrington were repre- sented by: The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph), Dr. Langdale, and Dr. Bowden. Mr. J. H. Smethurst (chairman of the Board of Management) attended on behalf of the War- rington Infirmary. The firm of Messrs. J. Monks and Co.. Ltd., was represented by Messrs. J. Monks, Henry Milling, and G. Milling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Mr. W. H. Payton's Report on the</head>
        <p>Mr. W. H. Payton's Report on the</p>
        <p>Old Instrument.</p>
        <p>TENDERS TO BE INVITED.</p>
        <p>The Town Council will, on Tuesday evening next, be invited to sanction public addertise- ment for tenders for the erection of a new organ in the Parr Hall, at a cost of £1,500. At a recent meeting of the General Purposes Com- mittee, the following report of Mr. W. H. Pay- ton, who was called in for expert advice, was read :</p>
        <p>Gentlemen,—Before proceeding to prepare my report and specification for a new organ for the Parr Hall, I considered it advisable to ex- amine the existing organ to see whether any parts of it might be economically utilised in the construction of the new organ, and I beg to submit the result of my examination:- 1. The wind chests and reservoirs are in an extremely dilapidated condition, and are incapable of performing the work required of them. 2. The sound boards are in a ruinous state. 3. The mechanism is corroded, out of order, and out of date. 4. The metal pipes, with very few excep- tions. are worthless except as old metal. 5. The Great Organ is entirely useless, both from an inadequate wind supply and the bad condition of the pipes. 6. There are a few • wood pipes in fairly good order. 7. There are only a few stops that are in anything like ft playable condition. _ 8. The case is in good condition, and may be used again, subject to such alterations as the builder may require. I am clearly of opinion that no part of the existing organ can be economically utilised in the construction of the new organ. and I ven- ture- to advise that the organ should be sold as it stands, but in any. case I think your cora- mittee would act wisely in making it a condition that no particle of the existing organ, except the case, be used in the construction of the new organ.</p>
        <p>I have very carefully considered the size and position of the organ chamber, and the acoustic properties of the hall, and I have prepared a specification of an organ Which, in my opinion, will be of ample power and in every respect suitable fof the building and for the purposes for which the organ will be required. I am of opinion that such an organ, built on the most. modern principles, and of the best materials and workmanship, can .be obtained from a good builder for the Sum of .C 1,500. I do not regard my specification as unalter- able, and when tenders have been obtained some modification of it may be found desirable. I shall be prepared to examine the tenders received, to compare the same, and to report thereon; but before making my report, I should like to have the authority of your committoe to examine organs in use. made .by the owners whose tenders I may wish to recommend.• I venture to suggest that the offer of the old organ and the invitation for tenders for the new one should be 'advertised in the. "Musical Times" and the "Musical Opinion." Awaiting your instructions, I am, gentlemen. Your obedient servant, W. 11. PAYTON, Mite. line. It was rem'ilved that the report and specifica- tion be approved, and that tenders for a new orron, in accordance with the specification, be invited by public advertisement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>COUNCIL TOPICS.</head>
        <p>COUNCIL TOPICS.</p>
        <p>Items for Consideration at Next Week's Meeting.</p>
        <p>The Town Council vill hold its monthly meet- ing at, the Town Hall on ,Tuesday evening next. Some of the more important topics which will occupy attention—notably the provision of ,a .new organ for the Parr Hall, and suggested concessions to users of slot gas. meters—are dealt. with in other parts of this issue.. Other -items of interest 'are the following :</p>
        <p>"-EDUCATION OF TUBERCULOUS CHILDREN. The -Health Committee has .considered the question of the edueation of tuberculous children:</p>
        <p>It was resolved that the attention of the Edu- cation Committee be called to the matter, aid that the following,persOns be appointed a sub- committee to .confer with the Education Com- mittee thereon: The Chairman (Alderman James Evans), the -Deputy-chairman (Alderman Bur- ton), the Mayor, .Alderman Tinnion, and Coun- cillor Broadhurst.</p>
        <p>NEW ROAD THROUGH- VICTORIA PARK. At .a meeting of. the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee.. the .Borough Surveyor, sub- mitted a plan • showing the continuation of a road from Howley .Bridge across Victoria Park, • • west side of the bowling green. The plan was approved, and authority given for the work to be proceeded with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PROPOSED TRAMWAY WAITING-ROOM.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED TRAMWAY WAITING-ROOM.</p>
        <p>A sub-committee of the Sanitary Works Cotn- mittee has waited upon the Electricity and TraMways Committee with regard to the pro- posed public waiting-room and convenience at the tram terminus at Sankey. _ _</p>
        <p>It was resolved that the Sanitary Works Com- mittee be requested to obtain nlans and esti- mates for the further consideration of the com- mittee.</p>
        <p>A NURSE'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee has resolved thaL there be paid to the relatives of Nurse Mutroy, who died from an illness contracted in the execu- tion of her duties, the sum of £2O towards funeral and other expenses. It will be remembered that at the May meet- ing of the Council a proposal to grant the relatives. £lO was brought forward, but, the generally-expressed feeling of the members was in favour of doubling the amount,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>KNUTSFORD-BOAD. IMPBOVENIEN T.</head>
        <p>KNUTSFORD-BOAD. IMPBOVENIEN T.</p>
        <p>• An application; for the letting of the Mersey- Bedstead site, in Knutsford-road, was consid- ered by the Street Improvement Committee. . It was decided: that the site should not be sold until the widening of the 'road had been com- pleted.</p>
        <p>THE GOVERNMENT AND LOCAL TAXATION. The Borough Treasurer (Mr. John Fairhurst), in a report on the annual meeting of the Insti- tute of Municipal Treasurers and Accountants, recently held ~at Tunbridge Wells, states : Amongst the Matters introduced were the ever- increasing problems of Income-Tax depreciation and Imperial subvention in aid of local taNntion. With regard to the latter, the Council regret that they are unable to report any progress in re- gard to the long-delayed adjustrnenks of the financial relations between the State and, local authorities in respect of services which, though administered locally, are national in cliaralt-,w. The Departmental Committee, which was ap- pointed in April, 1911, have published the minutes of the evidence taken .by them up to the end of June, 1012, but they have not yet" issued any report embodying the conclusions at which they have arrived upon the 'Subject._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EXPERIMENTAL PAVING,</head>
        <p>EXPERIMENTAL PAVING,</p>
        <p>'The Chairman of the Paving and ..ew.vage Committee (Alderman Tinnion) reported tu•on the samples of paving they had • seen • miring their visit to the exhibition of the tioa.t Con, gress, and the various places where pavoqr, m as Jaid d6wn in London.</p>
        <p>It was resolved that Museum-street, from Arpley-street to Winmarleigh-street, be experi- mentally paved with Lithofall paving blocks.</p>
        <p>FIRE PRECAUTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Fire Engine Committee considered tbe question of the supply of water in and :,ieoui.d the, Markets in case of fire, and the matf.r wns defeitred to enable the Borough Survei-)r to consult the Water Engineer thereon. ' The Borough Surveyor submitted a letter from the Leyland Motor Company, as to the progress in the construction of the Motor Fire Engine.</p>
        <p>It was resolved that he be instructed to obtain information from them with regard to tyres.</p>
        <p>Warrington Market Price.</p>
        <p>Wheat in poor demand, not much being offered. Prices ruled from 4s. 4d .to 4s. 6d. per 701 b.</p>
        <p>Oats still hold late rates, viz., 2s. to 2s. 64-1 per 451 b. Wheat Straw, 375. per ton. Oat Straw, 355. per ton. XX Flour, 235. 3d. per 2401 b. Bran, 9id. per 201 b.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Brave Efforts to Rescue a Drowning Playmate. BANK HOLIDAY TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>Brave Efforts to Rescue a Drowning Playmate. BANK HOLIDAY TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>"It was a very brave action on the part of this boy to endeavour to save his companion. You -.know the danger attached to the death grip of a drowning person. This boy did his 'best."</p>
        <p>So said Mr. H. C. Yates, the Cheshire coroner, of a Warrington boy, named George Henry. Pugh, who made a gallant, but unsuccessful, attempt to • rescue his drowning companion from the Bridgewater Canal at Grappenhall on Monday. The victim of the fatality was Robert Slater, 11 years of age, or 25, Hamilton-street, War- rington, and the inquest was held at the Police Court, Stockton Heath, on Wednesday. Thomas Slater, the father, Said that his son could swim a little, but witness did .not know of his intention to go to the Canal on Monday. He was informed of the boy's death after the body had .been recovered from the water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WOULD-BE RESCUER'S STORY.</head>
        <p>WOULD-BE RESCUER'S STORY.</p>
        <p>George Henry Pugh, an intelligent boy of eleven, ..-residing at 29, Hamilton-street, War- rington, narrated how Slater, another boy, named Hughes, and witness, went for a walk on the afternoon of Bank Holiday. They went on, to the canal. at Grappenhall, and the water tempted them to have a bathe. Pugh said Ile went first into the canal, and had coine out just as Slater was ready to go in. Slider dived in three times, and then got in!) difficulties. "I. Saw;" continued Pugh, "that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>Photograph by Burrell.</p>
        <p>GEORGE HENRY PUGH, who made a gallant attempt to save his drowning friend. •</p>
        <p>he XN'tlS struggling, and I asked him if he was drowning, nut Slater did not answer me.</p>
        <p>"I jumped into, the canal again, and got hold of Slitter, lie did not make any attempt to swim., Or to save himself. He lay -still, and then sank. I was taken down with him, and had to leave go my hold. I brought him near to the side three -times, and each time he snatched away from me. The last time I hed to leave go my hold upon him Slater sank and did not riser again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WOMAN INTERVENES.</head>
        <p>A WOMAN INTERVENES.</p>
        <p>"I then got on the canal bank and I called for help. to a man And woman passing. I told the man there was a boy drowning in the canal.</p>
        <p>"The man started to take off his coat, as if to go into the water, when the Woman put her hand • on his shouldee'and would not allow the mail to go in. The man and woman then .went on, and-I shouted for help, and-somebody else came. This was the first time we had gone into the canal. The water was warm on the top, but .cold. below." Mr. Mclntyre, the foreman of the jury, asked how long it was after Slater had got into the water that he got into difficulties.. . Pugh thought it was about three minutes. Slater did notattempt to swim across the canal, as he was not an expert swimmer. John Hughes, nine year 'of age, living at 41, Hamilton-street, Warrington, said it was he who suggested that they should go into the .water.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Can you swim? Hughes: No, sir. But I went into the water first.' And cannot swim? Oh, I stood on:the side of the water. Slater got in next,. and started to swim. Then he dived several times and began to struggle. When I'heard he. was :drowning 1 got on the side, but could not get hold of him. I saw Pugh try to get him out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>LIFE-SAVING RECORD.</head>
        <p>A LIFE-SAVING RECORD.</p>
        <p>Stanley Starkey, engihe driver, said he Was feeding his.- fowls at Brook .flouse,.where he lives, when his sister drew his attention to the crowd on the canal bank. He went to .ascer- tain Ithe cause, and was told that a boy was in the water. He dived into the canal and found the body . at., the bottom. . With a little manceuvring he managed to get it out. Slater then seemed dead. .</p>
        <p>The Coroner: You have been successful on three previous occasions in saving life in drowning cases? _</p>
        <p>Starkey (modestly) : Yes, sir.</p>
        <p>_ The Coroner: I must congratulate you upon that record, and regret you were not successful on the fourth occasion.</p>
        <p>P.C.Crompton said he was called to the canal about six o'clockon Monday and went in com- pany with Inspector Breeze. The body had by Alen beet -recovered, by .Starkey. -Ile ,tried artificial respiration for • nearly half-an-hour, without result. Dr. Binns came soon after, 4nd.propounced- the boy dead.: It was against the Canal Company's rules to-bathe in the canal and there were notices warning bathers:. CORONER'S COMMENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Yates said that he did not think the death of Slater was due to cramp, but was rather the result of heart failure. .They had been told by the witnesses.that the water was cold. "A doctor has said," continued the Coroner, "that if post-mortem examinations were .made in the case of persons who were drowned, the majotity of the deaths would be found not to be from. drowning, but from heart failure, due to the action of cold water upon a heated ,body. This is what -seems to have occurred in this case.</p>
        <p>"With regard to the man and woman whom Pugh spoke, to, the man was prepared to make an attempt to rescue the boy, but his better half would not allow him to do so. Women are a little inclined that way." The. jury returned a-verdict that Slater met' with an accidental death, and one juryman expressed the hope .that the gallant actions .by Pugh and Starkey would- be brought to the attention of the officials of the Royal flumant Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>HOLIDAY TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>HOLIDAY TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>How the Trams and Motor 'Rises were Patronised.</p>
        <p>The ideal weather which prevailed during the Bank Holiday week-end resulted in a gratifying increase of traffic on the local tramways. The great advantage of the new motor 'buses was freely commented upon, and there was spirited competition for places on these vehicles, which are used as much for pleasure as convenience. The passengers carried on the various tram- way routes numbered : Route. Saturday. Sunday. Monday. Sankey •</p>
        <p>Latchiord Stockton H. 6,944 ... 3,819 ... 7,064 Cemetery Longford</p>
        <p>Total - 13,326 .- 21,281</p>
        <p>The returns for the motor 'buses were : Route. Saturday. Sunday. Monday. Gorsey-lane ... 2,846 ... 1,843 ... 3,092 Bewsey Circular 2,093 ... 1,676 ... 2,158</p>
        <p>• - - - Total</p>
        <p>The receipts on the trams were slightly less on Saturday than for the corresponding Satur- day last year, but there was an increase in the takings on Sunday and Monday.</p>
        <p>GARDEN FURNITURE, TENTS, HAMMOCKS, CHAIRS, &amp;C.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53 and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>The Coalminers of St. Helens and their</p>
        <p>Strike.</p>
        <p>IS THE SEASON OPPORTUNE?</p>
        <p>(BY "A WORKER.")</p>
        <p>The miners of St. Helens and Haydock are on Strike.. For weeks past the threat, of such a rupture has been in the air, but now it has materialised, and, as 1 write, there are indica- tiOns that the dispute will be fought to the bitter end, no matter how long it may take. The fact that the Miners' Federation are talk- ing about a grant of £ll,OOO for strike pay, with the promise of more later, indicates that they are ,determined to make this area the battleground upon which the non-unionist question will be fought and settled, so far as Lancashire is .concerned.</p>
        <p>A Menace to Unity.' • That the presence of the non-unionist in the mine is a menace to trade unionism is proved beyond doubt. It is surprising that such an individual can exist, when every miner knows what unity of purpose has done for him, both from the aspect of wages and improved' condi- tions. These' non-unionists have gained many distinct advantages as the result of the efforts of trade uniOn miners, and the non-unionists continue to enjoy these benefits, and, at the same time, .endanger the solidarity of the cause. Surely their conscience must prick, or are they without a conscience?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>The Wrong Time. -Every trade unionist must- wish the strikers success, .but the question .arises: is this the best season for a striße? The summer is the dull time in the 'coat trade, Consequently the employers can afford to allow the pits to re- main idle and not seek to interfere in • the dis- pute, as they would have done had tfie coal boom been at the winter height. The coal- masters elsewhere in Lahcashire solved the non-union question promptly when a strike seemed possible in thp busy season. The same would have happened at St. Helens had a more suitable season been chosen for the strike. By making the initial mistake of choosing a wrong' .time, the local miners appear to be in for a long and costly fight. A Weaving Case.</p>
        <p>• A case decided under the Workmen's Compen- sation Act in Warrington County Court on Thursday is of interest to workers, and more •ticularly to those engaged in the great cotton industry. The chief point, which we need con- sider, was that raised in regard to the promo- tion of employes in weaving sheds. A, girl, who had acted as a weaver's helper, was put on to work two looms, owing to a weaver being away ill. While thus engaged, the girl met with an accident, and lost the tip of the right-hand fore- finger. The point raised was: Was she entitled to receive compensation on the basis of a weaver's wage, or on the lower earnings as a helper, or tenter? Quite a learned discussion ensued upon the technicalities of the position; and, finally, the Judge decided against the girl's contention that she was a weaver, and she lost her case largely becauSe of that decision.</p>
        <p>Far-reaching Effect. The judgment will have a much wider effect than if it were a case which interested Warring- ton weavers alone. It will be quoted as a legal precedent .in other cases, which may arise, in the cotton trade, and it almost equally affects weavers of woollen and linen goods. It has cleared up, whether satisfactorily to the workers or not, a problem which has been long in dis- pute, and has laid down the point that the tem- porary promotion .of a helper or tenter to " sick " looms does not constitute that person a• permanent weaver. At a master's request such a person would have to go back to the duties of helper, and could not claim that a -temporary charge of looms has converted her or him into a permanent weaving operator. Such a decision, however, is not altogether sat- isfactory to the operatives, but the line has to be drawn somewhere, and Judge Stanger, in thiS case, seems to have drawn it as fairly as he possibly could.</p>
        <p>"Feathering Their Nests." It is unusual, to find 'Labour intelligence in such an aristocratic organ- as "The Times " ; for once, the oldlady of journalism gives us something which is fairly accurate. The sting, however, is found in the tail of the article on ."Feathering their Nests," and it consists of a table showing how 374 Labour men have accepted Government posts since 1906. These figures I do not dispute; but I 4kdo object to the paper giving no explanation of the true reason for these men accepting Government jobs, and leaving the public to believe that Labourists aro •nothing but self-seekers. The greater number of these appointments were under the Insur- ance Act, and Labourists were selected because they were the best qualified for the task, which is chiefly centred amongst the industrial classes, and Insurance Committees have more than once commented upon the abilities of Labour men for the work. It is not fail' of "The Times" to Make the attack it has. Of course, we could not expect anything else from that quarter. • Child Labour.</p>
        <p>Agitation. has long been in progress for the abolition of child half-time labour in the cotton mills of. Lancashire and woollen mills of York- shire. It is now getting a decided fillip. Worn an unexpected 'quarter. amending the tariffs upon textile goods, the American' Gov- ernment are determined to exclude goods from mills Where children are'employed. Naturally, this is not to the liking ofillie mill-owners, who exploit the children of•the working classes, and Chambers of Commerce are protesting against the American stipulation, but President Wilson is not likely to pay much attention to such pro- tests. Child labour is bad for the child, bad for the adult employes, and a disgrace to the much-vaunted British civilisation. That it can be done without has been proved beyond .7.is• pute, and if the Yankee tariff helps to end it, all good trade unionists should rejoice. Employ- ers do not send their children to work in their mills; then, why should the children of the workers be recOired to do so?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>COUNTRYSlDE—continued.</head>
        <p>COUNTRYSlDE—continued.</p>
        <p>was very enjoyable, and we arrived at half-past nine, hungry, but well satisfied with our morn- ing tramp.</p>
        <p>A Correspondent's Specimen. A. R. NEWTON, Pagnall, Bucks :—The hairy caterpillar sent for, identification is that of the Common Vapourer Moth (Orgyia antiqua). Thc caterpillar is very destructive, especially when it occurs in numbers, as it feeds on all kinds of trees and garden plants—it is equally at home on hawthorn, blackthorn, willow, lime, rose, and Oak. It, is very abundant in some dis- tricts; in Norwich in 1890 there was an epi- demic of the moth, and many gardens were entirely denuded of vegetation owing to its ravages. When the caterpillar has undergone all its moults and is "full-fed," it seeks out crevices in tree trunks and • walls, and 'there spins a cocoon, in which it changes into a hairy chrysalis. The male moth is, unlike the great majority of moths, a day-flier; the female is wingless, and never leaves the vicinity of its cocoon, and lays her eggs on the web. The moths hatch out a few at a time over a period of ten weeks, so that the moth, larva, iand pupa may be found all at the same time. The moth has been taken at sea twenty miles from land. The "brushes," or tufts of hairs have no special significance, so far as I know. All the larvte of the* Vapourer and Tussock Moths have these hirsute appendages. G. A. DUNLOP.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>t 77) s •</head>
        <p>t 77) s •</p>
        <p>_... a , .... '1 irt li k rq THE -4 IN 4 vonTR i sir-SIDE 11,1. II . it</p>
        <p>A Ni4tralist'.3 Ramble Amid the SAitude of Scottish Lochs.</p>
        <p>REMLNDERS OF OLD QUAY CA214./.</p>
        <p>A vivid description of a naturalist's ramble amid the solitude of the Scottish lochs is supplied by our contributor. .Mr. 4, 4. Dunlop. Encountering much that was of oxeptionalk interest to the student of wild life, Mr. Dun- lop's thoughts were also directed; by more familiar species, to the many expeditions-bet has made in the vicinity of V- old Ottiay Canal. near Warrington.</p>
        <p>morning—that ts, beAginrnaminbglebeinfort.heeseroy,ceioareilLis not one of my failings, but on this occasi was DO alternative. I Wanted to take a few photo- graphs of aquatic plants, and to attempt it in the middle of the day was certain failure, owing to the intense, hard light, which beats on the surface of the earth in these hot August days. The morning was delightfully cool, with elk mounting the sky in the east. for a good forty- five moderate bracing wind, blowing from the west, golf e o five minutes' walk along the valley on the other Aansdl an n iirenarhed early sunl The e side of the hill to the loch, which serves as the reservoir for one of the popular holiday resorts, the haze which hung over the -silver firth .pro- phesied another scorching hot.day. The loch, which is formed by the natural drainage of the surrounding hills, is about half-a-mile.long,-by a quarter of a mile 'wide. Approaching the wood at the near end of the loch, we found. it to be thr, roosting-OW of thousmids of noisy starling, and, presently, after much incessant preliminary Chatter, they rose in a black mass, circled round and round, for a minute or two, then, st,parating, into parties, flew away at -all points of the compass., ie went over the hills in different directions, others crossed the firth. The loch, nestling in the seclusion of the surrounding,hills, and further protected by numerous notice boards and a girdle of un- climbable barbed wire, is a sanctuary for wild life, as we very soon realised.</p>
        <p>ori there</p>
        <p>Son</p>
