      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Haunt of Solitude.</head>
        <p>A Haunt of Solitude.</p>
        <p>Tim NvhOle atinospnere and feeling was one of solitude. The only sounds were the piping o ' btirlds at the edge of thc water, and dthe the the water teaching their fly. chere is a small gullery on ound at the head of the loch, wagdtidnlsg o young how swamping ho‘v to ground consisting of a mixed colony of Common and Black-headed Gulls. The _gullson the water was a very lively one; I noted four species----the Conn-lion, Herring, Lesser Black_ backed, and Black-headed Gulls. There were about two dozen young ones quite unable to tly, whose gambolling about on the water, like kittens on land, was pretty -to watch. It is quite evident that by adopting the naturalist's habit of keeping Perfectly still and motionleSs„ we may see many of the . r - vild crea- tures at ther ease, so we : .. ccordingly and recline on the high banks hadle not . We for presently we had the as rlow alight at the water ploofngseit; o eg7tit u edge, ten yards aWaY, and begin to probe in the wet sand with its long, sensitive bill. That the bird is excessively wary in habit Gould bet seen by th little nervous upliftings of the head, - and the t‘s'itching of. the short tail, and it dseisecnolevderield:',urib; some long time before the bird movement on our part, - that we ~vere‘ not, after all, inanimate parts of the .landscape. On realising its mistake, the curlew- hurried- away With a Aluick 'Marin: With the exception of the curlew, the birds hereabouts seem 'wondethilly : birne ; feitdpg they have become so secure in the solitude and seclusion of their sanctuary that they. have. Inst all fear of man;</p>
        <p>company</p>
        <p>flume</p>
        <p>OUS C</p>
        <p>let a,</p>
        <p>for a time</p>
        <p>-cry of</p>
        <p>The guns certainly did not take muchinotiee o presence, and did not trouble to olie away our m vaoy on our approach, but gulls are fearless birds, and some venturesome individuals will almost snatch food out of your fingers at the steamer rails, as they follow in its wake up the firth. Several plovers came to drink near Qur feet as we sat, apparently v6ry little disturbed: by our presence. A Rominder of Home.</p>
        <p>A little further along the loch side we saw on investi- two small blade, objects swimming about amongst INiTd them vegetation, to, and closer be two newly-hatched found chicks of the Little Grebe, or Dabehick. know_ g we the ing from our experience nearer home, On the Old ,Quay Canal, that the parent birds coutd. not be for away, we stood still for quite a long time Without any sign of the birds, although several of them were swimming about in the mit., Je of the loch. It is the habit of the Little- te when disturbed, to submerse all its body, but neck and head. We then began search- ing amongst the water plants, and were del ited to find the nest, with one egg in It, slit ..varm. This was too good a chance to lo Of good subject for a photograph, so wft tur -‘1 our attention to the two chicks once Inc •, and, after waiting a few minutes, they oar:,, swimming in to the side again, and were easily captured and placed in the nest, where they crouched quite still till they had their "p --!rails took," when we restored them 'xi the ,vater. We were surprised and interested to - their method -of progression in the water. Th—, submerged all the body, save the head, no, and tail, and used the short, degenerate f!s paddles under water. We have heard[ th; . e,e old birdS 11Se their wings' " un . the 'water, but were astonished to tie tiny, helpless chicks; not many hours doug the, same.</p>
        <p>, Another subject for the camera presented itself further on. We. had the cainera_rigge_3. up, focussing a rich clump of ScirpUs, when, from the centre of the clump, scuttled a fine liar, . nearly upsetting the camera in his haste. He tLoped just for an instant for a closer look at the strange machine, and then made off at top spted, kicking- up his heels behind • him, not eye -1 staying for his portrait. It is hard to say who was the most astonished, the photographer or "Puss" ! I had an experi- ence scmewhat similar to this earlier in the. Week, at the Millport Marine Biological Station, wlim I .was photographing a dead porpoise. To e left of the spot where the porpoise lay ih there was a thick growth of Tansy, and from this, all the time I was preparing the camera, a rabbit had its head stuck out; watching the pro- ceedings. When I went across to investigate, the bunny disappeared, but I was told by the Superintendent of the station that, though a purely wild rabbit, it. is quite tame, and spends yn ii !!-; 1,!•!</p>
        <p>Lb Wi</p>
        <p>The PrettieA Siiiht of the Loch.</p>
        <p>The vegetation of this hillside loch is interest- ing in its types and its luxuriance. Of indivi- dual species, perhaps the prettiest sight of am loch is afforded by magnifieent c rs, cover- ing from twenty to thirty square yards each, of the Amphibious Persicaria. From the dis- tance, each group of the plant gives a beautiful, delicate rose madder coloured patch to the landscape. Very conspicuous, too, are the great beds of short, succulent, deep green Litorella, especially on the west side, giving the bank, .clown to the water edge, a soft, velvety appearance. Above the Litorella, on drier ground, are luxuriant masses of dub, rushes (Scirpus palustris). The vegetation at the south 6nd of the loch, where it is distinctly in the drainaga area, the swamp being dissected with dilehes, is of the typical marsh associa- tion, such as is found at Hatehmere, yellow iris, meadow sweet, marsh horse-tails, skull-cap, water-mint, lesc- rort, marsh bedstraw, greater bird's- , and bogbean pre- dom in&amp; ,iigher banks all rOund the loch, ;i \va3 tom the influence of the water, the veg,-1 the usual dry mea- dow and The walk home to</p>
