      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Itttershi ibt Thitor</head>
        <p>Itttershi ibt Thitor</p>
        <p>WELSH DISESTABLISHMENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. F. Rhodes Smith contends for the nationality of Wales, and fot an overwhelming majority in the "nation" in favour of disestab- lishing and disendowing her ancient Church— the first organised Christian Church in these isles. But he will not approve of his conten- tion being put to the test of a vote of the people of Wales on the question, unmixed with any other guestion . lie wants your °readers to consider that the -electors voted upon the Par- liament Bill, Home Rule, the People's Budget and Disestablishment (to say nothing of' other minor matters), but that the result of their vote was due to Disestablishment 'only! And that, in spite of the fact (which Mr. Smith seems to admit), little was said about Disestablish- ment by the-majority of the candidates, it being in evidence mainly through "the solemn warn- ing" of the Bishops.. I am sorry to note the evident fact that Mr. Smith looks upon this question wholly as a political question. It must be settled for poli- tical reasons and by political tactics, appar- ently—not upon .the principles of religion and justice. He objects even to a religious census; —notwithstanding his statement that "not one in ten of the people in Wales belonged to the Church." .• But not. even a 'religions •CensuA would most thoroughly test .the position. What - would do so is a referendum of nil the people .above a reasonable age—voters and non-voters. Churchpeople and - •-NonconfOrmists....x47 ,•-sttould be,-given the- chlince to say whether they -wish 'the- Church- disinembereti and. despoiled. .wheth4v, they. are. -against -any;_stinh ,act. Tltis te.st cannot be got from -Mr. Smith or liis- .--friencls. despite all his statements and figures. Uut I sutanit that if no Matters or pOlitical party erpediency prevented, but an honest deaire ex- _ isted-to arrive at facts in the most -thiciatway,. it would soon'l*-carried through. It is-a fact, and surely your correspondent must know it, that very many ,religious NonconformistS in Wales (and in England) 'decline to regard the 'legislative robbery of Church property through political spectacles and apart frona the ques- tion of religion generally. Mr. Smith does not deny, I note—because he -ebuld not—that but for the alliance between Protestant Nonconformist M.P.'s and Irish Roman Catholic M.P.'s, to wreck a Protestant Church.; the Welsh Bill would • have -been utterly lost in Committee. It is something new in British history when Methodists; Baptists • and Congregationalists combine with' Roman Catholics for such a reason: and from a purely religious point of view the spectacle is a distressing one. Party • exigencies do not mitigate,. the _sting of it, one ..iota. It is ft weak reply to suggest. that the huge , petitions which have been sent nil. to Parlia- •. merit against the Bill from Wales, are to any extent fictitious. Why is it that Mr. Smith's tremendous majority in \Vales do,not send any counter petitions at :all? Moreover, the. coun- try has never seen such demonstrations. both in Wales and England, as are -being Tield con- tinually against the proposed unjust and meanest of Bills. But one never hears of any- thing like a demonstration on the other side. -Mr. Smith's friends appear to be asfiamed of their Bill! "The tithe is a tax," your correspondent , . says. But Sir William Harcourt Said in the --flouSe of Commons on April 13, 1894, that "it , .was 'a: fallacy to -say- that tithes are a tax on theland; they are nothing of the'kind.". Lord- Jiistife -Lindley said the- saine in a letter -to the .14,isliPp of ,Norwicli fh 1912 But wfiy rob the . CluiTch tithe, .11.nd • all Ow.• the- lay 'cii.vrier.. -Ottttti4i takeep•his? And why talk about tithe when- the .tithe -.payers have-16 -pay- it? The Bill does not propose to abrogate this "tax." It simply proposes to . despoil the --Church of her part of it but make it payable to the -County Councils: Taffy will have to pay his. tithe just the same, nut not for. the pur- poses of religion, where most it is needed. The political extremist Nonconformists Will not touch a penny of the Church's money, Mr. Smith informs us. For once he is absolutely • correct! One does not wonder. But if the means for carrying on the Christian religion, given by pious ancestors hundreds of years . ago, is taken away from the Church. would it not show at least a sense of religious respon- sibility if Nonconformity preferred to accept it and Use it rather than permit funds given for sacred purposes being diverted to secular pur- poses? There is not much "restitution" in • 'proposals of the "Church Robb4y• Bill." and -one has yet-to hear any answer to the repeated challenge, both .in Parliament and out:" What good will the Bill do to a single soul?" ...„ CONWAY STIDSTON-BBOADBENT. .Lymm, August 6th, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>NATIONAL INSURANCE. The Proposal for a Commlsslon of</head>
        <p>NATIONAL INSURANCE. The Proposal for a Commlsslon of</p>
        <p>inqulry.</p>
        <p>Sir, —At the last meeting of the Executive Committee of the Employers' Parliamentary Association I was questioned as to 'whether Or not any definite, action had been taken as a result •of'my letter of June 3rd, in'which I sug- gested the appointment' by the Government 14 it special Commission of experienced men to inquire generally into the subject of. National sickness insurance, with a view to obtaining and utilising that, mass of information and experience which is available, not only of sick- ness insurance in this country, blit in Germany. with its compulsory scheme, in Denmark, with its voluntary scheme, of the world. as well as in other parts</p>
        <p>The letter referred to had an extensive circu- latiOn in the Press of the country. It was forwarded to the Members of both Houses of Parliament, and to the heads of the great assO- ciations, both of employers and workmen: and it was favourably commented upon in many directions._</p>
        <p>But, fIS the suggested Commission had not been appointed, the committee of the Employers' I)l7trihunentarY Association requested Me to urge the appointment, of the Conimission once more upon the Government. "lhe universal complaint against the National</p>
        <p>Insurance Act is that it was passed in haste. Already we 'lave an Amending Bill before Par- liament, generally regarded as indispensable, and also being pushed through without adequate consideration--necessarily so, ' because of the glut of work in Parliament.. Then I suAgest it is high time that National Insurance-ceased to be a party-political question—the time has come when the interests of employers and of work- people shall be considered,, regardless of poli- tical exigencies. What is needed now is to lift this national question clear out of the poli- tical arena, and to put it before a Special Com- mission, consisting of men .of all parties and of none, of men representing all classes interested, and who will be actuated by patriotic motives, and with the sole purpose of finding the best, most economical, most beneficial, and workable scheme possible. It has been suggested that probably every Session of Parliament for some time to come will have to consider a new Amending Insurance BM. Any such tinkering methods with so great a scheme, involving many millions of expenditure annually, is im- practicable and menacing to the common weal. Mr, A. C. Thompson,,the.President of the Indus- trial Assurance Approved Societies' Conference (representing over 5.000,000 insured persons) recently pointed out the danger of passing hasty amendments before experience of the present. scheme had developed. • What is wanted now is a body which will collect and dissect in- formation, and eventually place before the country impartial and considered pro- poSals, ' with a. view to a sound and satisfactory scheme being developed. The need is. great. The men required for the work are,. I have no doubt, ready and willing to do it.. ..Further delay will. involve the Government-J. in further; responsibility. The interests of Mei nation require it Recent develcipthents.'haire proyed that the forecasts .of the EmplOYers'. Par- liamentary Assbciation .have been 'verified to': the letter. This association, although only in, the second year of its existence, has.. amongst its. members, no fewer than 28 Federations and Associations, as well s. many' large employers in special industries who are not connected with the great Federations controlling the staple industries. lam hopeful that ere long all the employe'rs' organisations will co-operate, and act together as a united whole. This question of national insurance is, of course. one which affects equally employers and workpeople : and I hope it May be possible for capital and labour to act together in dealing with it.</p>
        <p>CHARLES W. MACARA. President Emnloyers' Parliamentary Association, Manchester, August 6th, 1913.</p>
        <p>I k I• •</p>
      </div>
