      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>BANK AMALGAMATION.</head>
        <p>BANK AMALGAMATION.</p>
        <p>Warrington and Runcorn Savings Banks Join Hands. MUTUAL ADVANTAGES.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and ,Runcorn Savings Banks are to be amalgamated, under the title of the Warrington, Runcorn and District Savings Bank. The decision, as far as the Warrington bank is concerned, was arrived at on Thursday, at a special general meeting of trustees and managers. Established in 1818, the Warrington Savings Bank is now one of the most successful and highly appreciated institutions in the town. The amount due to 16,439 depositors on November 20th last was £353,124 3s. 2d., and the turnover for the year was £203,203 6s. 10d., representing 33,604 transactions. The amount invested with the National Debt Commissioners was £354,588. 4s. '7(l, The • Runcorn branch, which was established in 1842, wag originally a branch of the Warring- ton Savings Bank. On November 20th last the amount due to 1,507 depositors was £41,803; with a turnover of over £29,000, representing 5,255 transactions. The amount invested with the National. Debt Commissioners was £41,520 178. 4d. REASONS FOR AMALGAMATING. According to a report of the Committee of Management of the Warrington Bank, the ques- tion of amalgamation was raised last year. The Runcorn Bank was desirous, of giving additional facilities to its depositors, by opening a special investment department, and this could only be done, by amalgamation.- with - a larger bank. Accordingly, the advisability of .. approaching Warrington or Chester was considered. _ The conclusion arrived at by the Management Com- mittee of the Runcorn Bank was that amalgama- tion with Warrington would best suit their busi- ness, .and the joint interests of the two banks, since there is a very large rural, area, which is common to both banks, also by reason of the fact .that -Widnes, with a 'population of over 40.000 inhabitants, has no Savings Bank. -It adjacent to the Runcorn _Bank. but Warrington, haying a,- soecial investment department. has a considerable number of its depositors resident in Widnes:. the amalgamation, on the ground .of community of interest, would, therefore, be advantageous to both banks. - The remit further stated that the Runcorn Bank is in every way progressive and its finan- cial position is sound. HOW THE WARRINGTON BANK WILL BENEFIT. Discussing the advantages of amalgamation from the Warrington standpoint, the report stated that the bank would benefit in so far as the soheme would protect And conserve its inter- ests in the Norton and Halton districts, and also at Widnes, where it has many depositors. Since the opening of the investment department (the report continued), many accounts have come from -Widnes by means of - transfers- from the . Post Office Savings Bank, mostly large accounts; these persons being desirous of obtaining the extra facilities for saving and the benefits to be obtained from Warrington's investment depart- ment, and it is quite reasonable to assume that had Runcorn been able to offer the same advan- tages for saving as Warrington, many of these accounts would have gone to Runcorn. RUNCORN BANK'S ADVANTAGES. From the Runcorn standpoint the report put the case thus: There can be no doubt but tnat amalgamation will bring a large increase of business to the Runcorn Bank, as is instanced by the annual reports of those banks where amalgamation has been carried out. If the amalgamation is with Warrington, then War- rington will benefit, as any increase of business winch Runcorn -obtains will also stand to the credit of the larger bank. As a unit of the amalgamated bank, Warrington would be con- ferring a great benefit on the inhabitants of Runcorn and district by providing them with increased facilities for saving,-thus carrying out the object for which the Savings Banks were established, viz.: the• encouragement of thrift. By amalgamation Warrington would become a much larger and more important bank. Chester, Originally a bank with, just over £200,000 in funds, has become, by reason of its amalgama- tions, one of the most important banks in the country, :and its fends now. exceed .half a mil, , lion. As soon- as the funds of a bank -total £500,000 it is then entitled to nominate a representative for election on the Trustee Savings 'Banks In- spection' Committee. Runcorn :will secure the co-operation and assistance a the larger' bank-, together with its supervision and control, and by extending the hours of opening ,give greater facilities to the inhabitants Of 4 very laige rural area. It would also be able to open a special ifrVestment.deptirt- thent, and obtain for its depositors the lull bene- fits which this department confers, viz.: an increase in the limit for deposits of £5OO at a more remunerative rate of interest, the bank also, greatly benefiting,- by the increased margin .. , of profit. ENLARGED OPPORTUNITIES. The Amalgamated Bank will benefit finan- cially by the increased profit which will accrue through the extra facilities given to Runcorn. Wherever amalgamation ' has taken place, in- sea business ha. resulted. The wider the area served, the greater the opportunity for in- creased usefulness. The amalgamation of banks under the encouragement and sanction of he Savings Banks Acts, by the union of interests gives enlarged opportunity for saving under national Jlanction, ,and ,thie, more largely fulfilg the foundation phrpoies intended in the 'estab- lishment of banks for saving. The committee stated that they had every confidence in recommending the adoption of the scheme of amalgamation, feeling sure that re- sults would be attained which would be most gratifying, and which would alld to the..greater dignity.:antl..inaportsance..of. the.3lank. Ybich had done so much in aiding and encouraging thrift in the town and district round Warrington. OUTLINE OF THE SCHEME. , The amalgamation scheme provides that the i 1 banks shall be under the full control of the Trustees and Committee of Management of the ' Amalgamated Bank. Runcorn is to have a representation of four trustees, who will, by virtue of their office, become. members of the Committee of Management. . The Amalgamated Bank, as a whole, is to be controlled by a General Board, consisting of all the trustees and managers of the Warrington Bank, and the four trustees of the Runcorn Bank: The remaininktrustees of the Runcorn Bank are to vacate their office as trustees, and, together with the presentmanagers of Runcorn,. he managers.on the General Board of the Amal- gamated Bank. . 'The actuary of. the Warrington Savings -Bank will be 'the-chief actuarY of tke Amalgamated Bank,- -and :he will visit in person,, or by deputy, ':the local offiees' from time to time, and, advise --the 'local actuary, *Then .neeeisary, upon all HT natters relating. to the business of the bank. he local actuary will keep all accounts of his bank.</p>
        <p>THE- SPECIAL MEETING. At the meeting on Thursday there were pre- sent: Mr. C. Jackson Holmes (presiding), the Rev. G. Vale-Owen, Councillor T. Birtles, Messrs. William Tinsley, J. Longland, A. Lister-Kaye, 'William-Richardson, J. W. Ilayward', William Clegg, J. Fulton-Smith, A. Down, A. S. Wright, and W. A. Proud (actuary). The CHAIRMAN expressed regret at the absence of Mr. William Long (chairman of the bank), who had another important engagement at Knutsford. Mr. Long had written cordial . supporting the amalgamation scheme, and ing it every success. Mr. Holmes went on to'-,1 refer to the success which had attended other bank amalgamations. To come to their own immediate district, Chester had absorbed several banks, one of which was Knutsford. At that time Knutsford was a decaying bank, but, under the amalgamation scheme, the deposits had in- creased to a very large amount, and it was now a successful branch. Even more striking success had attended the taking over, of the Grimsby Bank by Hull, the value of the step having been demonstrated by such an increase .of business that new premises had had to be built. . THE PROGRESS OF THE BANKS. From the records, it appeared that many years ago Warrington appointed managers for the Runcorn Bank; therefore, they were now practically returning to the old system, and reviving the old connection between the two banks. Runcorn was not a decaying bank; it was doing a profitable business, and, as far as the present financial year had gone, there had been an increase. Nor did he wish them for one moment to imagine that the Warrington Bank was a decaying institution, in any sense of the word. On the contrary, since 1896 there had been an increase in deposits of £160,000, t. 6,000 new accounts had been opened. THE RESOLUTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Holmes then Proposed the following reso- lution: "That subject to the consent of the National Debt Commissioners being 6btained thereto, on the recommendation of the Inspec- tion Committee, the Warrington Savings Bank be and hereby is amalgamated with the Run- corn Savings Bank, pursuant to. Section 5 of the Savings Banks Act, 1904, as .on .and from the 21st November, 1913, under the title of the Warrington Runcorn and District Savings Bank, -certified under the Act of 1863." Mr. W. RICHARDSON seconded, and supported all that the Chairman had said. He also men- tioned that Mr. Cameron. Secretary -of the Inspection Committee. warmly approved of the proposed amalgamation. , Several questions on points of .detail having been answered, the resolution was carried unanimously. Mr. Jackson Holmes was thanked for presid- ing, on the nrciposition of • Mr. FULTON-SMITH. seconded by Mr. lasTER-KAYE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>. k !A _, ' .__ _ , _T!3 ,I C I pirmi 1::4,</head>
        <p>. k !A _, ' .__ _ , _T!3 ,I C I pirmi 1::4,</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>ANGLER'S RECORD CATCH.—Mr. John Plevin, of Sandy-lane, Oughtrington, caught an exception- ally large fish, weighing four and a quarter pounds, in the River Bollin at Heatley on Thurs- day. This is reported to be the largest 4 caught in these waters for forty year. THE RECTOR'S HOLIDAY.—The holiday season now being at its height. many well-known Lymm people are participating in a change of air and scenery. Early last Monday morning the Rev. G. B. Thurston, M.A., left for a month's holiday in Switzerland, where, he is joining a party of old friends, his place as Rector of the Parish Church in the meantime being supplied by Mr. Maynard, who has successfully performed simi- lar duties in Lyinm on previous occasions. The Rev. R. P. Wethered,. M.A., .has just returned from his holiday.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE 'METHODIST ANNIVERSARY.—TIte anni- versary services of the . Breomedge Primitive Methodist Church were held on Sunday, when. the Rev. W. Dew, of Altrinchem, the newly- annointed second minister of the Lymm and Altrincham Circuit, preached inspiring sermons. SUNMY SCROOL TRIP.-IsText week promises to be Quite a time of exodus for Lymm people,. a number of Sunday School and other trips +0 such nlaces es Blackpool, New Brighton. and Rhyl having been arranged. The most ponnler one is thsf. which goes to Blackpool on Monday, a fit-44681 trsin having to he requisitioned to ,cope with the domaud for accommodation.</p>
        <p>A eirvrln.Nry.—Tassi- Sabirder ihe Prn'idloath Methodist Sunday Snit°ol held thei nnwil trip to Mr. P. Warloitton's farm a+ Lymin. wl:ere a most eninvoldc! time Was.spt hi n•ohiq fon walks, or rloyin- 'Ames in the fields. tngm-her w;th P 41,1 1; - wss snrredf thn scholsrs in '-he vs,rden. Jies-ss,,t_ 4;nn this ontince. the 7nurg -neßltlln of the nnd TxtrllTi SeliPnls 'had a ntatel,. whinh resnlf,,d p win fn-n ths lstter team. for which 31-.. W. A. Sonthall was ft," p-nrer. T.yrnin tean, were rate-ws”rls kirdly erfnr4-ain.d tn tns. Tt was the' n Priinifive Methodis4- Snn- (l.,vnl from T"Willn was nlsn an a visit to Mr. Warburton's the same afternoon.</p>
        <p>RECORD RANK HOLIDAY.—ACCOrding to the reports from the various towns round the Man- chester and South-West Lancashire districts, there was a record outflow, of holiday makers laSt-Week-end, and, in common With the Popular resorts, Lymm received a large percentage of the day excursion traffic. This was especially noticeable on Monday. when, right through the day, until four o'clock, each train brought its quota of trippers, whilst motorists, cyclists and. Pedestrians were to be found on every road lead- ing to the. village. The weather was ideal for an open-air holiday, and 'large crowds visited the lakes, Dingle, and Cross. Needless to say, the caterers, who are all devout sun-worshippers, were highly satisfied with the state of affairs, ' and it was not until close on midnight that any- thing in the resemblance of the usual quietude was resumed.</p>
        <p>A DANGEROUS CORNER: FARMER INJURED IN COL- usiox.—On Monday, Mr. Harry Taylor, butcher, of Preston Brook, was on a visit to Mr. Paul ' Peters, farmer, of Heatley, and, taking advan- tage of the nice weather, started out for a drive. As they approached Burford-lane Bridge t heard a cart coming from the opposite direction, and, thinking that they could pass under the bridge before the cart reached them, they drove ahead. As all who know this dangerous spot will remember, besides the bridge being very narrow, it is impossible to see anything approaching from the Broom Edge side, owing to the road turning at right angles as soon as it leaves the bridge. Unfortunately, Mr. Peters had not judged the.tittie for the approaching cart correctly, and,....before an accident could ' avoided, the tran beeame jammed between the cart and. footpath, then bawled emir, thre7' the occupants out. Mr. Peters sustained in- juries to his head, and was unconscious for some days, but is ribiv; we are-glad to say, progrei3sing favourably. Mr. Taylor escaped With a severe shaking. _ -_ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>Tux Scours.—The pleasure the Scouts attached to St. Thomas's Church found in forming part of the guard of honour on the Royal visit is described by the Vicar in the current issue of the Church Magazine.</p>
        <p>THE CURATE'S WEDDIXO.—It is Officially an- nounced that the prospective marriage of the Rev. E. W. Clarke, curate of St. Thomas's Church, Stockton Heath, will take place at Goole Parish Church, Yorkshire, on Tuqi.lay. 2nd September. The bride is Miss Gwladys Lyth, and she resides at Goole. A home-coming reception is being arranged for the couple upon their4return from ..the, „honeymoon. ,</p>
        <p>WINDOW CLEANER'S EMBEZZLEMENT. The story of how a window cleaner, while working in Stockton Heath, embezzled the money paid him by customers, was narrated in Warrington Police Court yesterday. Arthur E. Batea,Y694".ltT;_tafaxd-road, .was chargefl with embezzling two sums of Is. 6d: eieh, the money of his employers, the Warrington and District Window Cleaning Company, Ltd. Mr. J. Longland, who prosecuted, said about 16s. was altogether involved. Bates had re- ceived payment for the windows he had cleaned in Stockton Heath, but had not receipted it in his book, nor had he paid it to his employers. Bates pleaded guilty, and said it was his in- tention to pay the money back. He was very sorry for what he had done.</p>
        <p>Bates was bound over for twelve months and ordered to pay the costs.</p>
        <p>rIRE PRECAUTIONS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CORPORATION'S TERMS FOR. SERVICES OF BRIGADE. '</p>
        <p>At a recent meeting of the Fire Engine Com- mittee -of the Warrington Corporation, a letter from the Stockton Heath Parish Council, ask- ing for the 'terms upon which the Corporation would-be prepared to allow the Fire Brigade to attend outbreala of -fire in the parish of,Stock- ton Heath, was read. It was resolved that 'the 'Stockton Heath Parochial Committee be informed that the Warrington Fire Brigade's services will be available for outbreaks of fire in their parish on payment of the sum of £73 per annum, and (in conditions similar to those offered to Latch- ford Without.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>BRIDGEWATER CANAL B rums.</head>
        <p>BRIDGEWATER CANAL B rums.</p>
        <p>The practice of bathing in the Bridgewater Canal, without proper bathing costumes, has become common of late, and, as it is most objec- tionable, the residents of Stockton Heath are protesting. _ _</p>
        <p>. - - k; Mr. Mclntyre, who was foreman of the jury is. t the inquest.. held . upon., a Warrington boy, wned in the canal, referred to the matter at the, close of the Coroner's inquiry, held in the Steekton Heath Police Court, on Wednesday. He Said that although notices were posted about the canal prohibiting bathing, there was a greac deal of bathing, and the company never prose- cuted any of the bathers. The residents, continued Mr. Mclntyre, com- plained bitterly about men -and youtlia bathing in the canal at Stockton Heath. without costumes, and even without -towels to dry themselves. The method of drying they employed was to race along the canal bank naked, and this practice was most objectionable. The Coroner (Mr. Yates) said lie hoped some action would be taken to suppress the nuisance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>THETA WALL.</head>
        <p>THETA WALL.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH SPORTS.</p>
        <p>"Ye olde eyty of Thelwall " Sports were held at Massey Hall Park on Bank Holiday. The school children,, headed by a band, marched from the school to the park, and soon after the sports commenced. In the senior events the prize-winners , yvere : s _ _</p>
        <p>50 Yards Marble and Spoon Races for Ladies.- 1, May Walker; 2 Mrs. M. Kite. 56 Yards Whistling Race.-1, J. Swinton; 2, M. Kite.</p>
        <p>50 Yards Flat Race for Ladies.-1, Lillie Stubbs; 2, May Worthington; 3, Ada Richards. 100 Yards Wheelbarrow Race, Blindfolded.- 1, M. Kite. 50 Yards Threading Needle Race for Ladies.- 1, May Speakman: 2, Mrs. M. Kite. 100 Yards Flat Race for Men.-1, R. Ravens- croft; 2, M. Kite; 3, E. Sutton.</p>
        <p>' Ladies.—May Tug-of-War fC)r team.</p>
        <p>SPeakMaß'S</p>
        <p>• 50 Yards Sack Race.-1, J. Swinton; 2, E. Sut. ton; 3, J. Phenix. , Bolster and Bar Contest for 3len.-1, J. Swin ton; 2, M. Kite.</p>
        <p>Tug-of-War for Men.—T. Nicholson's' tea</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>PEN K ETH.</head>
        <p>PEN K ETH.</p>
        <p>HOLIDAY-MAKERS.—Many holiday-makers visited Penketh on Bank Holiday, the majority coming from Warrington and Widnes. The banks of the Mersey at Fiddler's Ferry was a popular resort. Bowlers discovered excellent sport on the beautiful green attached to the Ferry Inn, and crowds of spectators watched them.at play. The Ferry Sailing Club had a considerable num- ber of boats out, and as the tide was favourable, the boating parties had an enjoyable time. The golf links were largely patronised by •'gowfers" of both sex. The children found ample amuse- ment upon the sand at Fiddler's Ferry. STUDENT'S SUCCESS.—Mr. F. Davies, youngest son of Mr. Thomas 'Davies, Warrington-road, Penketh, has secured a notable success in the examinations of the Leathersellers' Technical College, London. He has won the first prize in the final grade of leather tanning, along with 1300 arid-silver medal. Earlier. in the yearithis clever student won the second prize in the col- lege examination in leather tanning, and gained three certificates in light leather tanning and in the chemistry of tanning classes. His friends have heartily congratulated him upon his splen- did achievement.</p>
        <p>SERVICE REWARDED.—TweIve. ,years of ardent and devoted service to St. 'Mary's Mission Church Sunday schools was rewarded with an interesting presentation on Sunday afternoon. Mr. J. Pennington, who has acted as secretary for that period, was the recipient of a beautiful Bible and Prayer-book, each bearing his</p>
        <p>initials stamped in gold letters, these beilig the gift of his co-workers at the Mission. Inserted in the Bible was an illuminated • inscription, which stated: "Presented to Mr. J. Pennington by the teaChers of St. Mary's Mission Sunday. school, as a token of esteem and regard for his faithful serVices,_ as secretary to the school, extending over twelve years." In the unavoid- able absence of the Vicar, the Rev. J. R. Janes, Mr. John Hall (superintendent of the Sunday schools) made the presentation, and spoke of the excellent work Mr. Pennington had accom- plished. The recipient of the gift suitably re- plied and expressed the fact that his removal to Farnworth, near Widnes, caused him to sever his connection with a work in which he had always found great pleasure and interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>"NOWHERE TO NOWHERE."</head>
        <p>"NOWHERE TO NOWHERE."</p>
        <p>The Peuketh Right-of-Way Dispute.</p>
        <p>NO ACTION TAKEN.</p>
        <p>The Penketh right-of-way dispute was again under discussion at the monthly meeting Of Warrington Rural Council on Wednesday. Mr. G. Mather presided, and there were also present Rev. A. M. Mitchell, Messrs.. W. Fur- ness, J. Ball, T. Robinson, P. Longton, P. C. Garnett, and J. Backhouse, with Mr. J. C. Sut- ton (clerk), Dr. Sephton (medical officer), Mr. B. S. Newcombe (surveyor), Mr. H. Burnage (assistant surveyor), and Mr. F. W. Kerfoot (inspector).</p>
        <p>THE CLERK'S VIEW. The CLERK, speaking with reference to the meeting between a deputation from the Coun- cil and Captain Hughes, with regard to the Penketh right-of-way dispute, said the conten= tion of Captain Hughes was that the path had not been a free and open one, that it was put there for a certain purpose of his own, and originally was a way from a certain point to the public-house, which belonged to the family. Captain Hughes admitted that people, were in the habit of using it, and that they had not been interrupted, except occasionally. Person- ally, his opinion was that Captain Hughes had no sufficient case against their evidence. They had a strong point in the fact that the path had been in free use by the people for 80 years —beyond' the memory, of man. Mr. GARNETT asked if the matter were taken into court by the District Council, who would pay the costs? The CLERK said if they lost, the district would have to find the costs. _ -</p>
        <p>The Rev:. A. M. Myrenzm asked .whether there was any set number, of years that gave a right to the users of path? • .- The CLHRIC 'replied -that- there was not—it de- pended on other circumstances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>"NOWHERE TO: NOWHERE."_</head>
        <p>"NOWHERE TO: NOWHERE."_</p>
        <p>Mr. FrlttiEfiS said the impression be had formed was that the footpath was really not worth incurring litigation or any considerable expense in attempting to maintain the right. In his opinion the road led practically. "from nowhere to nowhere," as it did not tap a public footpath at one end or the other. They should not waste their time over the matter. The Parish Council should give them more informa- tion as to the right of use.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. BACXHOTTSE, it was decided that no action be taken in the matter at the present time, and that the Parish Coun- cil supply further evidence. They would then reconsider the matter.</p>
        <p>The CLERK also explained that there would be nothing to nrevent the Parish Council ap- proaching the County Council about the matter. ,</p>
        <p>WAGES INCRE A SEP.</p>
        <p>The workmen having petitioned the Council for an increase of wages it was decided that the men now in receint of 21s. should be mid 235. per weelr: five of the better paid men to receive an additional 2e. 6d. per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN WEDDING GIFT.—An interesting cere- mony marked the monthly meeting of the Wes- leyan Sunday School Council, held on Tuesday evening. This consisted of the presentation of a wedding gift 'xi Mr. and Mrs. Walter Higham, who have been closely associated with the Sunday school, where both are teachers. Mr. Roscoller made the presentation on behalf of the officers and teachers, and Mr. Higham ex- pressed his and his wife's thanks for the gift, which consisted of a beautiful fireplace suite, in beaten copper work.</p>
        <p>'H</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>r.-__...... WHAT IS LOCAL OPTION?</head>
        <p>r.-__...... WHAT IS LOCAL OPTION?</p>
        <p>am greatly surprised to find that even a leading teetotal advocate 'like Mr. Leslie does not clearly understand the local option pro- posals of the Licensing Act of 1908. Clause 2 of that Bill proposed to give the electors power to vote for "no licence" only. On the requisition of not less than One-tenth of the electors of an area a poll is to be taken on a "prohibitory resolution " ; if the resolution is carried by a majority, no new licence can be granted; while, on the other hand, if the reso- lution is defeated, • the majority against it can- not demand a new licence as of right. The one-sidedness of the proposals is very clearly and fully set forth in a book.on the Licensing Bill by two eminent lawyers, Messrs. F. E. Smith and E. E. Williams, 'to the study of which I would commend Mr. Leslie. _ _ -</p>
        <p>Further. it is in accordance with the principles of the U.K.A. :that a majority of etectort- ir fa ow of additional licences Shall- rerhain POwerleSs.. 'l"tiio, organisation ekiSts for Vie 'total suppression and prohibition of ,the liquor traffic, and all the temperance measures it has advocated and supported, known as Permissive Bills, Local Option Bills, and Local Veto Bills, have .been distinguished by this ofte-sidedness and 'undemocratic feature. _</p>
        <p>. Perhaps Mr. Leslie will cite the particular section. or sections, in Clause 2 of the Bill, to prove his . statement that I wrote . what. vas untrue, and wrote without knowledge. If he cannot, I know he will withdraw and apologise. EDW. TUCKER. August 6th, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WEDDING OF LORD ROOM/WAGE AND MISS SASSOON.</head>
        <p>WEDDING OF LORD ROOM/WAGE AND MISS SASSOON.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Rocksavage, eldest eon of the Marquis and Marchioness of Cholmondeley, end Miss Sybil Sassoon only daughter of the late Sir Edward &amp;moon, were married on Wednesday at the registry office of St. George's, Hanover-square, London, in Princes- row, Buckinginsm Palace-road. The Marquis of Chol.isondeley and Sir Philip Bassoon, MP., brother of- the bride, were among the very small group of close relations present. The bride -wore a helio- trope dress, with a "Dolly Varden hat and a brown fur; the bridegroom a navy blue loungesuit and a dark grey plush Homburg hat. 'The engagement was announced only on July 15;12 last, and it was generally expected that the weddinL would he, owing to the difference in religious faith. a quiet civil one, though no date had Wen publicly announced. The Earl of Rocksavage, who is 30 years of age, was formerly a lieutenant in the 9th Lancers and an A.D.C. to the Earl of Minto when the latter was Viceroy of India. He served in the South African War in 1901. He is an enthusiastic polo player, and very fond of both hunting and shooting. The bride is nineteen years of age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Farm Fire at Rixton.</head>
        <p>Farm Fire at Rixton.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Fire Brigade was engaged for upwards of ten hours on Saturday at a. fire on the premises of Mrs. Lawless, Milverton ,Farm, Rixton. Information of the outbreak was re- ceived at 10.35 a.m., and the brigade turned out, in charge ,of Sergeant Monks. On arrival, it was found that a Dutch barn of four bays, con- taining hay and pressed straw, and an adjoining haystack, were in flames. A fair •supply of water was obtained from a pit, 350 yards away, but it was ten o'clock at night before the fire was extinguished. -The barn and stack were totally destroyed, but another stack, of 50 tons, was saved. The fire is supposed to have been caused by a spark from a traction engine at work on the farm. The damage, which is fully covered by insurance, is estimated at between £3OO and .RiMO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>PETTY THEFTS.</p>
        <p>Richard li,enwright, sn old offender, was sent to gaol for One month on a charge\of stealing a quantity of rope from the flat Hilda, by the Widnes inagistrates on Monday. Anna Phillips,. of Farnworth. Widnes, was fined .21 for stealing several articles of clothing from the Widnes Market.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>BURIED IN IRON.</head>
        <p>BURIED IN IRON.</p>
        <p>Through the breaking of a lift chain in the Delph Bridge Foundry on. Tuesday, a quantity upon Edward Hulf, labourer, Light- Heath-road, and inflicted severe in- of iron felil f hen the man w got out it was found that his skull had been fractured and his body injured. He died soon after in the hos- i ouortie-cso. urtw, as pital.</p>
        <p>TEN MINUTES' COUNCIL MEETING. The monthly meeting of the Runcorn Urban Council on Tuesday lasted about ten minutes. The Medical Officer's report showed that 15 births had been registered during compared with 15 deaths and 49 deaths and 36 the corresponding .period of last mr. Gibbs said it was very gratifying to see btheireatri. i m y births. for an increase in the circulation of books at the library.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The half-yearly meeting of the Runcorn, and widues, Industrial ,Co-operative Society ~was held on Tuesday evening, at Runcorn. - The statement of accounts showed that the sales for the past six months amounted to the. record • total of £123,125, an advance of £4,166 on the takings for the previous half-year. The corn- milt-tee, after Pr"i"ng for share inteteSt and r.depreciation, allocated .t,15,104. for dividend, whielf _.vvorked-out -,at-2s. Id. in the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>rT,OSVER SHOW AND SPORTS.</head>
        <p>rT,OSVER SHOW AND SPORTS.</p>
        <p>The annual.,flower 1-A7:ow and -sports of the -Ilighfield Tannery Recreation Club, Runcorn, took place on Saturday. The flower and vege- table show was held in the Institute in Canal- street. and the sports were conducted on the works' recreation grounds. Mr. R. Hannagan judged at the show. The awards in the garden competition were garden, jtt.oßna;te!oHwe.rGaibnbdonvsef: je-. as follow: Large vegetable, J. Wellings, W. Miller, G. Marsh: large flowers, W. Pendie. bum T. fßr root; table, J. Rawlinson, R. Lewis, G. Goulden, W. Brown, IT. Miss Posnett (daughter of Mr. R. H. Posnett, Jitaohwnisionnso;n. chairman of the Runcorn Urban Council) pre- sented the prizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>ST. HELENS TOWN HALL FIRE.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS TOWN HALL FIRE.</p>
        <p>There was a lively discussion at St. Helens Council meeting on Wednesday over the report upon -ther recent-frre at the Town Hall. - A com- mittee reported that the brigade had done everything to deal with the fire. It was alleged, however, that the fire engine was not of suffi- cient power to combat large fires, and W4IB not equal to that owned by Messrs. Pilkington. Alderman Forster said this was the first he had heard of the comparative power of the en- gines used. Mr. Forshaw : Powerlessness. Alderman Bishop: But it is a very serious matter if our engine, for which we have paid , such a large amount of money, is so much in- ' ferior to tloiautghotf tMoesshrsay.eprieltionttgtsono.n Ivareally s Pilkington said that at the points_swine:romivat ka they had several large warehouses, and lowing ;an. mngizie -they specified fora power _engine, whereas ..the usual- type was a 4-50-power-jflS., . Mr. Rad-said the subject was being treated with far too mueh levity, when it wra; sci serious to'the town. —He did-not wish to cast any reflec- tion upon the'ehief Constable or anyone. but he would move that the minute go back, le that the Council could have a detailed report on the whole matter. He believed the time had come when they should separate the brigade from the police force and have a separate per- son in full charge. Mr. Glynn seconded, and said that four years ago there was a terrible row in the Council over " sacking." -the old brigade and the super- intendent to make room for policemen. He questioned the experience of the brigade, and said that the Watch Committee and the fire brigade were the` laughing stock of the town. Mr. Forshaw complained that the matter had not been gone into ,as extensively and ade- quately as it ought. Alderman Dixon-Nuttall said he was confi- dent that with calm weather the engines of the Corporation would have done all that was ex- pected in tackling the fire. Six only voted for the amendment, and the minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE SCHOOL 'STRIKE.. A strike of School children and their parents. is threatened at. Haslington,. near :Crewe, and as evidence of. their ,earnestness in the matter the children and -their fathers and mothers marched in procession through the village on Wednesday. The County Council have built a new school .and it is said that when they appointed Mr. J. owen,as,mastar.of the temporary school they promised him the same position in the new one. Now the local sub-committee have asked him to resign, so that a new man may be appointed. Parents and pupils alike resent this, and they declare that unless the committee redeem their promise, not a single child shall enter the new school. The opening, ceremony took place on Wednesday, and at the same time the protest procession was marching round the village. T affixed to the gates of the school a large banner hearing the words, "We want justice for Mr. Owen," and then surrounded the building and sang "He's a jolly good fellow.".</p>
      </div>
