        <p>p CREWE DIVISION• -/, PARTIES. 1 •N' t 41,ve 0- ' 1,4 AND LABOL , Vhel,_()44,, ruL litesday Ilitt;t11041. 4ticel lei! ,between re Preseill'atttirned front night there _.NNI:-eass of ftuirie- ;t),S) Jill,. ZI)(111,!-•,.partief; .(. being pi,"(NtAftr til,.-'1,3 .wychout ait I°three-cornered t4.i 14 'q the `" °ldanee of " trtry election. (ad our be clltiv,a etu,A, wloerxt. Parltamen,. Ivi (41(1 eaeh' 11e11. li'tnau Part.Y. There was. an independ- s again between four represen- itttllls 't 1)1, 1.---Nll% J. W. Deakin. of - mtative was th,Ntehie°4 UP Without any definite result N.orth- Vitßii hdh inio, '‘ Press "Pre" -- that, Itlsi ..° 4t -124'd by Mr. De.akin I,lh 'qloil tielnent had been arrived at, this_ It:, t°; t 4 were 'All -' I _ (!citif I going on. Tlitiedupeit•oecdeel9o- ii(ll),ll,(lliN et'ence had been but "to further 11,,,q,,,,,,..tiEtd , Dossible waY, l. . at ti'h'i i fLO tieor) arran,ed He was not cios'eciallYthing further, i on- but the matter ' all.d negotiations would )e c</p>
        <p>carved records to puzzle and delight the antiquary. The gate post we have referred to was doubtless the monumental stone of a Scandinavian chief, and many other such memorials are built into fences and the walls of farmsteads. But these are being gathered into the "Cross House" at Maughold, and the collection will soon be a most valuable one.</p>
        <p>MONA'S ANTIQUARIAN TREASURES. It ought to be said for' the advantage of the visitor to the Isle of Man that some of the finest examples of Runes and Runic Crosses are to be found in Mona. And they are of the deepest interest. For these carved stones belong to an age when the rock was the only book by which the primitive author could present his ideas or reveal his art, and the carvings give to ua the only glimpse we have of the beliefs as well as the habits and modes of life of this remote past. Yet another important point of interest is that t&amp;y are the earliest Christian memorials our land can show.</p>
        <p>It is generally agreed that the Runic Stones are memorials of the dead. Some of them are certainly shown to be such by the inscriptions, and the fact that so many of them are in the form of a cross carries the same suggestion. But many are carved with domestic scenes and have birds, animals, musical instruments, implements and weapons, and may have had other meanings and uses. The earliest inscriptions upon the Manx stones are carved in characters known as Ogams, and may be dated in the fifth and sixth centuries. But the origin of the Ogam alphabet is difficult to trace, and questions of date will have to be left, for the present, to the experts. The Scandinavian cuttings are in Runes, and as the Runic alphabet consisted almost entirely of straight lines the sculptor's task was not a difficult one. The inscriptions are usually upon the edges of the stone, and though they look very formidable, their decipherment is not difficult to those who can read the Runic alphabet. The words are the Norse vernacular, the letters are Runes.</p>
        <p>WHERE THE STONES MAY BE FOUND.</p>
        <p>And now let us direct the student to the resting-places of the Manx Runic Stones. There is, as we have said, a collection at Maughold Churchyard. These are stones brought from various corners of the Island, rescued from ignoble uses, and now happily housed in a romantic and beautiful village. Kirk Braddon has one or two, which adorn the churchyard, and of which we are able to give an illustration. Jurby, Rushen and Arbory have examples, the two last being inscribed in Ogam characters.</p>
        <p>But the most beautiful of all are at Kirk MiFhael, and these merit special notice. A very fine stone is now placed in the shelter of the porch of the church. It has a cross and circle, the spaces between the cross and circle being pierced. - -</p>
        <p>On one side the cr-os's is covered with an elaborate decoration in the form of a plait, which breaks into an irregular interlacing. On either side of the shaft is sculptured a curious dragon-headed figure. Both dragons have a hind foot and tail, which terminate in a figure-eight knot. Another good Runic Cross is near to the entrance gate of the church and is adorned with a ring and chain design. Its inscription is one of the clearest and best cut that has survived to us and reads:</p>
        <p>MAIL: BRIKTI : SUNR : ApAKANS SNlip RAISTI: KRUS : p ANO: FUR; SALW : SINA : SIN BRUKUIN KANT : KIRpl : p ANO : AUK: IMAUN Mael Bride, son of Athakan the smith, erected this cross for his own soul and that of his brother's wile. Gant made this and all in Man.</p>
        <p>The reference to the sculptor and the claim that he had made "all in Man" is interesting. Gant was doubtless a skilled craftsman to whom fell the whole of the work of his day. -</p>
        <p>And after -twelve hundred years cr</p>
        <p>story of the Cross. How curious to read upon a Manx stone " Son of the Druid !" There is also the interest of taking note of the animals with which the Vikings were familiar. Upon an Andreas Cross there are well drawn figures of the cock and hen, the dog and boar, the ox, wolf, stag, and serpent, with the inscription:</p>
        <p>Sandulf the Black erected :this Cross</p>
        <p>to the memory of Arinbiorg his wif-e:</p>
        <p>Other stones show the calf, goat, bull, ram, bear, wolf, horse and hound. And one quaint cutting is of an otter with a salmon in its mouth.</p>
        <p>Two other slabs of a different kind may be mentioned as giving a tiny sidelight upon a dim historic past.</p>
        <p>_ At Maughold are two flat fragments of stone inscribed with Runes :</p>
        <p>Christ. Malachi and Patrick . • • But of all the sheep is John the priest of Cornadale.</p>
        <p>This clearly is an invocation to Christ and to the Irish Saints from John the priest on behalf of his flock in Cornadale. The letters are cut as though with a knife, and on another he has written:</p>
        <p>John the priest out these runes.</p>
        <p>By how many centuries John is removed from us it is impossible to guess, but he feels curiously close and very human as we touch h:s scratchings upon the stone. Much more might be written upon the antiquities of the little Island, but we have only set ourselves to give some words of general guidance, and this purpose is, per- haps, now fulfilled. A. M.</p>
        <p>th, Al.' hy ''la)</p>
