      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <p>. . •. • _ _ .</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 9, 1913.</p>
        <p>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>THE FROLICS OF THE ROOK.</p>
        <p>The rook has been the subject of much interesting writing, but few have described the quaint and attractive bird more effectively than Mr. Alexander Urquhart in his newly-published volume "Odd hours with Nature," which is published by Messrs. Fisher Unwin at ss. We quote an interesting passage : "It is no doubt because he wears a black coat that the rook is reputed to take sober. views of life. In truth there is no more frolicsome creature living, and the business of getting a rookery a-going is enlivened with fun and joke throughout. The repair- ing of old nests and the building of new ones goes on very slowly, and in explana- tion of this it has been said that the rooks spend more time in stealing sticks from one another than in getting sticks for them- selves. It would be idle to deny that they do steal from one another, but when they do so it is probable that they do it to gratify their sense of humour rather ' than in the spirit, of larceny. Often they do no more than pull out the carefully adjusted twigs and leave them on the top of the nest; and those members of the com- munity whose own nests ate completed up to the lining stage, and who are, therefore, in no need of stieks, are not one whit less active in this sort of sport than the rest. In my rookery the practical jokers were numerous and determined, and their favourite joke was not to steal a stick,-but to pull it out, drop it to the ground, and next moment affect to be absorbed in the labours of plumage preening. Then they are all prodigious talkers, and while the renovation went on they conversed without ceasing all the time, criticising .one another's work and engaging in sentimental interludes. Twice the communal sense asserted itself. In one case a couple, presumaL'y young, com- menced to build a new nest at a consider- ably higher altitude than the general level of the rookery. They worked in peace for a day, but on the second day an Act of Parliament was invoked against the aspirants and their nest was pulled to bits. If a pair of rooks care to build at a lower level than prevails they are permitted to go on; the discomfort will be theirs. But if they try to get unduly up in the world, it is quite a different affair. Then the public interest comes in. The other pair which outraged the law did it by attempting to build in the top of an ash a hundred yards away from the group of elms favoured by the community. They were punished for their aloofness by having their work wrecked after they had laboured on it for three days. As all these leisurely repairing operations are carried out in the mornings, and the greater part of each day is spent in the fields, two or three weeks pass before the rookery is actually a going concern ; but in the last week of March black tails will become visible sticking over the edges of many of the nests, evidence that the labours of incubation have begun.</p>
        <p>It is during the sitting-time that the rook shows himself at his beat—and also, alas! at his worst—as a family man. Some observers of rooks have denied that they ever enter into the bonds of matri- mony in the strict sense of the term. Each male undoubtedly does undertake the duty of providing food for one wife and family, but this, it is alleged, is merely an apportionment of communal labour, and does not carry with it monogamist obligations. The indictment is unproved. What is true is that the rook is a great and unabashed flirt. During April he labours hard in the fields to feed himself and collect enough to satisfy the appetite of the sitting, lady in the tree-top. But when he has fed her he thinks nothing of hopping on to the edge of another nest and breathing soft nothings to its occupant. In rine cases out of ten the matron will receive him• with asperity, but there is generally la-. tenth case in which the conduct of the tempted fowl will not bear investigation. And the curious thing is that the legitimate spouse of the flirt may be eying him all the time, but she retains an unconcerned placidity. "It has often been remarked as puzzling that the number of rooks in a rookery never corresponds with the number of nests. In the case I write about there are twenty-three nests—three, however, untenanted--and I have been able to count 1 forty-nine birds. There are thus nine birds which are either bachelors, spinsters, widows or widowers. Probably they are bachelors and widows. The rooks that are shot in springtime by farmers who suspect depredations on their autumn-sown wheat are generally male birds, for after rookery business has got well under the way the 1 females go little to the fields. It is likely, therefore, that the rookery contains some widows. But there are bachelors, too. Among most birds a proportion ot the one-year-olds fight shy of family responsibilities, and rooks are no exception to the rule. The social instinct compels those unattached to join the rookery, and their influence, it is to be feared, is uplifting by no manner of means."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>CONGESTION OF TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>A CONGESTION OF TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Wullie Dalziel, the old shepherd, had retired at last from active service, after spending the whole of his life in a little cottage away at the back of the hills, a full three miles, as the crow flies, from the nearest road- He moved into a house near the head of the valley above Crashie Howe, situated on the side of the lonely road. But Wullie was not quite happy. - He was distracted. He was worried by the traffic. He told a friend that there was no peace in this bit for an auld body. "Three bicycles and a cairt in the same day! Man, it's a fair toon ! "—" Manchester Guardian" Miscellany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>WELSH VALHALLA.</head>
        <p>A WELSH VALHALLA.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. A. Thomas. a former Liberal member for Cardiff, offeied to bear the cost of placing in the Cardiff City Hall statues of the ten most eminent Welsh men or Welsh women in the history of 'Wales previous to the reign of Queen Victoria. and a prize was offered to the compiler of the best list. The judges (Sir T. Marchant Williams, Mr. W. Llewelyn Williams, M.P., and Professor Powell) had 354 lists sent in, and they unanimously agreed on the following ten Welshmen as being the most suitable subjects for the proposed statues : Dafvdci ab G-wilym.</p>
        <p>• St. David (Dewi Sant) Gerald the Welshman (Giraldus</p>
        <p>Cambrensis). Owen Glyndwr. Henry VII.</p>
        <p>Howel the Good (Howel Dda). Prince Llewelyn, the last Prince o Wales.</p>
        <p>Bishop Morgan, the translator of the</p>
        <p>Welsh Bible. Sir Thomas Pieton.</p>
        <p>William Williams, of Pantycelyn, the Welsh hymn-writer. Not one of the competitors named more than eight of these names, but seven named eight of them, and the prize was divided among the seven. The statues will be executed in Serravezza marble,and the cost is estimated at £1.0,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>CHILDHOOD'S ROMANCE.</head>
        <p>CHILDHOOD'S ROMANCE.</p>
        <p>Legendary Lore and Nursery Rhymes Awaken</p>
        <p>Curious Fancies.</p>
        <p>THE GROWTH OF THE CHILD MIND.</p>
        <p>BY S. JACKSON COLEMAN.</p>
        <p>In this article, specially contributed to the " Examiner," Mr. S. Jackson Coleman, recalls the legends and fables of childhcod, and their curious origin. He also enlarges upon the growth of the child-mind, and suggests many interesting tests by which the budding intellec- tual powers may be judged. Mr. Coleman Jackson is the author of " Our Friendly Societies," "The Psychology cf the Child," and " Easter: In Many Ages and Many Climes."</p>
        <p>" Behold the child, by Nature's kindly law,</p>
        <p>we hardly ever think of them without thinking of him, in the same manner as we hardly ever think of English drama with- out associating in our thoughts the name of Shakespeare. Even iEsop himself, how- ever, does not appear to have invented the majority of his fables. He simply drew upon the old stock and presented old tales in a new dress.</p>
        <p>Pleased with a rattle, tickled with a straw.—Pope.</p>
        <p>One of the most striking and significant contrasts between ancient and modern thought is the difference of attitude towards the past and the future which characterises those periods. While the ancients ever looked backward with wist- ful longing to a golden age, set in the mists of a legendary past, the moderns have reversed the process, and yearn towards a glowing future, confidently entrusting the realisation of their ideals to the time which is to come. This change of thought has brought with it a corresponding change of action and different modes of social life. The work of faith is now no longer to glorify the past, but rather cheerfully to benefit the present and take an enlightened care for the future. There seems, indeed, to be a more progressive spirit prevalent in regard to the training and supervision of children and the legislation of the past decade has left a deep imprint in the minds of the future generation. There is a very hackneyed couplet of Longfellow's which says that we may "departing, leave behind us footprints on the sands of time." This is, no doubt, a popular line, but we are nowadays hardly content to leave foot- prints on the sand that would be washed out by the next tide. We seem to think it a more profitable occupation to leave, not footprints, but a memorial which will last from generation to generation, and represent a certain amount of self-sacrifice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>REALM OF FANCY.</head>
        <p>THE REALM OF FANCY.</p>
        <p>Whatever gives us an opening into the mind of a child cannot be otherwise than interesting. It is pleasant and useful, too, to get a glimpse of the beautiful soul— almost as it comes from Providence— untarnished by misguided education or the evil influences of society. For—let it be clearly understood—the favourite traits of child-life are a far clearer indication of natural tastes than those of older life. We ' are seldom aware as to our own likings and aversions, as so many are merely the ' artificial product of our respective environ- ments or the possible result of casual pre- possession or antipathy. Indeed, we may become so corrupted as to love the glitter of the showy ornament more than the simple beauty of our natural surroundings ; we can even learn to assign stupidity as profound wisdom, obscurity as originality and raving extravagance as the utterances of an oracle. But the partialities and aver- sions of childhood are just as Nature pro- vided for them. All life to them is as the divining rod of Aaron, blooming ever afresh with magic flowers. The trans- forming touch of the magic wand of young Fancy has something of crude Nature poetry in it. This is abundantly illus- trated in what might be called childish metaphors, by which they try to describe what is new and strange. A little boy of 19 months peering into his mother's spectacles, will describe them as," Leetle windows." In this effort to get at home with strange ob-tiects, resort is often made to strange conceits, as when the stars are described as "cinders from God's stove," and butterflies as pansies flying."</p>
        <p>THE GROWTH OF THE INTELLECT.</p>
        <p>Almost the earliest growth of intellect, after the first eager curiosity of things has subsided, is imaginative. It is now that the wild stories of romance are a deep delight and people the solitude of life. Then the fitful firelight hour, with its dancing shadows blending in weird har- mony with the wonders of some tale of fairyland, is the sweetest hour of the day. The child lives in a world of imagination. There is nothing that is not endowed with nothing that has not gained an articu- late speech. Now, when darkness closes round the cot, or the dim night-light shakes uncertainty through the air, the black walls are covered with friezes of living figures that pass in endless succession before the eye. When sleep at length arrives the flushed face and nervous move- ment of the lids speak of some struggle or triumph in the sleeper's dream. The child lives in a realm of imagination. Fairy tales make their debut with all their inter- esting enchantments. Perhaps you would not think, for instance, that such verses as those in which Mistress Mary asked," How does your garden grow ? " were of a character very quickening to the imagina- tion. But did she not earn a reputation of a very queer child by holding conversa- tions of such a matter with this obscure horticulturist ? This initial stage, how- ever, is soon succeeded by one which presents a strange contrast, inasmuch as the imaginative powers then show a marked decline. In the place of unquestioning reception there arises a certain measure of inquiry. Jack the Giantailler is no longer the deeply serious being be seemed at one time to be. Indeed, there is a strange smile of half-beguiled credulity and half-vexed uncertainty upon the face. The creative power, previously exerted upon a thousand wild dreams and fancies, now exhibits itself in the attempt to verify by experiment all information on the subject of tops and boats.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>LEGENDS AND FABLES.</head>
        <p>LEGENDS AND FABLES.</p>
        <p>Although the legends and fables of child- hood never wholly fade from our memories, yet the colours are often dimmed and even the outlines unlimn. Yet should we not cherish such happy memories of the past with more than an antiquarian interest? Some of Scripture's most striking teaching is conveyed by means of fables. and Christ purposely used this method in order that those who wished might understand. It is quite possible that the world possesses older fables than any in Holy Writ. Many of those in Hindu and Arabian collections are of an antiquity which goes beyond all record and cannot be traced to any source in particular. Fables which are still our household words are thousands of years old and have taught wisdom to hundreds of generations in all parts of the world. The earliest fabulist—outside of Holy Writ—is the Arabian Locman, supposed to have been a contemporary of David's. Like lEsop, he appears to have been deformed, or, at least, he bore that reputa- tion which in both cases was probably a mere slander. His utterances so greatly impressed the people of his day that they received them as pure inspiration. It has been suggested by some writers that Locman and .2Esop may have been the same person, but such a proposition is quite unwarranted. In spirit their work may be similar, in detail it is very different. Locman is Asiatic; ./Esop is Greek. This slave, who lived six centuries before Christ, has so identided himself with fables, tha,t,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>NURSERY RHYME.</head>
        <p>THE NURSERY RHYME.</p>
        <p>We are all well acquainted with the nursery rhymes and tales of our childhood. Many of them are of quite an antiquarian interest, much too broad a subject for present consideration. Mother Goose, instead of being a traditional bard, or a creature of fancy, as commonly supposed, was a veritable personage. The mother- in-law of Thomas Fleet, the editor of the "Boston Weekly Reversal," was the original Mother Goose—the " old woman" of the world-famous melodies. Mother Goose belonged to a wealthy family in Boston, where her eldest daughter, Elizabeth Goose, was married in 1715 to Fleet. In due time she gave birth to a son, and,like most mothers-in-laws, the import- ance of Mrs. Goose increased with the appearance of her grandchild. Poor Mr. Fleet, half distracted with her endless nursery ditties, finding all other means fail tried what ridicule could effect, and actually printed a book with the title: "Songs for the Nursery, or Mother Goose's melodies for children, printed by T. Fleet, at his printing house, Padding-lane, Boston. Price 10 coppers." She was the mother of 19 children, and hence we may easily trace the origin of that famous classic. "There lives an old woman who lives in a shoe,</p>
        <p>She had so many children she didn't know what to do,"</p>
        <p>"Where are you going, my pretty maid ?" is a well-known quotation. In the original form, however, it was much longer and quite objectionable. Accordingly, at the beginning of the 18th century, fresh words were written and a new tune composed, so that, like the Irishman's knife, it was all new. It existed in the old Cornish language. The well-known lines beginning 'Hickory, dickory dock," described in a poetic way, the action of the old-fashioned clock when about to strike and while in the action of striking.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>HUMOUR OF CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>THE HUMOUR OF CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Much might be written concerning the humour of which children are so prone, "Teacher, you are very kind to me," said a young lad to his teacher one day, "I call you my second-hand father." Absences from school cause quite a stock of humour. A parent writes to the schoolmistress: "Dear Miss . . . Dorothy's absence was , required at home yesterday afternoon." ' Another child tells the head teacher that her mother had the " new-maticks." Parents often receive some amusing answers. "When I was your age I didn't have as much money in a month as you spend in a day," ejaculated a proud father. "Well, pa, don't scold me. Why don't you go _ for grandpa ! " Humour is not unknown in Bible examination answers by school children. Here are two examples which are warranted as authentic. "The Pharisee's prayer was—" Lord, I thank Thee that Thou hast not made anybody else like me." And "He turned the water into wine, saying, 'Blessed are the poor in spirit.'" This writer once heard a Catholic priest catechising the children in regard to Purgatory. Asking the children who first entered these lower regions, they replied in one voice, "Adam and Eve." He told them that Adam and Eve were two separate persons, whereas he had asked for one person only. This naturally caused the children to divide their attention between Adam and Eve instead of Cain, whom the priest had in his mind at that time. One lad, however, more zealous than the rest, noticing the diminished interest, spontaneously ejaculated "Dr. Crippen," which naturally caused the ecclesiastic to burst into loud laughter. Another story relates that'a Sunday School teacher had been explaining to her infant class how people are made of the dust of the earth, when a small girl put up her hand and asked, "Please, teacher, are niggers made of coal-dust ? "</p>
        <p>THE MENTAL AGE OF THE CHILD. Many parents desire to find some effec- tive method of discovering whether their offspring is really up to the average as regards intellectual power. For such the following tests will be found of high service. The average child is a very uncertain as to its sex before the age of four, and children of three nearly always fail in noting the difference between a key, a knife, and a coin, whereas those of four generally succeed. A child of three should be able to repeat two digits and not three, whereas one of four can usually manage three, but no more. By eight years a child can generally repeat five digits, and at twelve he should be able to cope with seven. The ability to com- prehend the longer of two lines does not generally arrive until the fourth year. Three-year-old children may see that the lines are unlike and yet not know the manner in which they differ. A child of three rarely ever goes beyond the mere enumeration of the people and objects in a picture, whereas, at seven, the child should be capable of true description. Then it is that the qualities and relations of people and objects are first mentioned The child is probably three or over if it knows its last name. .It is six years mentally, in one respect, if it can distinguish between morning and afternoon. If it can tell the date off-band it equals the average child of eight in that particular. A child of three should be able to repeat a simple sent- ence of six syllables, at five to deal with twelve syllables, and at six with something like sixteen. At eight a child should ,be ableto discriminate between familiar colours and to count from twenty backwards. With the ninth year a child should have no hesitation in giving the days of the week in their proper order,and at ten in displaying with- out difficulty its knowledge of the months of the year. At eleven, he should be ready, after a caution, to listen very carefully, to deal with sentences containing some stupid observation, e.g., "There was a railway accident the other day,but it was not at all serious. Thirty-seven were killed and a large number injured." This is a most effective test,and experiments shew that at ten only 25 per cent., at eleven nearly 50 per cent., and at twelve practically every child will be ready to point out the ridiculous nature of the sentence under review. RECASTS AND FORECASTS.</p>
        <p>In the course of our several histories we haye, many of us, without the least doubt,</p>
        <p>visited the little counrry town so well- known in the days of our childhood. We have felt that everything had undergone a, change. The streets had undergone, how- ever, no real alteration, and the houses were just as tall as before. But we had grown and brought the standards of a new world to our old view of life. So much with child-life. Many may feel a little ashamed that nursery rhymes should charm and please, and that we should feel a glow about the heart as we rehearse for : another generation the doughty deeds of old-time heroes and heroines, or sing the melodies of the cradle-side songstress. , There is, however, a great deal to be said in favour of training a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not depart therefrom. You can bend the twig, but not the tree, which will break sooner than bow. This fact seems to have been recognised by our education authorities, and all the children are now medically examined, whilst some com- mittees have actually opened Juvenile Employment Bureaux to assist them in placing the children under their charge in more beneficial employment than would otherwise be the case. "To. cure is the voice of the past; to prevent the Divine whisper of to-day." It is not for us only to root up the tares, scattered across our path, but also to take judicious measures to (pl laaynst t oa osoeme d e ,wwhi ti hc ha srha ac el l N‘f,ueri, ifish u tt, ects, t and conquer in the battle-field-of life</p>
        <p>iYI the ght</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>RELICS OF THE VIKINGS.</head>
        <p>RELICS OF THE VIKINGS.</p>
        <p>Manx Memorials Which Tell of Far-off Days of Cpnquest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>RESCUED FROM IGNOBLE USES.</head>
        <p>RESCUED FROM IGNOBLE USES.</p>
        <p>Twelve years ago when on the quiet north-west coast of the Isle of Man, we discovered a beautiful Runic Cross in use as a gate post. Six years ago we saw it again, this time encased and cared for in the porch of Jurby Church. Upon the same visit we saw in Maughold Church- yard a "Cross House" opeued in 1907 by Lord Raglan, and designed to become a permanent resting-place for the Runic Stones that dot the Island. We record this contrast in gratitude that Manxland has at last awakened to the value of some of her most precious antiqui- ties, and propose to offer here for the advan- tage of visitors to the Island a brief account of some of these memorials of a distant past.</p>
        <p>WHEN THE VIKINGS INVADED MONA.</p>
        <p>Far back in the days of the Saxons the Vikings came here from the frozen north, and established a little kingdom. The event is still in the popular mind, though the Manx people as a whole only know it in crudest form: Xi g Orry of old Was a pirate bold, Aid he came from over the sea, With 4 hundred ships and a hundred men, A Viking bold _was be.</p>
        <p>Memorials- of that early conquest are abundant. It can be traced in the laws and customs, and it has left everywhere</p>
        <p>changing weather and steady neglect there is enough of beauty to stir our admiration.</p>
        <p>A KIRK BRADDAN CROSS.</p>
        <p>The Wheel Cross at Braddan is another good example, and and well worthy of study. Most students consider its carving to represent Daniel in the lions den. The figures of two lions resting on their haunches are seen in the upper part, and a man's head appears between. The head may be that of Daniel, and the fact that the lions' chins rest upon their forepaws may mean that their jaws are closed in his presence. The rest of the stone is covered with plait work of regular pattern. The name of Gant, the sculptor, occurs again upon a fine stone at Andreas. The words that remain are: . . Erected . this . . to the memory Of Ofeig her father. but Gant Bjornson of Cooley made it. This inscription is typical. Most of the crosses are discovered to be memorials to the dead erected by near relatives of the departed. One at Michael has an epitaph: Better is it to have a good foster than a bad son. And another at Braddan has the very interesting words:</p>
        <p>But . . Hross Katill . . betrayed in a truce his own oath fellow. At Conchan is another writing which has been read:</p>
        <p>. . A man must fear little and do well. so can he have the benefit of the Cross of Jesus Christ.</p>
        <p>Calf Island contributes to the stock of Manx antiquities a very remarkable stone engraved with a Crucifixion. It shows a soldier with a spear at the side of the Cross, and were the stone complete a sponge bearer would probably be found on the other side. The figure of Jesus is complete and the style suggests that tile carving belongs to the 6th century. SCANDINAVIAN BELIEFS AND CHRISTIAN SYMBOLS.</p>
        <p>Of uncommon interest are the carvings which represent Scandinavian beliefs mixed Nvith Christian symbols. Some have scenes from the life of Thor, and some of Sigurd, the favourite Viking hero. It was a special pleasure to us to learn that the stone we discovered as a gate post in Jurby was such. It shows Sigurd slaying the dragon with a sword. The brute snorts venom upon him and lashes out in the agony of death as it turns upon his destroyer. A second panel shows Sigurd with the dragon's heart upon a spear. His thumb is placed to his mouth as though to cool it after it has been scalded by the hot blood, and not far away is the hero's steed Grain. Those who wish to see Thor may find him doing deeds of valour upon a stones at Kirk Bride, and at Andreas we see Odin swallowed by the Ferris Wolf. STONES WHICH BRIDGE FOURTEEN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
