      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>CENTURIES.</head>
        <p>CENTURIES.</p>
        <p>This blending of symbols—the carving of Thor and Odin upon a Christian Cross— takes us back to a remote and interesting age. _ _</p>
        <p>-We are back at the period when Ireland was the missionary nation, and when her brave sons taught the fierce Vikings the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <p>"Front _Manchester City Yews." RUNIC CROSS IN MAUGHOLD CHURCHYARD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>GOOD TEETH.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEETH.</p>
        <p>ADVANTAGE OF THE POOR AND THE</p>
        <p>:DARK COMPLEXIONED</p>
        <p>It was stated at the conference of dentisl.s at Cambridge on Tuesday, that decay could spread not only from tooth to tooth in a mouth, hut from the teeth of one individual to those of another. _ „</p>
        <p>Dental caries was the most prevalent of all diseases of childhood, and there was a con n et— Hon between dental caries and the inflamma- tory conditions of the neighbouring tonsils and glands. Everything showed that dental caries was a coidagious disease. Mr. C. Wallis, dental surgeon at King's Col- lege Hospital, London, controverted this I heory, his explanation being that it was simply and solely the bringing up of the children. A poor child brought up in a fearful struggle for exist- ence who had the advantage of being fed froni the breast, grew up to school age with perfect dentition, whereas the children of well-to-do parents, who gave them sweets and cakes and comforts. bad unwholesome teeth. Dr. Smith, of Massachusetts, suggested that nationality had a great deal to do with caries. In America they found that dark complexioned races had sounder teeth than light com- plexioned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>CHILDREN'S PRNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. \</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S PRNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. \</p>
        <p>- THE NORWEGIAN BOY WHO DISCOVERED AMERIC A.. We have all been made familiar with the statement that Columbus discovered America, and he has had lavished upon him all the fame that attaches to such an honour. He deserves all the homage be has received, for he was a brave navigator. But if you ever visit Bristol you will see upon the bridge near the old harbour a tablet which states that long before Columbus left the shores of Spain Sebastian Cabot sailed from that place and discovered the Continent of America. But there is another claimant to the honour. It is said that 480 years before Christopher Columbus was born a Norwegian youth made his way across the Atlantic and travelled far down the coast of what we now call America. The story of this adventure has been told in an American ournal, " Harper's Young People," more fully than I have seen it told elsewhere, and I propose to re-tell the chief incidents here. The youth was named Biorn, son of Hergolf. He was known by no other title, for in those days sons did not share the father's name. In the year 1002, Hergolf, an Iceland colonist, fitted out two small vessels for a trading voyage to the Greenland settle- ment and placed one of these under the command of his son Biorn, a youth of 16 years, who, having been bred to the sea almost since infancy, had mastered the details of his profession at an age when other boys usually commence their apprenticeship. When near th? southern coast of Green- land, Biorn's ship was driven far to the south and west. One morning he dis- covered a strange land close aboard. Sail- ing along the coast for some distance he found a large bay, into which he steered and dropped anchor. Upon landing the country was seen to be clothed with vegetation and the streams Awarming with fine salmon, Trees of large growth grew in great numbers just back from the shore, and the climate was balmy and delightful. Of natives they ,-;aw nothing, and believed the land uninhabited. _ . .</p>
        <p>- Rejoiced over his important discovery. Biorn returned to Iceland and communi- cated the news to his friend Lief, son of Eric the Red, who had founded the colony on the coast of that island. The two ambitious young men immediately entered into an agreement. to share the expense of equipping a suitable vessel, sailing to this newly-discovered land, and bringing back whatever cargo promised to reimburse them for fitting out the ship. Their first sight of the new land was not calculated to impress Lief :with a promise of fruitfulness, fo-e it was rocky, barren, and gloomy. This gave rise to openly expressed dissatisfactiOn on his part, but Biorn assured him that further south they would meet with green fields and woodlands. After the fashion of the early navigators in naming geographical discoveries accord- ing to the features first presented, this place they called Hal'eland, and to the low, sandy shore which they observed beyond it, and which wss co-Yered in spots with clumps of small frees, they gave the name of Markland. Two days later they fell in with a new line of coast, and sailing along this for several hours Blom made out the bay in which + he had anchored on his previous voyage. Into this harbour they brought the ship and moored her. The explorers soon succeeded in catching a number of fine salmon from the river that emptied into the bay, and collected quantities of fruit of different varieties and of most agreeable flavour. The tempera- ture of the air, a:, on the former voyage, was found to be delightfully mild to the Icelanders, and the soil proved to be rich and productive. One of the member's of the crew, a German, met with enormous quantities of grapes growing wild, and from this circumstancs the two navigators decided to name the country Vinland, meaning the land of grapes. This Vinland of the early voyagers is known at the pre- sent day as Newfoundland. After making several short cruises to the southward and westward, and sailing through the gulf of St. Lawrence unti. the river of that name was reached the ship returned to her first anchorage, where the explorers passed the winter. . .</p>
        <p>During the months that the vessel lay at anchor many valuable furs and skins were obtained by the hunters, and in the spring the ship was freighted with valu- able lumber hewn close to the shore. With the proceeds of this venture the two boy discoverers fitted out their vessel second time, but an unfortunate quarrel breaking out between them, Lief sailed alone to establish a colony after the example of his father For this purpose he embarked some men and women, glad to exchan;.- bleak shores of Iceland for the rich, meadows and purling " this promised land. Norwegian history asserts that Liei. sailed as far south is the plize now known as Newport, in Rhode Island, and points for confirmation of the claim to the remains of Norse fortifications and implements that have been found in various places on the New England shores.</p>
        <p>ge th(</p>
        <p>streams of</p>
        <p>THE WEEKLY SMILE. NOT A CASE FOR SCIENCE</p>
        <p>The following story is told of a small boy who was laboriously pushing a heavy hand cart up a steep hill and stopping every few minutes to 'wipe the perspiration from his face. A clergyman watching him, and thinking to help the lad, called out, "Push it up zigzag, my little friend, and you will find it go much easier." "Not so much a' yer bloomin' advice," replied the boy; come and give me a shove."</p>
      </div>
