      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>BOOMING THE BOROUGH.</head>
        <p>BOOMING THE BOROUGH.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Corporation Trade Committee has passed a resolution to support a movement by which local authorities in England and Wales shall, as in the case in Ireland, have the power to levy a fate not exceeding id. for the pttrpose of advertising the commercial and other advantages and-attractioua of their dis- tricts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>LARGE DOSE.</head>
        <p>A LARGE DOSE.</p>
        <p>Isaac Kinder, 5, Clarence-court, Peasley Cross. was remanded at St. Helens on Monday on a charge of attempted suicide. The Chief Constable said that ou Friday night Kinder drank a large quantity of lina- ment with sufficient poison in it to poison half the town, and Dr. Reid did not understand how he had managed to survive. He had been taken to the hospital, and it was necessary that he should be kept under re- straint at least another week, for he was in a bad state. A week's remand was ordered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>CURIOUS STORY.</head>
        <p>A CURIOUS STORY.</p>
        <p>A curious story was told to the magistrates on Monday, when James Frodsham, glass flat- tener,'Fox-street, was remanded upon a charge of housebreaking at 49, Parliament-street. Mrs. Emily Bradbury, wife of a boilermaker, said that her husband was working away in London. Six 'months ago they lived in Union- street, and .prisoner was friendly with her hus- band. He pestered her with his attentions, and followed her about. He succeeded- in persuad- ing -her" to go ivith him to Stockport; but she went direct to her sister's, knowing that he would not be allowed to go there. In consequence of prisoner's attentions she moved. with her family to Parliament-street, Thatto Heath. On Sunday night, about nine o'clock, she went to bed with her .children, and at a quarter to eleven she heard the back win- dow break, and someone began to walk up- stairs. She jumped up, locked the door, opened the window, and called for help. A number of people came, and she shouted to her sister to have the door burst in. Some men came in and caught prisoner at the top of the stairs, he having gone into the back bed- room.</p>
        <p>Prisoner said that since the woman's hus- band had been away he had been at her house fourteen or fifteen times. He went in on Sun- day night by the backyard • door, which was open. He believed it had been left open for him, but in going past the window he fell, and his shoulder broke the window.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable suggested that a re- mand should be granted in order that inquiries could be made.,</p>
        <p>The Bench ordered a remand. and granted bail in £lO and two sureties of £5 each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>TRADE DISPUTES.</head>
        <p>TRADE DISPUTES.</p>
        <p>No Compulsory Arbitration.</p>
        <p>After a long consideration of the subject of trade disputes, the Industrial Council have issued a report upon the inquiries made of both employers and employes. The Council expresses itself as against com- pulsory arbitration as a general rule. Effective organisation on the part of both employers and workpeople and "moral obligation" are re- garded as essential to the fulfilment of agree- ments. Thesquestion of money penalties is re- garded as undesirable, and such penalties as prohibition of assistance to persons in breach should not be made legally obligatory. The declaration, however, is made, with the "mica- mum degree of emphasis," that where a broach of an agreement has been committed no assist- ance, financial or otherwise, should be given to the persons-in breach by any of the other mem- bers of the associations connected with the agreement.</p>
        <p>The opinion is expressed that the general adop, tion •of a system of monetary guarAntees in the form of a deposit of money cannot be e- garded as constituting ct practicable and efficient means- of ensuring the fulfilment of agreements. This is added: " At the same time, where mone- tary guarantees are voluntarily offered •we see no _objection to their adoption."</p>
        <p>The° suggestion is mtide that agreements. shOnld in general he limited to a period of three years, but there is no wish to interfere with any practice of maldup the time longer.</p>
      </div>
