        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 9, 1913. A GARDEN VILLAGE.</p>
        <p>to exceed 50 square yards): 1, W. Melling; 2, G. Langley; 3, G. Fleet. The neatest and best- arranged small front garden: 1, Mrs. Bennett; 2, G. Leigh; 3, J. Battersb2,-. The neatest and best-stocked backyard garden: 1, J. Holt; 2, G. Morris; 3, F. Langley. The neatest and best- arranged back garden: 1, G. Houghton; 2, H. Lawrence: 3, J. Ellison. The neatest, cleanest and best-stocked backyard greenhouse: 1. F. Acton; 2, W. Howard; 3, J. Colley. The neat- est, cleanest and best-stocked greenhouse in any other position: 1, D. S. Birrell. The neatest and best-arranged villa garden: 1, J. Blinston; 2, F. Broady; 3, W. H. Woodcock. The neatest and best-arranged window box (the plants must be grown in the box): 1, Mrs. Bennett; 2, J. Bretherton ; 3, Mrs. J. Morris. The best dis- play of creepers on house (winner of 1912 not eligible): 1, W. Skelton; 2, C. 'Young; 3. J. Blinston. Best-kept privet hedge: 1, F. War- burton; 2, H. Robinson; 3, D. -Binns. Collec- tion of rose trees in beds: 1, J. Blinston; 2, J. B. Baxter; 3, G. Houghton._</p>
