      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>Stockton Heath Ga!den Society's Work of Beautification.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath Ga!den Society's Work of Beautification.</p>
        <p>A SUCCESS</p>
        <p>The Stockton fleari Oarden Society has come into its own. Public recognition—once tardy and somewhat half-hearted—has now been bestowed in full 'measure, as witness the splendid success of the summer show, held in Wilderspool Park on Monday: Everything conspired to, make the exhibition one that will always have a proud -Once in the society's records. The day was gloriously fine, the entries were excellent in quality, and gratifying numerically, while the fact that the show was the principal Bank Holiday open-air attraction for those who remained in town did much to ensure a constant stream of. visitors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>GARDENING ENCOURAGED.</head>
        <p>GARDENING ENCOURAGED.</p>
        <p>To appreciate the work of the society, the seeing individual has only to take a walk through the village. onhlfuesthtbeest_stoi: Naturally a pleasant place of abode, Stockton Heath has taken upon itself the character of a garden village, where iehsoifce blooms thrive on every hand. Most of the inhabitants seem to be horticulturists ,in a lesser degree and ,the friendly resulting in gardens which can only rivalry, resulting be described as perfect pictures of beauty, be ascribed to the stimulating influence -Garden Society. Prizes are given for lied and most tastefully-arranged gardens, the classes being So contrived as to give all classes of 'residents an equal chance of success. Another very praiseworthy. feature of the society's operations is the encouragement it gives to children to take up Nature that most refreshing and educative. of bobbies. _ __ _ which study,</p>
        <p>---- A BLAZE OF COLOUR. For the show on .Nlonday.there were upwards of no classes. The marquees in 'which- the flowers were arranged were a blaze of nicely colours, and at no previous exhi- hiliajorribisilig as such a striking display been wit- The outstanding .features! were the nessed sweet peas and table decorations. There was exceptionally keen competition in the ! first- named seqtion, and many beautiful varieties of the flower were most effectively staged. In the devising of the table decorations the. lady conipetitors .had shown. pronounced artistic taste, and Mrs. F. Broady's array of crimson rambler- roses -deservedly secured the premier award . The roses were excellent, especially in view of the fact that the show was held between the seasons. The more attractive varieties included the "Lyon," " Frau Carl Druschi," and "Rayon (For:, There were many lovely specimens of carnations, violas, and pansies. The herbaceous cut flowers were flaolslovetii s stib.oerii4secsthionnwn ~so. me The very flowersfine wildsPi gathered by the children were evidence of pro- les o f taw rambles by the, countryskle, !and one could only marvel at the very rare specimens Iwhich some oftlle• point Whichildrenadshsouucicdeedroed i!.:i. 4,atibeehring. One. upon. as having a distinct educational !sviasitnecelThe , is uality the correct naming of the flowers. ! of the vegetables was good,! . though, probably ,owing to the late season. the ! ! entries were not so numerous as in previous work ! !•years. ,_ out "r j , gi , , q ! The ng. the exhibits was under- ! ! taken in a very thorough. and conscientious ! manner by Mr. A. Calderbank, of the Horticul- tural College,,, Holmes Chapel; Mr.• Joseph . Stone'', of Wootton ; Mr G. Corbett, of Alt rinc- ham ; and Mr. W.. Bram Well, of Altrincham. The gardens were judged i) Poul- ton P. wiikhisoil, and j.. Ryan. R. !eThe society was melt !indebted,. to several firms of eminence In th horticultural world for some remarkably fine.exhibits, not for corn- neution, 'which greatly enhanced the appear- Lance of the tents. Messrs. Bees, Ltd., Liver- pool, sent ' a wonderful .collection of roses; MesSrs. Dick &amp;MS, •Ltd., Chester, niiscelhineous blooms; !Mr- Henry Eckford, Wem, ,many varieties-- of Sweet. peas; ;Messrs. .Ker and Son, Liverpool, foliage plants ;' Mr. 11. Middlehtirst, or Liverpool, sweet peas • , Messrs. Youngs: West Derby, carnations. by Mr. Robson J. Chorley and Mr. Ja lovely Who bike a, great interest in the society's work; while Mr.. W.,. Antrobus, of Inis ,se rewnr carnations. In addition, some Ryan. James -Wallow Inferior, had an exhibit of bees. - ! . Visitors to the exhibition during the afternoon included Lord and Lady Aeton and Mrs. Lyon. -• Athletic sports 'were 'a welcome adjunct of the show, and the various events were keenly contested. - A novelty was the three miles Marathon rtre. A concert _party, under the leadership of Mr. w.. w. Whittle, gave delightful entertainments at intervals. the sentimental and humorous &amp;e- -ients being well blended.. The artistes were n -Mrs. E. A. Sanders, hiessr.s.! J. w. Willett,. G. Gough, A. Hale, and W. W., Whittle, and Miss Hale (accompanist). The Pioneer Band played selections, and in the evening there was danc- ing on the green. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>OPENING CEREMONY.</head>
        <p>THE OPENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>There was an exceptionally large attendance at the opening • of the show by Mrs. -It 1. Wynne Jones,. Councillor W. H. J. Jenkins presided, and among others present were: The Nfayor and 'Mayoress (Councillor Dr. Joseph and mrs. Arnold Nield), Colonel Alan Sykes, M.P. Mr axr\i„(,l. Bolton,lll s.. iAbiadeArirl: aan ge, Smethurst, Aldermanie Rev. and Mrs. Pigot, Messrs. R. I. Wynne W. 11. Woodcock (chairman ojNiftlinseN'tsrhej eHo'inCtni flirtieeP\ 11. J. Westbrook A. S. 'right. G. J. Brown, D. E. Watson (honorary treasurer), and E. G. White (honorary secre- tarA• Tile PIAMNIAN announced the receipt of letters of apology for norkittendanCe from Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart.', and Lady Greenall., Lord Wollner, COlOnel and Mrs. Cornwall-tegh, Mr. Boiler C. Pa!r.,- Mr.. Piers Egerton-Warburton, Mi.p. M. Oliver, and Mr.- John .Coxon. Mrs. WYNNE JONES, in declaring the show opensexpressed the hope that •it - would be the most t successful, both horticulturally and finan- cially, that the society had yet promoted. (Ap- plause.) Lime Freda Morris then gracefully presented Nis' wynne Jones with a lovely bouquet of orchids and carnations-, the gift of Alderman Bolton._</p>
        <p>—file CHAIRMAN said the society had grown in numbers, and its position had also improved financially. Still, there were many residents of Stockton Heath who were not members, and the society would be only too delighted to enroll them. It was also hoped that more voluntary helpers Would come .forward as prize-givers.</p>
        <p>TfIE MANTIII'S HINT AT AMALGAMATION. illtovoctoeutoj trthortsieltpo ;1:s1.1 Nikvi4yA.-11;°nne' iJno npers7 ossaile noting, with some degree of pleasant irony, that the Stockton Heath Garden Society had to conw to ,Warrington to obtain a site for its show. because he did not think there ought (Laughter.) He did not say that in any spirit between Warrington and tob ete(tonl 111. Y v Hreiat}l'il.ry He believed the time must of rivalry, to come—and very likely at an early' date—when the two places would be One municipality. He was not there to advocate amalgamation, but he was Only expressing his own view when he said that the interests of Stockton Heath. and Warrhigton—social, municipal, sanitary—and the supply of the necessaries of life, such as gas -and water, and other beverages—(laughter) _made it incumbent that the union of the two places should take place very soon. (Applause.) Mr. WOODCOCK seconded,- and said. Mr. and Mrs. Wynne Jones had . been loyal friends of the society from its inception. Mr. WYNNE JONES, in acknowledging the com- pliment, on behalf of his wife, expressed sans- faction .at the great progress \\ hilt the Society had ma(le_ The Mayor seemed 1 hint that the show was an appanage of Warrington, because it was held in .the' borough. As matter of fact, however, Warrington was only an appanage of Stockton Heath. "(Laughter.) Where N'as Warrington's flower show? They , had to come to Stockton Heath for one. (Laugh-1 ter.) He (the speaker) Mid known that society in its, troublous tithes, and he was glad now to be .with it in .its prosperous dti S. Tnfortunately, the show could not be main- tained without money. The Stockton Heath shOw had not a, picturesque setting like the Shrewsbury sltow, but. with their enterprising and energetic committee, there need be no limits to the extension :'of the'socie, t s work. , (Applause.r SYKES, M.P., proposed a vote of thanks to Councillor Jenkins for presiding. He Colonel ALAN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>FUL SHOW.</head>
        <p>FUL SHOW.</p>
        <p>said he had listened to the Mayor's remarks as to the future of Stockton Heath with a great deal of interest. A few yOars ago he (the speaker) was Mayor of Stockport, and he rejoiced that that borough: had recently been able to ' include part of Lancashire within its boundaries. He was not quite so sure whether, as Member for the knutsford Division, he would be equally at one with the Mayor of Warring- ton in wanting to annex Stockton Heath. (Laughter.) '1 hey were pleased to be there, in friendly rivalry, to promote the interests of that society. They had set the seal of success on its work, and he believed they would make it their business to see that they did not go back, but continue to improve.. (Applause.) Mr. WYNNE JONES seconded, and the vote was warmly passed. Mr. CALDERBANK expressed the opinion of the judges that the exhibition, as a whole, did great credit to. the district. When. they considered the spare limitations of the gardens, as well as the locality, they must realise the difficulties which exhibitors must have in providing such line specimens of flowers and plants. He con- gratulated the committee on the numerous classes, and particularly on the selection of classes Which were suitable to the district. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>OFFICIALS.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The society has an excellent band of workers, under the presidency of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. .Mr. W. H. Woodcock is chairman of the committee, Mr. J. H. Fletcher vice-chair- man, and Mr. D. E. Watson honorary treasurer. A special word of praise is due to the hard- working honorary secretary, Mr. E. G. White.</p>
        <p>"CULTURAL 'SKILL AND EXCELLENT TASTE."</p>
        <p>The judges in the garden section report as follows: We should like to record that the standard of excellence set up in previous years has been well maintained. In the class for the best arranged gardens, of 50 square yards, and in that for the neatest and best stocked back:</p>
        <p>-ard garden, the exhibits were especially most</p>
        <p>pleasing and, reveal in the exhibitors he 'pos- session of cultural skill and excellent taste. This applies to the non-winners almost equally to those.who secured the.awards. In the class for the best kept and best stocked cottage gar- den, the gardens were also very good, the first and second - prize-winners showing ,splendid examples:. In--the class, for greenhouses there was only one competitor, and anyone desirous of seeing a beautifully kept .and stocked green- house would do well to pay a visit to that of Mr. Birrell, in Ellesmere-road."</p>
      </div>
