s of mixed varieties of sweet peas: 1, V. Flettther; 2, F. A. Rathbone; 3, Mrs. F. Langley. Best pot, tub or box of growing sweet peas: 1, P. Starker; 2, W. Buck. Twelve pansies: 1, E. W. Whitehouse; 2, E. E. Lucas; 3, W. A. Timperley; C. J. Gleave. Six asters: 1, E. W. White,house; 2,- E. E: Lucas. Six stocks: 1, E. W. Whitehouse; 2, A. J. Mot- tram: 3, F. Kippax. Six dahlias (cactus): 1 E. W. Whitehouse. Six roses (distinct varie- ties): 1, J. Blinston; 2, A.J. Mottram; 3, R. Dain- tith. Twelve. roses: 1, J. Blinston;- 2,..R. Dain- tith; 3, J. Owen. Bowl or vase of cut roses: 1, Daintith; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, F. A. Rathbone; c, F. Broady. Best single spray of any variety of rose: 1, A. J. Mottram; 2, R. Daintith; 3, J. Blinston. Six carnations or pi icot ees : 1, E. E. Lucas; 2, F. A. Rathbone; 3. E. W. Whitehouse; c, A. J. Mottram. One vase of carnations or picotees : 1, P. A. Rathbone; 2, E. E. Lucas.; 3, A. 3. Mottram. Eighteen violas (three varieties): 1, A. J. Mottram; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, J. Blinston. Collection of cut blooms (twelve varieties): 1, F. Kippax; 2, E. W. Whitehouse. Four vases of cut blooms: 1, E. W. Whitehouse; 2, A. J. Mottram; 3, F. Kippax; c, E. Tavlor..• One vase of one variety cut bloom: 1, S. .t. Woodcock; 2, Mrs. F. Lang- ley. Two asters: 1, H. Barron; 2,_ H. Mot- tram. Two stocks: 1, H. Mottram; 2, G. Hough- ton; '3, W. Melling. Two carnations: 1. J. Gleave; 2' H. Herron; 3, A. J. Mottram. Two roses: 1, W. Lockett; 2, L. -Bretherton; 3, H. Herron. Two dahlias (cactus): I,' W. Lockett; 2, H. Mottram. Two perennial phlox: 1, F. Kippax; 2, 'H. Herron: 3. E. Taylor. Three vases of cut blooms: 1, H. Barron; 2' A. J. Mottram. Vase of any one' variety of flower: 1, R. Daintith; 2' E. W. Whitehouse; 3, A. J. Mottram. Vase of mixed flowers: 1, A. J. Mot- tram; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, F. A. Rathbone. Collection of wild flowers (members under 12 years of age) : 1. B. Langley; 2, H. Acton; 3, D. Mills. Collection of wild flowers (members 12 