      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>JUNIOi SECTION.</head>
        <p>JUNIOi SECTION.</p>
        <p>_ Best display of plants in flower, grown in a box not exceeding 2ft. in length: 1, Bertha Langley; 2, Jack Gleave ;3, T. Colley. The neatest and best-stocked small garden plot: 1, S. Birrell; 2, p. Birrell.</p>
        <p>A new feature was the gift class. There were many competitors, and the stock was after- wards sold by auction by Mr. J. H. 0. Johnson, of Warrington, for the benefit of the society. The prize-winners were: 1, J. McDermid; 2, F. A. Rathbone; 3, D. E. Watson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>SPORTS.</head>
        <p>THE SPORTS.</p>
        <p>The sports programme was one of exceptional interest, and the various events were followed with great enthusiasm. Mr. F. A. Jenkins threw himself whole-heartedly into the duties of secretary, and he was ably supported by a committee, consisting of Mr. J. Ellison (starter), and Messrs. J. Holt, G. Houghton, E. A. San- ders and J. McDermid. A popular feature was the tug-of-war, • in which the following took part: Messrs Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., Rylands and Sons, Ltd., St. Helens Cable Co., Ltd., Greenall, Whitley and Co., Ltd., Mr. Sey- mour's team of St. Helens Cable Co., and Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Ltd. The last- named firm took the first prize, with Messrs. Greenall and Whitley a good second. The Marathon race was won by three mem- bers of the Warrington Harriers as follows: 1, J. Hanson ;•.. 2, J. Nurse; 3, H. Payton. The other results were :</p>
        <p>Skipping-rope Race (Girls).--1, Lillie Clarke; 2, Mary Begg; 3, Jean Bunting. Plant-pot Race.-1, A. Clarke; 2, R. Darks; 3, G. Birrell..</p>
        <p>PH low Fight on Pole (under 15).—W. Clarke. Flag Squadron.—H. Fletcher, J. Hanson, S. inswortlj_and</p>
        <p>Egg-and-Spoon Race (Girls).-1, Jean Bunt- ing; 2, Susie Twigs; 3, Mary Begg. Veteran Race (age .60).-1, P. Reddington. Obstacle Race.-1, J. Nurse; 2, J. Hanson; 3, H. Boyle.</p>
        <p>Pillciw Fight on Pole (over 15).—T. Baguley.</p>
        <p>THE RECEIPTS. The "gate" receipts realised • £46—about in advance of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>ABOUNDING PROSPERITY.</head>
        <p>ABOUNDING PROSPERITY.</p>
        <p>Bank Chairman on Record Year for Capitalist and _Worker.</p>
        <p>UNION BANK OF MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>The abounding prosperity of the past year formed tne subject of congratulation at the tli annual general meeting of the shareholders of the union Bank of Manchester, Limited, held at the head office of the bank, lurk-street, Man- chester, on Friday of last week.</p>
        <p>The (lair was Occupied by Mr. G. C. Haworth, chairman of the bank, Who was supported by sir Charles Behrens (deputy-chairman), Mr. R. Oliver, Mr. Michael Cababe, Colonel C. IL France-Hayhurst, .D.L., Mr. Allan li, .Bright,l Sir Joseph Verdin, Bait, D.L., Right Hon. Lord Rochdale (directors), and Mr. P. Forrester (general manager). The Chairman, in moving the adoption of the report and balance-sheet, pointed .out that cur- rent, deposit, and other accounts now exceeded six millions of money. That was an increase on the last ten years of close upon 60 per. cent. The increase up to five years ago was 311- per cent. It would thus be seen that the growth of this item was important. The "commission- able turnover," by which he meant the turnover in customers: accounts, and which did not in- clude the business transacted on account of numerous home and foreign banks, showed an increase during the 101 years of: 873- per cent. The increase from 1903 to 1907 was 63 per cent. The number of accounts opened in their books with customers and depositors, excluding the accounts of numerous banters and others, showed an increase during the past ten years of 87 per cent. The increase for the five years, 1903 to 1907 inclusive, was approximately 50 per cent. . The net increase in the number of accounts during the 12 months ended June 30th, 1913, was twelve times as great as the net in crease in the number of accounts in the 12 months ended June, 1903, He was speaking of actual net increase, not merely the number of accounts opened. They would readily under- stand that a very large number of new accounts were necessary every year to fill the gaps caused by death,: change. of .address, &amp;c. He thought the shareholders would agree with him that these figures indicated the great progress of the Institution, and showed an increasing confidence in the Union Bank of Manchester. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE TRADE BOOM. Branches of the bank had been opened at Lymm, Patricoft, and Rawtenstall. The open- ing of these three branches had been justified by the satisfactory results which have followed. When the history of the year ending 1913 was written, it would be found to rank in prosperity for the capitalist and the labourer greater than any previous period. Whilst it was true that the cotton trade enjoyed a boom commencing with 1905, and ending with 1907, a period of three years, it must be conceded that that boom was confined to the textile trades. The period .under review had experienced an activity and prosperity not only in the cotton trade, but in every other branch of industry throughout the country. . • Much of the prosperity we have recently enjoyed had been in the export trade, and had come from our largest markets,--India,.. China, and elsewhere.</p>
        <p>It was as remarkable as it was significant that, although finances ana money had been restricted, resulting from a high rate of interest not frequently experienced, which under ordin- ary circumstances would have hindered trade, yet, heedless of the interest charged, trade had prospered.</p>
        <p>_ --- The demands made upon the spindle and the loom were greater than the supply, whilst the shipbuilding yards proved unequal to the strain on the builders. During this effort to increase production, labour was making demands for increased wages and reduced hours; at the same time expenses and materials had been advanc- ing, one might say, by leaps and bounds. For- midable as such additional costs were, 'satisfac- tory returns weremade.</p>
        <p>Air. W. 11.•Verdin seconded -the adoption of the report, which was agreed to.</p>
        <p>DIRECTORS AND AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>Lord Rochdale, Sir Joseph Verdin, and Sir Charles Behrens were elected directors of the bank.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Jones, Crewdson, and Youatt were appointed auditors. A vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Forrester, general manager, and the administrative officers of the bank for their services during the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>LUCKY LOSS IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>LUCKY LOSS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>To speak of a lucky loss seems a strange anomaly. But after reading this Warrington resident's experience we shall be the first to admit that the loss was a lucky one indeed. Mr. J. Milner, of 131, Foster-street, Orford- lane, Warrington, says: "For about three years, off and on, I suffered from shooting pains in my back, and after I had been sitting for any length of time I found it difficult to get up out of. my chair. When I stooped the pains would dart across my back like knife stabs. "I had nasty headaches, which made me feel miserable. There was distress in relieving the bladder; the water was painful and full of a dark, .heavy sediment.</p>
        <p>"I tried nany medicines, but never seemed to he able to get any permanent benefit till I used Doan's I3ackage Kidney Pills. Three boxes of these pills drove every symptom of kidney com- plaint from my system, and also improved my general health. That is now about six months ago, and I have not felt the slightest sign of 4 return. Dean's pills are a Splendid kidney medicine, and I have told many people abolt them. (Signed) .T. MILNER." Price .2s. 9d. a box, 6 boxes, 13s. 9d.; of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells- street, Oxford-street, Londe n, W. Don't ask ,for ro‘tiv for Bonn's Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mr. Milner had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
