      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>HUMBLE POTATO.</head>
        <p>THE HUMBLE POTATO.</p>
        <p>Romance and Superstition Attached Mr. John Burns's Outspoken to the Tuber. on Infant Mortality Que A POLITICAL STORY. PAMPERED PUPPII</p>
        <p>[" Examiner " Special.]</p>
        <p>Romance attaches to the potato, the humble</p>
        <p>article of diet. It plays a conspicuous part_ in our national</p>
        <p>life, apart from that of food. It aids in certain industries, and is the subject of much superstition.</p>
        <p>Everybody knows the important part potatoes PlaY in our national dietary, but everybody does not know the share the tubers play in some of our industries, nor are most people aware of the many quaint and faddy uses to Which the potatoes are put. In funland the potato-is used for the pur- pose of foot racing. At some Lancashire sport meetings a potato race is an attractive feature on the events card. The tubers are placed in rows along the course, and as the racers run they have to gather these. The first to com- plete the gathering and to reach the tape is de- clared the victor.</p>
        <p>Superstitious Reliefs. There are some faddy folk who believe that the potato is an infallible charm in warding off threatened attacks of rheumatism. The potato is most. frequently applied internally, but, like Certain poisonous liniments, it. has, in this instance, to be applied externally. One method is to procure a, small, round tuber, which must be gathered from a field when the moon is full, and'sew it in a silken bag. Then if the charm is to be effective the bag must be hung from the neck by a silk cord, and must be worn night and day. .Some others who prac- tise the superstition believe that the proper thing is to carry the potato in the waistcoat pocket. It is surprising to hear of the number of people who still religiously _believe in the .effi- cacy of this potato charm. I know of at least two local persons who are never without such a " protection " when the weather is damp and of the rheumaticy type, 6s it often is. Possibly a search might considerably augment the num- ber who follow this curious faith cure. Others again believe in placing slices of a potato in the heels of their boots as a " protection " against the complaint.' An old-fashioned cure, like our grandmothers used to make for toothache, was the applica- tion of steaming hot mashed potatoes, sewn in a flannel bag, to the face when the molars ached. For many generations, in Lancashire, this was believed a sovereign remedy. The reason given was •curious. The belief was that toothache was due to a small, white worm that ate its way into the tooth as tiny worms drill holes into the wood of old furniture. All worms, argued tradition, were fond of pota- toes, and, so, by applying the 'mashed potato poultice to the face the tooth worm was enticed .froffi the tooth and the sufferer procured in- stant relief. Doubtless the.nervehad.leen con- jured into an imaginary worm. In Industry.</p>
        <p>In some industries the humble tuber plays an important part. It is whispered that a per- centage- of our daily bread does not consist of pure wheat flour. Potatoes take its -place, and thus help to add to the profits of the baker. It is even hinted that a considerable part of that flaky pastry which decorates the afternoon tea :table is ground potato in disguise. Then there are cornflour biscuits, "so light and digestible, you know." In greater part the cornflour is In reality potato .flour. . Most people. _ like to wear clean,„ bright, starched collars. To .administer to this desire the humble potato is pressed into service. The starch makers ,purchase large quantities of potatoes. These they treat with different chemical processes and the result is starch. Little does the dandy with his six-inch deep, glazy collar, wot that the sheen from his neck- wear is due to the potato, which he may be inclined to despise as ‘'a very common article. Perhaps one may have a piano in the house, and one takes a special delight in sitting on the stool and gently strumming on Hie white ivory keys. Except in the case of the most ex- pensive pianos, the "ivory" has never come from a inammolis tusk. It is the product of applied science to the humble potato. If one's particular "-wanity," to quote a favourite word of Sam Weller, is to finger such ivory keys, first make sure the "ivory" is not potato.</p>
        <p>An Old Recipe.</p>
        <p>Potatoes were at one time jargely used in Lancashire for the purpose of cleaning silk, cotton and woollen goods. The recipe, which is well over a century old, states: "Take two middle-sized potatoes, freshly dug from the soil, wash them well, and then rub them to a fine pulp. MN with a pint of water, pass the mixture through a sieve into a small tub of water, let the small particles of potatoes pre- cipitate and preserve the mucilaginous liquor. With this liquor lightly sponge the article until the dirt is removed, wash in clean water and dry." This process was put to a severe test, and It proved so efficient that the inventor, who I believe was a Lancashire man, was awarded a premium of fifteen guineas from the old Adelphi Society of Arts. The discoverer of the proceks, it. seems, used the parts of -the pota-- toes Which' could not puss through the sieve for the manufacture Of starch and for white powder to dress the wigs of the dandies who lived in those days. He also ,made cornflour, and strongly recommended that with milk as an excellent source of food. The potato liquor was for many years used as the best medium for cleaning valuable oil paintings, and for other purposes. An Irishman's Trick.</p>
        <p>Many .jakes have4Jeen made about the potato, but .one of the most extraordinary was perpe- trated in the House of Commons about a cen- tury ago. It Was the custom of the Parliamen- tary reporters then. to leave two of their confreres in the Press. Gallery, while the rest NVelli out for amusement, the two men on duty giving a report of what had transpired in the absence of tlie others.</p>
        <p>It happened that one night two Irishmen were left alone in the Gallery, Thomas Finnerty, a notOriotis practical joker, and another. named Moran. The latter fell asleep. When he awoke Finnerty -dictated to Moran a somewhat lengthy speech, whieh he declared Wilberforce, then .a leading politician, had delivered on the virtues of the Irish potato, and how it had saved Ireland from ruin. The "speech" was excellent copy, and Moran in turn dictated it to the other reporters upon their return. Wil- berforce.'s potato "Speech" appeared in the London daily papers next morning, with the exception of that to which Finnerty was attached, and. created. a great -sensation. The reporters who had given such promi- nence to this "speech" were astounded when, next day. Wilberforce rose in the *House and Indignantly denied that he had made such a speech, and demanded an apology from the Press. The apology was given, and then for a long time Finnerty had a very Uncomfortable life in the Gallery.</p>
        <p>W.D.B.</p>
        <p>SOUTHPORT BALLROOM TRAGEPY. Shortly before ten o'clock on Tuesday night a married woman named Houghton, 61 years of age, and residing in Prescot, collapsed in the ballroom of the Southport Whiter Gardens and fell to the floor. She was helped outside to the grounds, and a doctor and a police ambu- lance were summoned. As the ambulance was first on the spot, Mrs. Houghton was driven in it to the infirmary, and on'arrival it r-tis found that she was dead. The deceased had been on a trip from .Prescot to Southport, and had intended returning by a train shortly after ten o'clock. Her son was with her, and his evidence was taken by the police before his return to Prescot by a later trail). if- cn-rgißed that the cause of death was heart disease. ,</p>
