      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>BOWLING. WITH THE WOODS.</head>
        <p>BOWLING. WITH THE WOODS.</p>
        <p>To-day's Works Bowling Association Final. DRAW FOR ANNUAL MAIN.</p>
        <p>With such glorious it eather as we had last week, it was in accordance with expectations that the bowling greens were in fine condition on Saturday. As far as local competitions were concerned, it was , pretty slack day, but friendly games were indulged In to a large extent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <p>The first game in the final ties for the club championship in connection with the Warrington and District Bowling Association was played on the Sankey-street green, and the Senior Cofiaerva- tives beat the Cemetery Hotel men by 58 points. There was a large number of spectators, and the individual games were full of interest. W. Thomason made a poor start for the Conservatives, when h• came off the green with only 10 points to his credit against R Antrobus, who ran the winning world at ' the 22nd end. J. Holland made up for this rebuff by disposing of J. Bibby by 21-7 in 19 ends.</p>
        <p>F. Heesom (Conservatives) played an exception- ally brilliant game against J. Ashton. At the out- set Ashtln was on every time, and bt the 9th end led by 12-3. He gained a single on the next length, but said " good-bye " to a score until he reached the 20th end, when the game was 14-14. Heesom was now warming to his task, and at the 27th end he only required a single chalk to game, whereas Ashton wanted five. A single on the 28th length rut Ashton to 17, which was his final score recorded, for Heesom found a good stretch and secured the Ideciding single.</p>
        <p>H. Barnes (Conservatives) and C. Smith were a. well-matched couple and played a tight game, peel- ing at 4,9, and 10. From the 10th end, when the score was 10-10, Barnes secured the "little 'un " and ran to 20-17 at the 27th end. A double and a single on the next two ends put Smith on level terms. However, he failed to land a winning wood on the next length, and Barnes scraped home by one point.</p>
        <p>The other games were fairly interesting, and the Conservatives have every reason to be proud of their win, although we cannot hold out much hops for them when they play the return match on the Cemetery green. The Manchester-road men are players who will take a lot of shifting, and on their own green they have surprised better combina- tions than the Conbervatave tiuudiera.</p>
        <p>A number of games were decided in the secind round of the Liberal Club handicap. In another column will be found the draw for the third and fourth rounds in connection with the same competi-</p>
        <p>We also give tbe draw for the first round games in connection with the Warrington Works Bowling Asscciation nth annual handicap, which opens on Saturday next. The prizes are valuable ones and the competition promises to be a greater success than ever.</p>
        <p>To-day's final in the Works Competition will pro- bably prove a very close thing, as both Rylands and the Cable Co.'s team have done well this season. The wireworkers have won the competition twice before, but the Cable team have never been so near success as to-day. They have, however, put in a lot of practice, assisted well by the heads of the firm, and thty mean to struggle hard against Rylands, who are undoubtedly the best team in the competition. The President (Captain 0. R. Crosfield) will be present to-day. along with several prominent gentlemen of the town. Mrs. W. Webster will present the cup and watches after the match is over.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District Bowling Associa- tion have decided to run the Individual Merit Com- petition on August 23rd, 30th, and September Gth, the games to be played at the Cemetery Hotel. All the members who are affiliated to Libetal A sqocia- don and "Peter Peacock" competition are eligible to join. The firet prize in the merit competition will be £5 ss.</p>
        <p>The annual ladies' main in counection with the Cemetery Hotel, which was played off on Thursday, was a great success and 6etails appear in another column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>CROSFIELD'S ANNUAL MAIN.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S ANNUAL MAIN.</p>
        <p>The annual bowling main in connection with Messrs. Crosfield's took place on Saturday, The 128 entrants provided many interesting games, and in the final J. Watson beat P. Wright by 13-5 after 15 ends had been played. In the semi-final J. Watson was almost defeated by J. Oakes, who was only short by one chalk. At the ninth end, ( takes wanted only two pc;ints, whereas his orponent had only five recorded. Three doubles on the next lengths and Watson peeled, only to be one behind again after the next length. Another two singles, well judged, put Watson in the final, which he won in such fine btyle.</p>
        <p>P. Wright had not much difficulty in Pntering the finhl, for he beat R. Warburton by 13-U in thirteen ends. From the second length Warburton did not find the jack until the eighth end, when, however, he landed a couple of beauties. These he followed by another double, but broke down again at. nine. He never recovered his play and Wright went to game with three singles.</p>
        <p>Watson had things all his own way in the final and romped home with a majority of eight. " TLIUMB BIAS."</p>
        <p>1.45. Bladen, Longford, 5 • Uddy, Rylands, 4 Appleton, Longford, 6 • Meakin. P &amp; K, 6 Millman, Longford, 3 • Brook; Whitscross. 6 Craven, Longford, 7 v Daenali, Crosflelds, 5 Ratcliffe, Longford, 5 • Allen, Fennel-street, 5 ' Patrick. Greenings 2 • Sankey, Locker's, 4 Smith, Riohmonos,4 • Leigh, P &amp; K. 5 H Gatley, Riolutionds, 6 • Barnes, Ryland., 6 2.10 Tomlinson, C.L.C.. 5 • Ward, Naylor's, 5 Dal; C.L.C., 3 • Ashton, Warrington rannery, 7 Higginson, .C., 7, • Feeney, P sai K, 5 Maines, C.L.C., • IJuckwortb, Ryland., 4 I Crozier, C.I .C., 5 • filllson, Stubbs Jordan, 3 • Cook, Rttpery, 7 Hatton, Whitecross, 3• ti airbrother, Castle, 5 Edgington, Whitecnoss 2 • dpeakman, Howley, 7 2.35. Maddock, Whitecross, 4 '• Mellor, Richmond., 7 Taylor, Whitt:cross, 4 • Williams, Nlllslolli 5 Appleton, No. 1, Whitecross, 1 • Po•ey, sen., Orford, 3 Ridge, Robinsons, 7 • Bradshaw. Whitecross, 5 Nealing, Robinson., 7 • Wynne, Fennell-street, 4 Warburton, Robinson., 5 • Graham, Houghton., 3 Jones, Wiawick, 3 • Langley: Howley Tannery, 6 Welsby, Winwick, 4 • Poole, White Lead, 7 3.0. Shave, Penketb, 6 • Narroway, Ropery, 4 Ainsworth, Penlietb, 7 • Coyne pen, P and K, 5 Bowyer, Penketh, 2 • A Heruhell, Cromffelds, 5 Randle., Pletcher; 2 • T Platt, Rylands, Mather, Lockers, 4 • 634 ' Wilson, Lookers, 4 v Rigby, Crosfieldr, 6 Dale, Union Tanneries, 6 • W iotter, Firth, 6 3.25. Sisson, Joiners, 7 • Wart urton, Richmond., 1 Higbam, Firth, 2 • Rushwortb, P &amp; K, 1 Walker,, • 632 Attach, Itichinonds, 7 • Mulvey, Longford, Miele; Richmond., 5 Battersby, Ropery, 7 Glesve, Whitecross, scr • Bridge, Fletcher., 2 Stanley, Grappenhall, 6 • Rutter, Houghton., 2 Jarman, Grappenball, 7 • Catching, Ryland:, 6 3.50. Fellow.. Whit ecross, 6 • 637 Lunt, Warrington Tannery, 6 • Warburton, Cros- fields, 3 Badger, P&amp; K, 5 • 633 Seed, Crostields, 6 • Molyneux,Rylands, 4 Farrington P K, 5 • Lingard, Ropers,. 4 Appleby, &amp;oilfield ' 5 • Roughtey, Ryland; 1 Hands, Crosfields, 5 • H