      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>LADIES' BOWLING MAIN.</head>
        <p>LADIES' BOWLING MAIN.</p>
        <p>Successful Competition at the Cemetery Hotel.</p>
        <p>Nhw that the ladies of Warrington have increased facilities for indulging in the game of bowls, we are seeing a more expert class of female trundlers on the green. There was abundant evidence as to the high state of perfection which some of the ladies have attained at the annual ladies' main in con- nection with the Cemetery Hotel Club on Thursday. The arrangements for the competition were admirably carried out, and some capital bowling was witnessed. We have seen many male players who could not send better woods than were delivered by some of the gentler sex. There were over fifty entries, and tour pairs occupied the green at a time. Mr. J. Wille.t was in charge of the green, and was ably assisted in his duties by memhers ofithe committee. During an interval tea was provided by Mr. and Mrs. A. Denham, the host aid h6stess of the Cemetery Hotel, and afterwards the elnnpetition was resumed. During the evening Messrs. Gros- field's Band played many delightful selections. At the close of the core) etition, Mrs. Allison presented the prizes. Mr. W illett (the chairman) remarked on the success of the comoetitions, and congratu- lated the winners. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded the host and hostess for their excellent accommodation. The prize-wint:ers were ;-1, Mrs. Gatley ; 2. Mrs. Pennington; 3, Mrs. R. Gilling ; 4, Mrs. Carman; 5w 8 Mrs. Wilcox, Mrs. Garnett, Mrs. S, Dale, Mrs. Meth( urne ; 9 to 10, Mrs. W. Ellison. Mrs. F. Dale, Mrs. S. Firth, Mrs. Berry, Mrs. A; Forster, Mrs. Allison, Mrs. Baldwin, Mrs. Milner 16 to 32, Mrs. Fergocon, Mis t-underland, Mrs. Whittle, Mts. W. Bridge. Al zsi• T, Ellison' Mrs. Finch, Miss A. Clarke, Mrs. A. Bailey, Mrs. W. Roylance, Mrs. E. Bennion' Mrs. J. Willett, Miss L. Gilling, Mrs. Knight, Mrs. Wilson, Miss C. Willett, Mrs. Drury. For those who were knocked out in the first round of the competition a consolation com- petition was conducted, and resulted as follows :- 1, Mrs. Brobbin ; 2, Miss Jordan; 3, Miss Bridge ; 4, Mrs. Belli s ; 5 to 10 Mrs. Butterworth, Mrs. A. Willett, Mrs: Barton, Mrs. C. Roylance, Mies M. , Jones. Miss A. Willett ; 10 to 20, Mrs. Rothwell, Mrs. Bibby. Mrs. Burdett, Miss Efigharn, Mrs. A. Roylance, Mrs. Kay, Mrs. Denham, Mrs. A. J. Jones, 1 Mrs Watkihson, Mrs. J. Roberts.</p>
        <p>18 12 12</p>
        <p>This is not a colliery village. It is situated on the south aide of the Ship Canal immediately opposite Hale, from which it is distant exactly four miles, as the aeroplane flies, to bring one's language up-to-date. A turn to the right at Peck Mill Budge on the Heisby-Chester-road, shows the rider three villages in succession. Hapsford, Elton, and Ince, of which the last is by far the prettiest, the fruit oi having a good lady of the Manor, Mrs. Park-Yates, and a most genial vicar, Rev. F. G. Slater. In the churchyard can be seen the grave of the eleven navvies who were killed in a terrible accident whilbt the Canal was being made for it happened close at hand, and the visitor may ride almost down to the canal bank and find there clean salt water. Returning to the Chester-road the fourth village of Thornton-le-Moors is passed. That completes the quartet of villages north of this old Watling-street, and enclosed by the Canal, the Gowy, and the Frodsham marshes on the other three sides. The exit from this charming hit of Cheshire is made near the inn at Bridge Trafford. The ugly Georgian hall on the left is Wirnbolds Trafford, the residence of somebody-or-other— see Cheshire directory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
