        <p>THE CYCLIST'S NOTE BOOK.</p>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>ASSOCiatioll.</p>
        <p>Picturesque Villages Within Easy</p>
        <p>Distance of Warrington.</p>
        <p>(N.A. CENTRE.)</p>
        <p>and</p>
        <p>cent</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Angling</p>
        <p>A fair muster of anglers turned out last week, both for contest angling and specimen hunting. Chester, Ilartfoid, Mickie Trafford, were all -visited and although specimens were scarce, with the exception of pike and " Daddy Ruffle," Warrington. Northern Anglers more than held their own in the open contests, Mr. F. Jolley capturing a first and fourth, and Mr. Jas. Green third in the ;open matches at-Northwich last week-end. , The prize distribution at headquarters on Thurs- day of last week, was a successful affair. Eighteen prizes were given, and a jovial evening was 'pent by the members and custornera., The latter are being encouraged to join the society, and several will probably hand in their names on Monday next.. The distribution of the Bay Horse prize e took plaec on Wednesday. Fourteen very useful awards were given to the successful angler. _A smoker followed</p>
        <p>HARTFORD. Members who visited this resort had very fair sport with roach, bream and perch. Several stayed over the holidays, occupying their time in ordinary angling during the 111'11111'g ing and contest fishing during the a MICKLE TRAFFORD.</p>
        <p>even-</p>
        <p>f ternoon</p>
        <p>The Gowy was visited by several members and, although specimens v.feie not to be caught, one or the stream could not 13,4 Bpproached with success, as two anglers had fair sport. The drawback was that the water is clear and it regt4r.es all the craftiness of the angler, when conditions are at their best, to fish it to advantage. It is a well- known fact that not 20 per . of anglers who visit the place c.in fish the stream with success, and that is where the fascination of the Gowy comes in. The angler foes trying new ideas week after week, and, as a rule, If anangler sets his mind on fishing a stream with succees, it is a long time before he will admit defeat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>RIDES WITH AN OBJECT.</head>
        <p>RIDES WITH AN OBJECT.</p>
        <p>EBY SILVER-STREET.] In answer to T.J., the shell-beach I mentioned in , last year's notes is Mochras Island in Merioneth- shire. Llanbedr is the third station from Barmouth and is the starting point for boats rowing to the shell beach, or "island," single fare 6d., return to station 9d. If the tide is out one has to walk to "North Point" and be ferried across for 2d. I have a list by me of the shells found there. It contains 39 univalves, and 45 bivalves, and can be obtained at the boat house. The cause of so many shells being found at Mochras seems to be the great bank,Sarn Badrig or Patrick's Causeway, that stretches for 15 miles into Cardigan Bay. It is a great breeding ground for fish of every kind and the current combin?d with the opposing flow of the Pensarn River piles the sand and shells and shingle into a great bank of sand- hills lining the shore for miles towards Barmouth. No one, by the way, should visit Barmouth without a bicycle, if he has one. Another fine shell beach is at the shore end of another great bank in the same bay. The Welsh spell it Ton fanau, and pronounce it Ton-vannah. It is the next station to Towyn, but the quantity of shells to be found there varies very greatly. Still another shell storehouse can be found in the Isle of Man. It is off Dalby Point or the Niarbyl, five miles south of Peel. The shells to be found in these places are not the ordinary whelks, and cock spurs of Blackpool or Rbyl ; they include cowries, and wentletrans, and the most beautiful of winkles and dogwbelks and venue shells. If T. J. wishes to spend a holiday near one of these places he might choose Llanbedr for Mcchras, Towyn for Tonfanau, or the pretty little village of Dalby in the Isle of Man. At all these places apartments can be found from the railway farm house lists or the Isle of</p>
        <p>Mau guide. CHESHIRE REST HOUSES.— 7. WELLINGTON INN, INCE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>RIDES WITH AS OBJECr.</head>
        <p>RIDES WITH AS OBJECr.</p>
        <p>The above round cin be made any time from now to October. The following can only be seen, per- haps, to-day, and it is worth riding for. • Bandiway Flower show was held last Monday, the first garden prize going to Mr. Maddock, next door to the post office, Sandiway, and Mr. Kettle, of Marton, was awarded the second. If any of my- readers is either judge or lover, I should like to recommend a tide with the object of seeing the second prize garden, I never saw anything to equal it in beauty. It is a very small one on the left of the road to Over, just before reaching the viaduct carrying the C.L.C. single line from Whitegate station to Winsford, but no one with two eyes can miss that garden, although in the middle of a grand coast down into Marton Hollow. Come back for tea to the Plough Inn at Whitegate, and then ask for Sixes hole, and whether you have your camera with you or your fishing rod, or even if you have nothing but a pipe and a sixpenny novel, you will spend a delightful evening by one of the prettiest of Cheshire's many lakes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>CLUB RUNS</head>
        <p>CLUB RUNS</p>
        <p>Pickmere on Saturday, and Beeston Castle on Monday, were the destinations of the Wesleyan Cycling Club. To describe the runs is only to repeat the same story of pleasure which week by week comes to the score or so of members who join in the rides. It may be mentioned that the route to Beeston on Monday was somewhat new. Lunch was taken at Hardman's on the Forest, and then the motors were avoided by taking the road as follows : The switchback to Mouldsworth, then through Ashton, Tarvin, Duddon, Clottou, Huxley, arid Horton's Mill. This last place needs some careful circum- venting, and this approach, though roundabout. presents the most splendid view of the castle and the hills. Tea was excellently served at the Castle Gateway, and the cycles safely stored at Id. each, which deserves mentioning. A quick .though dusty ride home was achieved by the main road through Cotebrook and Weaverbam. Lamp time to-night, 8.53 ; next Saturday, S 3S</p>
        <p>NORTON. The members who went to thia spot doing the holidays ehjoyed good sport, fair catches being reported both on Sun:lay and Monday. The roach here seemed to be or: feed, and very fair roach they were, although none were brought to headquarters to beat those already weighed in. The quantity returned to the v•ater as undersized was very small in comparison with the ordinary run of roach in our canals near home. Bailey and Bryers had a splendid hag on the Sunday, and Messrs. Stc.ut, Potter, Taylor and Glover had the hest catches they have recorded from Norton this season.</p>
        <p>NOTICE</p>
        <p>Will officials plesfe noto-the chimp of date for the officials' match, appe4r next week A. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>More Local Successes.</p>
        <p>Indifferent train service spoiled the chances of many local athletes during Bank Holiday week- end, and there was almost an epidemic of missed events, but those that " got there" rendered a fair account of themselves. On Saturday, at Rudder- field, the brothers Siddall arrived in time for Arthur's heat of the 440, yards and to the last crack of the pistol for the final of that event the War- rington man ran unbalanced until his case appeared hopeless. lloNever, he iecovered in time to snatch the first prize in the last stride. At Royton, W. Bolton ran well t ) get second in the mile, and on Monday at the same place he was third in the mile, and was disappointed in the half- mile on both days, because an official error omitted his name from the programmes. At Retford, W. Siddall had to be satisfied with two seconds from the 110 yards and 440 yards, although in the opinion of one of the judges the Warrington man won the former by nearly a yard. At Southport and Stoke, Charlie Berry was pick- up second and third prizes.</p>
