      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <p>on</p>
        <p>PILCHARD FISHINC-.</p>
        <p>summer and autumn visitors to the Cornish zoast count themselves fortunate if they happen to witness the catching of a school of pilchards. Few scenes are more inspiring. Day after day the "huer" on' the headland has kept his anxious look-out over the sea, while the fisher- men wait by the boats below. At last the patience of the watchers is rewarded, says the Globe. The experienced eye has marked the change in the hue of the water from green or grey to purple, which denotes the appearance of the fish, and the cry of "Hera!" wakes the sleepy scene to animation and. excitement. Every man, woman, and child stream down to the beach to lend a helping hand, and watch with bated breath the progress, of operations on which so much depends in feed and clothing and :com- fort. As tho large black boats put out the "huer" shouts instructions THROUGH A SPEAKING - TRUMPET, emphasised with much waving of the signalling apparatus of wood and calico which he calls "bushes." If fortune attends the venture the whole of the shoal is enclosed by the seine nets, and nothing remains but to pray for a continu- ance of calm weather until the fish are "tucked" ita a smaller net and landed in baskets. Hour after hour, perhaps for days together, the air is redolent of the smell of fish, while the busy toil goes on until the last of the silvery harvest .has been gathered. The fisherman's calling has about it an element of chance almost unknown to other industries. In the pursuit Of the pilchard the 'rnornitiff.'s work may represen;-, LIS many .as 13,000,000 fish. On the other hand, the nets may be clot in vain, for the hoal may escape before it can be encircled, or by some Mischance may get away after it seemed to be caught.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>WORDS OF WISDOM</head>
        <p>WORDS OF WISDOM</p>
        <p>Frappin es s is alwPys .a, home-made article. Who conquers indolence will conquer all the rest. We never can begin to live unless we. dare to die. A real man is he whose greatness is part of himself. To-day is filled with opportunities that are still ours. It takes bravery of the best stamp to be trul. to oneself. Better no faith, no hope, .no love, no God, than shams thereof. . Be upright in principle, pure in morals,' sin- cere in religion. The only way to do ,the St.rong .thing is to be strong beforehand. / Of all wofk that produces results, nine-tenths must be drudgery. It is safer to be humble with one talent than to be proud with ten. To have failed is to, have striven; to .haTe striv6,ll is to have 0,:43,77".</p>
        <p>FORESTEES AND PUBLIC-lIOUSES</p>
        <p>- - The Foresters' Conference at Manchester yesteiday discussed the holding of lodge meet- ings'in,public-houses. Dealing with a report on the subject, Mr. Phippen, a Welsh delegate, asked where were friendly societies formed, if not in public-houses? Up to the present, the best friend of the friendly society had been the publican. (Hear, hear, and dissent.) 'He hoped they would not forget the pit from which they were dug. Because some had not been served well in the I)ublic4muse they must not think all publicans were bad. One swallow —(laughter)—did not make a summer. - (More laughter.) • A. Glasgow delegate strongly protested against the conference .giving the public-houses a cheap advertisement. The Meeting decided that lodges and approved societies should meet where they chose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 9, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 9, 1913.</p>
        <p>AMATEUR STEEPLEJACK. Alderman Bennett's Daring Climb Described by Himself_ NOVEL iXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>Amateur steeplejacks have conquered the Parish Church spire, interesl, in which ..land- mark has been aroused anew by the article in last. week's "Examiner." On the third stone from the tOp are to be faintly traced the initials of a well-known member of the Corporation. and others, of former climbs, remain there, cloquent of the climbers' daring feats. We are able -to furnish an account of one of these venturesome climbs, and we should not be surprised to -learn, in :these 'days of adveri- turesome women,. that sonic lady sought to equal the past record made by men. The exact height of the spire is 281 feet, and :there 'are indications that it was once a few feet higher, the top being lowered some forty Vars ago to weather storms liettZ-r. At its present height it -ranks the -third 'highest in England, exclusive of the 'Cathedrals. Salis- bury Cathedral hOlds the record with 404 feet, -Norwich Cathedral's spire is 315, and St. famous Michael's, Coventry, aspires to the height of 3sOix3. higher thth'san hPatriaSh Church. spire is 287, t of Warrington's landmark. To this dizzy height of 281 feet above Mother Earth climbed, one day, Alderman Bennett, Who, writing of his experience under the pen name of ,t E. Fairfax ROchester," In- "The Dawn," for 1903, an interesting Warrington. magazine now defunct, gives a graphic descrip= tion of his adventure. Arriving on the tower at. the base of the spire, the writer encountered the steeplejack, Mr. Hewitt, father of Mr. Hewitt now execut- ing the repairs, and persuaded him to allow the trip, to be made. "At the base of the south-west pinnacle," he writes, "I read the name of Owen Jones, a spire, d wholeajaneken wgag'easd..kin previous work upon the killed whilst working on a Widnes 'chimney. "Three steeplejacks awaited me, and pointed out an object not unlike the seat of 'a Swing, by oriaanegrulsas a le r rope, , which, in its attachedtur n,w b drawnas at a dizzy altitude immediately above the topmost louvre, ltovhNeeps t at fulplelyboerimngo of attached, ched, apparently, to the airy seaffolding, which clustered round the summit of the spire. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>" JOY "</head>
        <p>A " JOY "</p>
        <p>"1 was to ascend alone. I flung my hat aside, buttoned up my coat, and took my seat in the swing; but the steeplejacks did .not intend to let me run unnecessary risks, and pinioned me as though I were a criminal going to execution, so that, if I chanced to lose my head, I 'Could not fall, and, at the worst, should be hauled down again (no doubt considerably battered), like a log of wood, say, 'or a sack of potatoes. " ' Are you ready?' they inquired. 'Quite!' I answered, and the next moment I lost solid foothold, and was being swung above the battlements, and swinging slowly up the slop- ing surface of the spire ere I knew, I found my feet were resting on the loftiest louvre. I swung exactly like Mahomet's coffin, midway between earth and heaven, but very thankful for the foothold, which the afore- said louvre afforded me. "My outlook was restricted to the views im- mediately to left and right, and, as the dtp,j was dull, and the town enveloped 111.6 pall of amount to much. smoke,ethcuesretdOidinneottt,aatftbe; It did swinging completely. round, with My back to the spirt, I might ; obtain a full view of the town, or as much of it as the abounding but, then, smoke. allowed. my native caution told me that gymnastics such as these might disarrange the rope or pulley, and that it was distinctly wiser to refrain."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>" BEANSTAJ,K " LADDERS.</head>
        <p>" BEANSTAJ,K " LADDERS.</p>
        <p>Descending by the swing, he ventured to ascend the ladders fixed to another part of the friends vhhto be happened to causing spire, and thus describes his adventure "I felt cool enough, and (not desirous Of exciting to tingaattneyntioof m n,, or, it be passing), I successfully disguised myself in the blue slop which Mr, Hewitt's brother kindly lent me, put. his own soft cap upon my head, and wriggled round the gaping parapet until I reached the beanstalk ' ladders some 14 in all), attached with ropes, and reaching from the base of the north-west 'pinnacle, in giddy sequence, to within an arm's length of the gleaming weathercock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>NEAIIING TIIE SUMMIT.</head>
        <p>NEAIIING TIIE SUMMIT.</p>
        <p>"Immediately above t.he parapet the outlook broadened strangely, .and the second ladder was attached to its inunediate predecessor at an awkward angle ; but I looked before I climbed, kept firmly hold of both sides. of the frame, and never left the full weight of my body to One limb alone, and, by the tinie I reached the third or fourth, I felt quite used to it. • "At the top the space between the scaffolding and the ladders was very scant, and the steeple- jack negotiated it by turning sideways, but, fortunately, I was not a ,Falstaff, and I con- trived, with some exertion, to creep through without materially changing my position ; .and, after a brief 'rest, proceeded by a second doge a squirming to the next and loftiest platform, where I sat down to rest. • .</p>
        <p>•• .1••-•• •-• "The view in all directions was extensive, but again the day was not particularly clear, and the town itself was almost hidden ia tte cloud of smoke, belched forth in four black streams from Bylands's, ',and hanging in a sable canOpy above the town. •</p>
        <p>SCRATCHED HIS INITIALS</p>
        <p>"The Rectory looked .plain enough. however, and the Colleges,-and Park, and Cemetery—l saw a funeral, by the -way, proceeding quietly down Church-street as I gazed—and, eastwards, the view was tolerably extensive. Presently my friends suggested that I Should stroll round the platform. It was but two planks wide, but I was getting quite aeclimatiSed by this, aild, holding on (M case of accideull by the strong chain which girt the spire. on whtoll the sal?y of the whole aerial fabrie very much depend.! I, I contrived to eircuninavigate the planks in safety, and prepared to follow my companions up the last two ladders. The foremost steeplejack pereltediwith very great agility, upon the very top ; I folloiv_4l him as far as I could get ; and Mr. Jlewitt tlien produced a formidable knife, and, at his s!•gg(s- Lion, on the sixth stone from the weathercock, scratched my own initials. Finally, by remh- ing out as far as possible, I managed to serat,th them in pen(ll on the third stone from the top, and with that I had to be content."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>THEATRE ROYAL,</head>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL,</p>
        <p>The pictures at the popular Scotland-road house have been of an exceptionally high stan- dard this week." Esther " will be shown to- night. It is a striking and beautiful film. (kill- ing with the story of I;sther from the Old Testa- ment., Other interesting productions will also be shown. "A Fair Saint," a representation of a legend in natural colours, will he the chief picture for Monday. TneAday and Wednesday next. For the remaining days of the week " The Cth.se." a powerful tragic story nrinnocent lives ruined by a savage crime, will occupy premier. posi- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>PICTUREDROhIE.</head>
        <p>THE PICTUREDROhIE.</p>
        <p>" East Lynne " in Pictures</p>
        <p>• The management of the Picturedrome have obtained the exclusive rights .for this district of the great dramatic film, "East Lynne," which they will she* at every performance next week. In this drama of five acts there will be one hundred and seventeen scenes. The theme is adapted from Mrs. Henry Wood's famous novel, and ,not from the stage produc- tion. The 'film takes two hours to show.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT DitAIRE.</p>
        <p>" A White Slave."</p>
        <p>"A White Slave," the drama with which the Royal Court Theatre re-opened on Monday even- ing, does not, as the title would seem to imply, deal entirely with the nefarious traffic which all parties are uniting to suppress. The main theme centres in the trials and difficulties of a young inventor, and the crafty scheming of an artful villainess to compass his overthrow, but a strong light is also thrown upon the methods by which trustful girls can be decoyed into establishments a itl-fame. The subject is handled in an unobjectionable, yet telling, man- ner, and the play has produced a great impres- sion upon crowded houses. One of the most thrilling scenes is 'that in which a foreign emis- sary endeavours to steal the up-to-date gun which the British Government has purchased from the inventor, but so ingenious are the pre- cautions taken to guard against that kind of treachery, that he brings about his own death. The story is relieved by a comedy element, which is really bright and clever. Mr. and Mrs. Joseph .Millane's company give a most successful representation of the play, their acting being acceptably free from the usual melodramatic exaggerations. Miss Mabel Rose takes the heroine's part in a most natural, yet appealing, manner, while Mr. John S. Millward imperson- ates Victor Ashton, the inventor, with capital freedom and power. Mr. George Porteous and Miss V. St. Lawrence make the most of the un- popular roles of designing accomplices in evil. Mr. Dunlop Stewart enlists sympathy as the old man who. for a time, is the willing dupe of sharpers. Mr. Clive Seymour introduces excel- lent comicalities as Billy Jarvis, the Boy Scout, and is capably supported in many amusing pas- sages by Miss Olga Jefferson.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. Next week the sporting drama, "The Gambler," will be presented by Mr. Harry Fox- well's company. It is announced that the horses running in the great race scene will gallop at full racing speed over one mile in full view of the audience..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>SEASON'S BOOKINGS.</head>
        <p>THE SEASON'S BOOKINGS.</p>
        <p>The management announce an unusually at- .tractive list of bookings for the autumn and winter season. One of the most interesting features of the arrangements is that Mr. A. M. Loader will bring the original production from the .Aldwych Theatre of Her Side of the House," "'When Knights were Bold," " Clandian," "Proof," "Hindle Wakes," "Bunty Pulls the Strings," and "Pride of Byzantia" are among the plays Which will pay welcome return visits. Mr. Alexander Marsh and Miss Carrie Baile, who are favourites with Warrington playgoers, will present a high-class repertoire. including "The -Three Musketeers," " Oliver Twist," "The Bishop's Candlesticks," "Taming of the Shrew," "A Tale of Two Cities," Nell Gwynne of Drury-lane," and "The Sleigh Bells." The Warrington Musical Society's Opera Company will produce "Miss Hook of /Tolland,' and the Warrington Dramatic Society has also hooked a week at the theatre. Other attractions will include "A Waltz Dream," "Baby Mine," "The Labour Leader," "The Queen Mother," "The Younger Generation." and the pantomimes Robinson Crusoe," "Dick Whittington," " Cinderella," and " Alladin."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>"You may play with a bear, or nurse a rattlesnake, and come off free—but don't get monkeying with Chows—t'aint safe." The wis- dom of this assertion, freely circulated on the advertising stations, is strikingly illustrated at the Hippodrome this week in a clever sketch, "The Big Joss," which has been presented by Mary Maypen's and George Pickett's company. The story of how two deoperadoes were pre- vented from taking the jewels from the Big Joss god of the Chinese, and the vengeance of a saloon dancer, ia intensely exciting, and is enacted amidst " surroundings. The sketch grips one Trona the first, and although a trifle:" merry," one cannot help but appreciate the pleevr acting of the company. Mr.. George Pickett makes up and acts the part of Tong Lo in a very realistic manner, and Miss Violet Campbell is a, caßital Xymena, the saloon dancer. Mt. Lawrence Osborne portrays Ah Lun in a truly Oriental fashion; and • as the desperadoes, Mr. A. Gordon Laws and 'Mr. Jack Vyvyan are a success. Ernest Scharff and his assistant give an up- to-date and breezy instrumental performance. Twenty instruments are manipulated with great skill bv Scharff, who is most, vociferously ap- plauded. .Sainnroff and -Miss Sonia introduce their wonderfully trained dogs. The Cora Sisters thoroughly captivate with their latest successes, including the " Cingalee," their _voices blending most harmoniously together. Du 'Gros Trio are expert comedy acrobats, and 'perform many hair-raising feats. Arthur Woodville scores with "It's a nice time to i-ome home," and Jack Roscie is a smart comedian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>SILENT MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>SILENT MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>Visit of Chung Ling Soo.</p>
        <p>The silent exponent of mystery and magic, Chung Ling Soo, visits the Warrington Hippo- d!•ome next week, to delight and bewilder War- rington audiences. He is the king of conjurors, and works his effects with lightning-like rapidity. Chung's father was a Scotsman, his mother, a native of China. and on his father's death he took the name of Chung and added "Ling Soo," which means "extra good luck." He was apprenticed to a Chinese conjuror, who left Chung in Mexico with a little show, which has</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>CHUNG- LI\U- zsOu.</head>
        <p>CHUNG- LI\U- zsOu.</p>
        <p>now grown into the huge entertainment which has made him famous all over the world, and. .which has been given before many strange audi- ences. _ _</p>
        <p>"While in Auckland," he says, "I gave a per- formance to an audience consisting almost entirely of Moans. The Maoris are great lovers of magic, and ;witnessed my Performance in perfect silence, and with awe spread over their faces, until the end of the performance, when they rose en masse and shouted out in their melodious tongue loud appreciation .of the enter- tainment. They shouted ' Kio Osa ' and ' Coo-ee,' meaning 'good luck, come .back again.' They were dressed in native costume, which is most quaint and picturesque. Their native chief presented me with a New Zeiiand jade charm, taken from his neck. where it had reposed from his childhood. This charm is sup- posed to ward off all evil spirits and disaster. " I have been an extensive traveller in all parts of the globe. In the old days there-was not the railway accommodation as we have now. Travelling Was done then mostly in stage coaches in the summer, and by sleighs in the winter. Many a time I have crossed the frozen river St. Lawrence by sleigh. While in Mexico I gave several commhud performances before the deposed President Diaz, and many of the Indian!: and half-castes aftended. They were so terrified during my fire-eating performanee that fl rushed .out . screaming from the building, claiming I was a demon."</p>
