      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>Health Committee's Warning to Allegea Offenders.</head>
        <p>Health Committee's Warning to Allegea Offenders.</p>
        <p>BOWLING GREEN DAMAGED.</p>
        <p>The local agitation against the emission from works' chimneys of excessive volumes of black smoke is apparently bearing fruit. At a recent meeting of the Health Committee a report on the emission of black smoke was considered, and the Town Clerk was instructed to serve notice upon the offenders.</p>
        <p>ARE THERE OTHER NOXIOUS .FUMES? The head gardener having reported on the damage to 'grass and shrubs at the bowling green in Manchester-road, and a report by the public analyst on the probable cause thereof having been read, it was resolved that the Town :lerk call the attention'of the manufacturers in the vicinity of the bowling green, and that the attention of the inspector under the Alkali Acts be drawn to the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>Correspondent's Suggestion to the Health Committee. – THE OBJECT IN VIEW.</head>
        <p>A Correspondent's Suggestion to the Health Committee. - THE OBJECT IN VIEW.</p>
        <p>To THE EDITOR OF THE "EXAMINER." Sir,—The smoke question is not in a satis- factory position. We have your paper, and particularly your correspondents, • implying that the Warrington Health Committee is neg- ligent in its duties, that it favours the wealthy manufacturer by allowing him to break the law in creating a nuisance, and promptly punishes the poorer man if he commits or allows a nuisance ; in other words, that this committee .is a .respecter pf persons. Die re- sult of this, it is claimed, throws the incon- venience and cost arising from .the smoke nuisance upon .the residents and ratepayers of the town, whose interests the Borough Coun- cillors, who form the Health Committee, are elected to further and safeguard. Considering the composition of the Health Committee, it is strange if these charges can be subM,antiated. All have done good service for the town, and have -given much time and thought to its interests. My inclination, and I think that of most .Warrington people, would e not to condemn .unheard. I would; there- fore, suggest that the Health Committee frankly take the people of Warrington into their con- fidence. Let them show that they have taken action in all cases when they have received reports of the commission i of a nuisance; that they have dealt gently with first offenders and rigorously in cases where the nuisance has been repeated. Only by such a course can the cloud of suspicion, as thick as -Dallam smoke, which hangs over the Health Committee, be dispelled. If this is not done, judgment will go by de- fault, the defendants not putting in an appear- ance.</p>
        <p>There is the possibility of the authority. standing on its dignity and saying that they will continue to ignore what they may call a newspaper agitation. But is this wise? Whether the charges direct or implied can be made good ac no, they have been brought for- ward, not from personarmotives, but with the scile desire of making the town cleaner and healthier, and as uch ought to receive con- sideration of the Health Committee, the object of whose existence Is the same.</p>
        <p>A MINIMUM OF SMOKE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>MATRICULATION EXAMINATIONS.</head>
        <p>MATRICULATION EXAMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>Numerous Local Successes.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Joint 'Matriculation Board, Universities of Manchester, Liverpool, Leeds and Sheffield, the following local stu- dents have been successful : Matriculation Examination.—First Division: Frank R. .Uone (Warrington Secondary School), Gladys M. Hodgkinson (Ashton-in-Makerfield Grammar School).. Second Division: Harry Barnes (Runcorn County Secondary School), Lionel H. Cansdale (Ashton-in-Makerfield Grammar School), Harry C. Farrimond (Ash- ton-in-Makerfield Grammar School), Hilda N. Hesford (Ashton-in-Makerfield Grammar School), William E. Mason (Warrington Municipal Sec- ondary School), John Pigot (Ashton-in-Maker- field Grammar School), Lily A. Sharpe (Ashton- in-Makerfield Grammar School), Daniel Thorn- ley (Ashton-in-Makerfield Grammar School), Dora • Webb (Ashton-in-Makerfield Grammar School), David N. Worswick (Ashton-in-Maker- field Grammar School). Among the candidates. already holding certi- ficates, but who have satisfied the examiners for higher papers in mathematics and phy6ics is Harold C. Emmerson, of Warrington, and in physics James 11. Dewar (Warrington). Amongst those who have been admitted for a supplementary examination, William E. Mason (Warrington) was successful in additional mathematics, and Alice Jarman (Warrington) in Latin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>SENIOR SCHOOL CERTIFICATES</head>
        <p>SENIOR SCHOOL CERTIFICATES</p>
        <p>Mis-s— Ada A. Joynson---(i,;-m—m---67;;;Ilmar School) has obtained a senior school certificate, which is also a matriculation certificate.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL CERTIFICATES.</p>
        <p>School certificates have also been won by John D. Broadbent (Lymth Grammar School), and Ruth W. Willan (Lymm Grammar School).</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COUNTY SCHOLARSHIPS. A scholarship, value 6O per year, for profi- ciency in secondary school subjects, has been awarded Thomas Smith (Leigh 'Grammar School).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS. Ogilvie—Johnstone.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS. Ogilvie—Johnstone.</p>
        <p>A ‘;;. i; ; ill" of considerable interest to \Val.- ring kin 1:kolile was solentinsed;at Aberdeen on Saturday. -The „bridegroom Nvas Dr. lan in .practice at ‘Vilderspool, Warring- ton, and son of Inc Hey. John Ogilvie, a well- known Scottish clergyman, .\\-ho is Hie minis- tot' of the Parish Church. Siains, Aberdeenshire. The bride was Mis,i I. Johnstone, a daughler or Alt.. John John.,:itone, of the ".‘l,ordeen Journal."</p>
        <p>The ceremony NV:IS performed by the 'ley: W. 11. Leatharn, ininiter of Ito'burn parish, Aberdeen. and he was assisied by the Bev. .1. Ogilvie, father of the bridegroom. The wed- ding was what, might, lie termed a university marriage, us both I,ride bridegrOom graduated at. Aberdeen University.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>Woods—Smallwood.</head>
        <p>Woods—Smallwood.</p>
        <p>There Avos ta pretty wedding at St. Paul's cnur,•ll on wainesday. wifen '‘`...o,)ds, of Latchford, was married to liss Maggie Smallwood second daughter of Mr. S. B. Smallwood, of Froghall-Time. The Bev. . Brayeitinty, vicar of St. Paul's, officiated. Mr. E. \\"iikts; was at the organ, and played a choice sole,. lion of music front Dubois, Wagner, \fn- hLssoliii other composers. The hymn -Tile voice that breallted o'er Eden " wus sung. the bride, who was given nWfly by her father, was charmingly attired in it dress of ivory satin trimmed with pearls and hovers' knots, a \vrenth of orans.fe tssont and a veiled Brussels net, and cart-it'd it bouquet of lilies. roses aild white healltt•:•. 'The brides- maids were the Misses .Tessie and Hilda Small- wood (sisters of lhe bride), who w-ore dresses or perntinche satin ain't black- velvet hats. .trimmed with pint: roses. and carried bouquets of pint; 1-01-11;dhons. E:c..11 also. wore a. gold pendant and; signel ring. the gifts of the bridegroom. Mr. Ihit•ry Bibby was the best man and -Mr. T. Bibby the groomsman.</p>
        <p>?‘ reception was afterwards heid at, the \Via- warleigh Cafe. a: large • number of guests attending. In the evening the festivities took the form of a whist drive and dance, the guests being entertained at intervals hy Mr. Sam Auk- land. huntorist.</p>
        <p>1 he honeymoon is being .spent in London and Bournemouth. • The bride travellel in a navy 1)1lie costume, and wore a Nark velours hat with blue lancer plume. Many 'lonesome pre- sents have been received. includina a pair of silver candlesticks from the teachers of St. Paul's Sunday School. The bridegrocim's pre- sent to the bride was a set of furs. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>AVIATION TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>AVIATION TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>Colonel Cody Killed in Biplane Wreck at Aldershot.</p>
        <p>WELL-KNOWN CRIGKETER'S FATE.</p>
        <p>Colonel Cody, the most picturesque and most popular figure among the airmen of to-day, met his death at Aldershot on,Thursday.-</p>
        <p>He was flying above Laffan's Plain in the new biplane which he had built for the race round England. Something went wrong—the cause of the disaster is not known with certainty— and the machine fell from a height variously estimated at irolll 200 feet to 500 feet.</p>
        <p>The biplane crashed into a clump of trees and was smashed to fragments.. Colonel Cody was killed instantly, and a passenger who was with him—Mr. W. 11. 13. Evans, an Indian Civil Ser- vice official and a, well-known cricketer— shared his fate.</p>
        <p>Colonel Cody had 'won the sympathy of the public by his pluck and perseverance against great difficulties, and the news of his death will be received with the deepest regret. On behalf of the Army Council, Colonel Seely, Secretary of SI ate for War, has sent a telegram of condolence to Mrs. Cody. The accident happened with startling sudden- ness, and practically without the slightest warn- ing, at a time when the machine was apparently flying steadily and well.</p>
        <p>Watchers on the hill saw the elevator rise almost straight into the air, and the machine made a couple of erratic movements—" like a wounded bird," as one eye-witness described it Before the onlookers had time even to imagine what was happening the two occupants of the biplane were seen to fall, almost simul- taneously, from the machine. The aeroplane, after a few erratic movements, as if driven before a stronger wind than was perceptible on the ground, 'seemed to turn right round and fell with a terrific crash into a small clump-of trees some 50 yards from the hill.</p>
        <p>The nearest spectators rushed at once to the sPot: where the bodies of the two men wen* found lyin.g. only sa few feet apart, sonic 60 yards away from the wreck of the waterplane. Both men had evidently been killed instantly.</p>
        <p>Messengers were at once dispatched to the Flying School for assistance, and in a short time a large party of officers and men of the Flying Corps were on the spOt. A motor lorry was obtained, and, under the charge of a party of blueincliels belonging to the Naval section of the Flying Corps. the bodies of the dead air- man and his companion were conveyed to the Connaught Hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>EARLESTOWN SENSATION.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN SENSATION.</p>
        <p>Joke Which Ended in a Young Man's Death.</p>
        <p>/4. REVOLVER TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>A sensation has been created at Earlestown this Week by the death of a young man, who was accidentally shot by a companion, who was joking with a revolver, which he thought to be empty. A sequel to the affair was heard at the War- rington County Petty Sessions. on Wednesday, when John William Jackson; Of 180, Earle- street, Earlestown, was charged with causing the death of James Farthing (20), of Wargrive- road,. Earlestown, by shooting him in the chest. with a revolver.</p>
        <p>'lnspector Lewis staled that on Monday morn- ing. the prisoner, along with Farthing .and an- other young .man named Thomas George Simper, were playing .football in a. yard at the rear .of 'Bridge-street; Earlestown. The ball, was kicked over. the wall into the yard of a confectioner's shop adjoining the premises of Simper, who was acycle agent. The thee men Went into Simper's shop, when it was de- cided' to toss as to who should •go into the yard and get the ball. It turned ,out that Farthing had to go for the ball.. As an excuse to go into the shop, Jackson and Simper gave Farthing 711 d. each in order to make a purchase of sonic chocolate. After -Farthing had received the money he jocularly remarked that the con- fectioner's shop was closed, and he would "do a bunk." Jackson noticed a revolver on the desk and jocularly. said, "I will shoot you if you do."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>SIIOT IN THE CHEST.</head>
        <p>SIIOT IN THE CHEST.</p>
        <p>Jackson followed Farthing, and, pointing the revolver at him', pulled the trigger three times. Twice it .clicked, and the third time there was a report, - and a bullet entered Farthing.- He Beemed dazed, and stared at Jackson, who said: ." I have not shot you, have I?" Farthing did not speak, hut placed his hands to his, chest. lie was carried into the kitchen and placed in an armchair. ' .Tackson loosened his clothes, when it was seen that there was a small bullet wound in the chest. A- doctor was sent for, and he immediatply dressed the wctunds. The deceased was attended by Dr. Valentine, but died on Tuesday night. A remand for two days was obtained, as the inquest laid - not been held. Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, of Warrington, applied for bail, which was granted, in one surety of £2O. • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>JAcKsON DISCHARGED.</head>
        <p>JAcKsON DISCHARGED.</p>
        <p>Coroner S. Prighouse held the inquest at Earlestown Town Hall on Thursday, and the jury returned a verdict of '"Death from mis- adventure."</p>
        <p>Jackson was brought before the magistrates at Newton yesterday afternoon. .</p>
        <p>Inspeclor Lewis stated that in view of the jury's verdict the police did not desire to Offer any evidence. ,</p>
        <p>Jackson was therefore discharged</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>PRICE OF G.</head>
        <p>THE PRICE OF G.</p>
        <p>Should Slot Meter Users have Concessions?</p>
        <p>The IerIIIS Upon Whiell users of slot gas Meters ii supplied ith gas vill be discussed at the nicHing of the Town Council on Tuesday evening next. Councillor Gough lots given notice to move:— That all slot meter gas consumers in the bor- ough be allowed sd. per 1,000 cubic feet on all gas consumed on and after the 30th September, 1913; that all, slot meter gas consumers in the borough pay a rent of is. 6d. for each 3-light meter,,a.nd for each 5-light meter shall pay rent of 3s. per annum ; that discount allowed by the Corporation to the consumer shall be returned each quarter, or each time the slot meter is emptied of money. The meter rent shall be paid each quarter, or a quarter's rent kept out of the discoiml allowed by the Corporation by the coqc,Thr cdch quarter or each time the meter is emptied ; thai all slot meter gas con7i sumers in the horough, on and after the 3011) September, 1913, shall pay a, rent .for all slot cookers or grillers slippliecl by the Corporation, Wll4 lIIVO not purchased them.</p>
        <p>A return prepared by Mr. W. S. IRAVard, assistant. secretary of the Gas Department, shows that the total consumption of gas through siU! meters for the year ended March 31st, Wl3. was 138,745,000 cubic feet, producing, an income of £18,229 15s. 4d.</p>
        <p>Couldn't Ciimpete WitllQ</p>
        <p>Two anglers were•comparing .notes after the day's •sport, and they soon began recounting past triumphs.</p>
        <p>I onee caught a trout so. long," said the tall one, measuring off the length of one arm.</p>
        <p>" That's nothing.- I once caught a pike so long," replied the short one, stretching out both arms.</p>
        <p>over in Canada I onee caught a salmon ,so long," \vont on the tall one, and he, too, stretched out both arms. •</p>
        <p>"Oh, uo use arguing with you," said the short ono, turning away in disgust. "You know you've got longer arms than I have."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-08-09_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
