        <p>"The 'Legion of the Lost Ones!" That the name given to the French Legion 11 of "lost ones" themselves. What a world it meaning there is in the phrase! Who'd 0 strikes the eye it conjures up in the Into: military body consisting of men who 1"0. every one of them, `!pasts" in atriking .c it treat to their "present." Though this 15 ;it a great extent true of the Foreign Legit 4 is Rot altogether AO, for a goodly nurabtte! YAWLS' _Zen join the Legion out of pure „akt of' a4Thinture and quite a fourth of the ?trent'', _moreover, is made up of men P 7,00: In tho provinces of Alsace and Lorraine, '4/v 11.,y the Ireneh in the war of MO, led to jet) the Foreign Legion -by a mitt.o4,.= loyalty to the flag of their fattw'.-'</p>
        <p>FOUR DESERTERS. • b.Ol The remainder of the Legion aril jre eggs " of one sort or another, but ti:el „,gt not by any means all foreigners. A re'ii-alf official statement affirms that quite oue7• of the total strength of the Legion, vi,)eco consists of two regiments of six Ihousall'4"3/01 each, are pilrely and avowedly 'French 3die of the remainder some 3,000 are natives of .3're lost provinces, 1,200 are Germans, 600 f;aa Swiss, 606 are Italdns and Spaniards, -,ot 300 are of other nationalities. There are ite many Englishmen in the Legion, but , writer of. this article on one occasion calll-teo the conclusion that there were four he many: It happened some years ago wile" or, was doing military duty at S:erra Leone, tile as it is somewhat libelously called, tp:: White Man's Grave. Early one • Sueii morning he was looking out of a vrindovi' oir, his quarters, which overlooked the liar lii when his gaze fell on four men, dresse4,„o- - white duck uniforms and lookino g '740</p>
        <p>era lly disreputable, who were diserah-arlP:t •041 . from a small row boat. Driven by curl ho went down and interviewed them wile.,ll,ev, was told, in the English of educated P," that they were deserters from the Legion tile had -rowed in their crazy boat frau French post some forty miles up the efi'fe'l' The writer took compassion on them as low-countrymen in distress and took thcOlp-- his quartqqs where he entertained theole,oll' ishly and nut them up for the night. e. their conversation it was easy to div)to though they made no direct statements loci that ele*.ect. that two of them at least oici. served in the British army as officers ,004- th9t. all of them were men- of pretty' prnciples. This latter fact was well °Ater., lished ncxt morning, for when the awoke he found that his gists had della".,ol without beat of drum and that his watch A- cha, n . a particularly fine specimen of 3 live-made zodiac ring .he had been sh°,:iiierr them the previous day, and what money was in hig pockets had gone with thenl•„ig never heard of them again which he pr°iler. would have done -had they put in at : ° places down the coast and the infer-41e° that they wcre drowned..</p>
        <p>A GERMAN PRINCE. te4l One of the greatest romances connee in' with the. Legion had its commencemeut, • 1 1897 with Ihe enlistment of a young Ger"::;:ile. of some 2r, years of age. who gave the 11- of. Albert Frederick Nordemann. lir, very aristocratic-looking man, • had•_;„elot haughty manners, and was very reserwe' seemed to brood' a good deal. After -,5&gt; time he fe:l sick and died in the militarY Pops" pital at. Geryville .in Algeria, when it, the di?covered that this •simple "soldier Oil Legion was a. nrince of the German ,;,;,,of. family—a very near relation of 'the 1111111-allt William himself. His body was h.r.e In.'ti; home to Germany by a man-ofwar ffyiab flag at half-mast. - In Tonkin in 1894 a certain riva te• 0 p of the Len gio won the admiration of•11190r. -. f.cers and comrades by his excessive rd ed ing. lie was a strong, vigorous, b"i cionV about 'forty years of age and F-ie „ire seen. even when urrlzr fire, Without 11. i! ,511,' in his mouth.. One day, afier be:ug riddled IN bullets. he was carried t°,. 1:1 ambu n?: lance' ,sti!l puffi aWav at his ete- il(rili pipe. though iii life Was fast ebbiag a severed artery in his leg Four mouth% A ter his death it. was discovered that he SVil(ier-- Count von Bandissen, ex-captain in the I.oa. man army and .son of a general Nvh.„,.° been governor of the great fortress of burg. fl wits At • Geryville on one oecasion th;e„ such a great scarcity of drinking 'Nva'e";ottslY- the authorites began to be iC alarmed. When the outlook was at Its est a corporal , sought the commanding ,iolia; and offercd to find water. • The coulrnalifics' officer's doubts as to the corporal's (P°t", tions for such a task forced the latter t° Close' his identity and the colonel •learneto, his astonishment that he was talkini.ioe- Iv_ former officer of the. Austrian army .pire- vyutatiou as a military engineer Was pean.•</p>
